{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba BLIND3-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP on one registered line, as written** (council round 57 confirms): \"an accepted reply with a named defect (marked by both readers …)\".\n     4\t- **R01 (S, Claude writer).** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on F14: the outcome is a range of $679.25–745B, and rung 4\n     5\t  ($700–800B) does not hold all of it.\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n    39\t- R01 \"two forecasts\" (Microsoft backed out of the four-company tallies, a reader matter through `rests_on`);\n    40\t- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n    41\t  rejected 8 marks covering 7 distinct allegations (F14 was marked twice);\n    42\t- R06's \"number misread\" claims on the $18B schedule and the Lancium tranche.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Support:**\n    45\t- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n    46\t  100%:\n    47\t  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\n    48\t  - e2759 and e5376: NVIDIA's \"nearly $800 billion\" for the top five, written both as capex and as data-center capex;\n    49\t  - Meta's own figures: about 96%.\n    50\t  At 94% or more, the same $745B gives rung 4. The reply is accepted and publishable, but its rung does not follow from the evidence.\n    51\t  This is not a registered stop line (usable is counted per case, and S has R01). It is a real judgement error in an answer that would\n    52\t  publish.\n    53\t- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\n    54\t  passage as in both earlier runs.\n    55\t- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. What the test shows\n    58\t\n    59\t- **The writer-side contract holds.** In three runs, the request has moved from 11 of 12 accepted with a checker error (run 1), to 11 of\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l AGENTS.md docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -l AGENTS.md docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"     492 AGENTS.md\n     201 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n    1039 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\n    1732 total\n     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   111\t   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   112\t   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   113\t   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   114\t   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n   115\t3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   116\t   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n '\"'^#|D725|D741|D742|D744'\"' SMALL-DESIGN.md; cd ../../.. && rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} +D(725|741|742|744)|D(725|741|742|744)'\"' docs/DECISIONS.md docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n '\"'^#|D725|D741|D742|D744'\"' SMALL-DESIGN.md; cd ../../.. && rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} +D(725|741|742|744)|D(725|741|742|744)'\"' docs/DECISIONS.md docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n16:- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n19:## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n32:## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n59:## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n78:## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n143:## 4. Build\n162:## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n277:D725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n279:**[D742] Registered before the third run (council rounds 56–56c).** The re-run stopped on one S4 refusal of a sound answer\n325:**[D744] The second blinded re-read (Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; council round 58), registered before any packet is built.**\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: Alex chooses whether to authorize one more bounded blinded re-read (D744).**\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:458:**Earlier next task (done, D744): Alex chooses how to proceed after the third blind run's STOP (D743). Council 57 recommends a blinded\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:462:  - The writer side is clean: 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, and D742's rule followed by all six P-family writers.\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:471:The record of the third run's setup (D742):\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:474:request D742 fixed, and score it on the registered lines (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D742]).**\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:476:- **The re-run stopped on one line** (D742, `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md`).\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:494:The record before D742:\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:496:**Earlier next task (done, D742): re-run the v13s blind test once with fresh writers and readers, after the reader calibration on\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:497:Alex's rulings (D740, D741).**\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:504:- **Alex ruled the readers' boundary questions** (D741):\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:509:- **The reader calibration on six stored boundary replies** (`calibration-D741/`) runs before the re-run.\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:546:  The result does not answer D725's question.\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1069:| D725 | 2026-11-22 | Council round 45/45b (Alex: find the design cause of unreliable numbers): related number questions have no shared, checked calculation — prose answers code cannot check, one question judged alone, split inputs; recommended: a new judge version judging related families in one call with labelled figures and identity/arithmetic checks, \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim, a $0 gate then a 36-call paid gate | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1085:| D741 | 2026-11-22 | Alex rules D740's reader questions: a guidance-implied growth benchmark is not a second value (\"不算错\"); consensus total × a past quarter's share is not the market's reference (\"不能\"); a total used for a narrower quantity must state its deduction or a supported bound (\"必须写明扣多少\"); S5 enforces the carried share, the request and reader brief carry 1 and 3 | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1086:| D742 | 2026-11-22 | v13s re-run STOP: S4 refused a sound answer that computed a guidance rate straight into Q4 (request pulled three ways); council 56–56c: blanket and narrowed exemptions both let a reset to guidance publish, so S4 stays and the request states one rule (guidance informs the pace; the pace is an estimate naming its benchmark; the quarter's dollar value is the one forecast); refusal review registers sound and stated apart; two reader splits disclosed (O's $18B reading, prose numbers); third $0 blind run next | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1087:| D743 | 2026-11-22 | third v13s blind run: writers clean (12/12 accepted, D742 rule followed by all six P writers); STOP as written on R01 (both readers marked a range across a rung edge; both adjudicators rejected under the schema's range clause; cause: reader brief lacks that clause, Claude's error); disclosures R07 unsupported 90% share, O $18B split, R01 total-capex reference; council 57: ask Alex to authorize a blinded re-read of the 12 replies with the corrected brief and controls; Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\" | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1088:| D744 | 2026-11-22 | D743 re-read: range repair works on both controls; STOP as written on Q10 (both readers marked the permitted plus-or-minus midpoint; both adjudicators rejected; 11 such marks across four readings); one mechanism: readers lack the writer's format rules; council 58: shared rule text for readers and adjudicators, five clean controls, ask Alex for one bounded re-read; S 90% reply flipped between reader sets; Alex: \"批准再重读一次\" | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10993:- **The questions** (after D725):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11001:  - A new judge version is opened for the bounded test of D725: related number questions judged together, with labelled figures and\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11067:- **Not shown:** how a model uses the format (the paid gate, D725), production lifecycle, the product request builder.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11080:- **Decided (Alex):** the sender and the blind reading packets for D725's paid gate are built, rehearsed at $0 and reviewed by the\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11090:- **Decided by the council (D725's lines unchanged):**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11091:  - The first attempt is scored on both arms. D725 registers the arms \"under the same format and checks\" and says nothing about\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11126:- **Registered result (D725's lines, scored as written, D680):** all 72 answers refused by `calc_check.py`, none published; the joint\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11129:- **What it measures:** not D725's question (whether one call over a family gives better numbers), only that the answer format, as\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11163:    - Only then is a re-send of the same 36 requests asked for, under a new decision with D725's lines unchanged.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11180:  - A clean result goes back to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests under a new decision, with D725's lines\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11194:  - Codex answered that the six drop C8's guard against adding a segment into its total, a defect D725 named. Shrinking changes the\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11255:    D725's lines.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11346:## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11369:## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11383:      O's rung, and D741 ruling 2 does not settle it.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11397:- **Decided (council, under Alex's \"那你自己持续迭代\" and D741 ruling 1):**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11408:     The stop lines are unchanged (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D742]).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11421:    - all six P-family writers recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark (D742's rule);\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11449:## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in codex-r56*.md codex-r57.md codex-r58.md; do nl -ba \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in codex-r56*.md codex-r57.md codex-r58.md; do nl -ba \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**STOP on one confirmed line. Reject the blanket `%` and `x` cutoff.** The saved answer used $108B to derive a growth rate and $112.5B as its Q3 revenue. The proposed fix also accepts an answer that uses $108B as the dollar starting point for Q4 while counting $112.5B for Q3.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The verdict is right under the adjudication procedure used in both runs.** Both refusal reviewers judged the P-v4 answer right. I reconciled all 12 first replies with the saved requests and checker results. All 12 passed the schema, 11 published, and one was refused. There are 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Every case has at least one usable reply, and both joint replies have both forecasts supported. The Part A logs show 104 passing cases, 40 caught mutants plus the control, and 19 passing rehearsal checks. I independently reran the 104 cases against both checker versions. [Registered lines](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [Refusal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:138) · [Refusal review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json:3) · [Part A logs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-cases.log:105) · [Mutants](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-mutants.log:42) · [Rehearsal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-rehearsal.log:20)\n     4\t\n     5\t   **Wording — R07 has two disputed defects on F3.** One reader flagged an estimate presented as the market’s reference. The other flagged two values for the same quantity. The registered line requires agreement on the named defect itself. Each adjudicator pair split, so neither defect adds a confirmed stop under the first run’s agreement rule. I would write “one confirmed stop, with unresolved marks” because the registration says a third reader settles disputes, and the procedure used here added two adjudicators who could still disagree. Across the six packets, five marks were jointly rejected, nine split, and zero were jointly upheld. [R07 votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:157) · [First run’s procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:37) · [Registered wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:252)\n     6\t\n     7\t   **Minor — Two details in the write-up need correction.** Only E’s Claude writer copied the two CPU-market figures and exercised the range fix. The write-up also omits R11’s split over F13, which assigns a time period and a funded/unfunded scope to an outside $70B figure. F13 feeds neither the outcome nor the reference. [E source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:30) · [E Codex reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:1) · [F13 votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:377)\n     8\t\n     9\t2. **Serious — The proposed checker change is wider than Alex’s ruling.** I reproduced the reported census: one of 24 saved replies changes, and the candidate passes all 104 existing cases. I then tested a variation of the saved P-v4 reply. It keeps the $112.5B Q3 forecast in the annual sum and projects Q4 by applying the guided growth factor twice from Q2: `96.2 × (108 / 96.2)² = 121.25`. The first multiplication reconstructs the $108B Q3 guidance as the dollar base for the second. The reply passes the schema. The current checker refuses it on S4 alone, and the candidate publishes $411.57B. A second probe also flipped when Q4 company sales were calculated as `$116B ÷ ($99.9B / $108B)`. Neither probe needs to relabel the original Q3 guidance. Alex approved using guidance to set a growth benchmark while the forecast supplies the quarter’s dollar value. The cutoff lets a ratio carry the second dollar value into the outcome. [Alex’s exact question and answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [Saved P-v4 reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/claude.json:1) · [$99.9B source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:613) · [Candidate cutoff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt:638)\n    10\t\n    11\t3. **Serious — Change both request sentences with the narrower checker fix.** The system asks writers to carry guided growth forward. The schema says the rate can inform an `estimate`, which is a specific figure type, and the header can be read as requiring every calculation path to go through the quarter forecast. A code-only change leaves that tension in place. I would say that a growth rate derived from guidance may inform an estimate or be used in `apply_growth` with the shared quarter’s forecast as the dollar base. The header should require the single forecast wherever the calculation uses that quarter’s **value**. Drop the reflection’s parenthetical that a growth rate is another quantity. The unit alone cannot tell whether the calculation uses it as a benchmark or to recover a second dollar value. Give readers the same distinction. [System text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Schema wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:152) · [Header](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:176) · [Proposed wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r56.md:21)\n    12\t\n    13\t4. **Serious — Disclose the $18B split as affecting O’s answer. D741 ruling 2 leaves it open.** The sources report a $25B July balance and $18B committed for the rest of the year. They do not establish that the $18B is included in the July balance or will all be paid by year-end. Those assumptions can support a conditional forecast. They do not establish a $7B company-schedule reference just because the subtraction carries no past share. R11 presents the funding schedule as something the company said and states only the overlap as an assumption. Without the deduction, the July balance alone starts in rung 2. The first run split on the same passage. This adds no registered stop and needs no Alex ruling before the $0 checker test. If the council still disagrees when deciding whether to advance, put the reference question to Alex explicitly. [Original passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:36) · [D741 ruling 2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11356) · [R07 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:9) · [First run’s split](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:58)\n    14\t\n    15\t   **Minor — Wrong numbers confined to prose are a disclosure under this test.** The registered definitions cover copied and computed figures. The errors still matter to a reader. R09’s example reaches $430.82B against a $431.2B threshold, and R11’s full-slip scenario gives $32B on its own assumptions. Neither adds a registered stop or needs Alex before the next $0 test. Making prose accuracy a stop condition would be a separate decision. [Reader definitions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169) · [R09 arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json:20) · [R11 arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R11.claude.json:49)\n    16\t\n    17\t5. **Recommendation and falsifier.** Narrow the checker to the demonstrated use of guidance as a growth benchmark, align the request and reader wording, and replay the 24 saved replies. The repair fails if R03 still refuses, either counterexample publishes, or another saved decision changes. Once that check passes, record the council decision and run **one fresh $0 blind test** with the revised request and the disagreement procedure stated up front. The old replies can prove what the checker does. Fresh replies show how writers read the new wording. Any registered stop keeps the work in council. A clean run goes to Alex with both disclosures for the separate paid-run decision. [Council authority](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4871) · [Registered next step](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n    18\t\n    19\tNo files were edited. The two counterexamples ran in memory.     1\t**Do not build the narrowed change.** It publishes an answer that counts **$112.5B** for Q3 in the annual sum, then resets Q4’s starting amount to the **$108B** Q3 guidance. The current checker refuses that answer.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The growth-rate exemption still lets a second quarterly value into the outcome.** I changed the saved P-v4 reply to start Q4 at `$112.5B × (1 + growth($112.5B, $108B))`, then grow that starting amount by 12%. The first step gives exactly $108B. The narrowed checker publishes **$411.28B** because it sees the adopted forecast in the base and skips the rate that cancels it. The same probe with the quoted guidance range publishes **$408.86B–$413.70B**. When I put the guidance directly in the base calculation as a dollar deduction, the narrowed checker still refused it. In a separate joint-answer probe, a Q4 Data Center base inherited the $110B company Q3 forecast through Q4, and a rate built from Q3 reset it to the $108B guidance basis. That also published. [Checker diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/calc_small.py.diff:22) · [Arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:230) · [Saved P-v4 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/claude.json:1) · [Joint fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:80)\n     4\t\n     5\t   **Minor — `rests_on` is an existing reader case.** I put the same substitution in a Q4 estimate that explicitly relied on the $108B guidance as its starting amount. Both versions published and recorded the competing Q3 figures. The check for two adopted forecasts follows `rests_on`. The check for bypassing a listed forecast follows arithmetic inputs. D738 assigns the use of a number inside an estimate to readers. [D738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11258) · [Adopted forecasts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:594) · [Reader records](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:732)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Serious — The code is both wider and narrower than Alex’s answer.** Alex approved using guidance to inform a 13% Q4 forecast when $113.6B supplies the only Q3 dollar amount. I encoded that example as a 13% estimate resting on the guidance-implied rate. Both versions published. The narrowed code also published when the rate used the outside $113.6B estimate in place of guidance. In the other direction, it refused the saved answer when I wrote the same Q4 calculation as `$112.5B × ($108B / $96.2B)`. The recomputed annual outcome was still **$416.62B**. It also refused the new Data Center example when I expressed the same $101.75B starting amount as an estimate resting on the shared company forecast and the mix. The rulings record puts the meaning of the benchmark in the request and reader brief. [Alex’s exact ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11346) · [Outside estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:258) · [Checker diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/calc_small.py.diff:22)\n     8\t\n     9\t3. **Wording — State the dollar-value rule consistently.** The schema says *guidance*, while the code skips any qualifying percentage. The header calls it the “one exception” and says “built on,” which leaves writers guessing whether an estimate resting on the forecast qualifies. Both reader versions preserve the single-dollar-value condition, but mentioning `apply_growth` invites readers to treat the operation as proof. The system’s “run-rate that carries the guided growth forward” leaves all of those readings open. Use the same plain rule across all four places: guidance can set the pace, and the adopted forecast supplies the quarter’s dollar value. Tell readers to look through the rate for a second dollar value. Any restriction on how writers must express the arithmetic belongs in the request. [System text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Schema and header diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/render_v13.py.diff:10) · [Reader diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/blind_test.py.diff:10) · [Adjudicator diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/blind_adjudicate.py.diff:3)\n    10\t\n    11\t4. **Minor — Both new mutants are decisive, and one of the three refusal cases overlaps an existing check.** Both new passing cases fail with the exemption disabled. I also tried requiring the forecast as the immediate base. Only the Data Center case failed, so it earns its place. The Q2-built run-rate is the only case that catches removal of the base condition. The repeated-multiple case catches a return to the blanket cutoff. The explicitly labelled rebuilt Q3 still refuses under the current, blanket, narrowed and no-base versions because the walk sees the rebuilt Q3 figure before reaching its rate. It adds a computed-figure example to the existing direct-bypass check. All 109 cases and all 42 mutants reproduced in memory. The stored-reply replay also reproduced one change in 24, the saved P-v4 refusal. The decisive missing pair is the saved valid answer and the guidance-reset version that this round showed can publish beside it. [New cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/small_cases.py.diff:17) · [Existing bypass case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:388) · [New mutants](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/small_mutants.py.diff:10) · [Stored-reply census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4_census.py:1)\n    12\t\n    13\t**Verdict — Do not build.**     1\t**Choose A′ with revised wording.** The saved answer used $112.5B for Q3 and used guidance to set Q4’s pace. The blanket change would also let an answer quietly switch Q4’s starting point back to $108B.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — Keep S4.** D738 explicitly checks whether the arithmetic reaches a figure for a listed shared quantity without going through its forecast. D741 approves using guidance to inform an estimate. Alex’s example uses the roughly 12% guided pace to choose a 13% Q4 forecast, then applies 13% to the one Q3 forecast. The saved reply instead feeds the historical guidance-derived rate straight into the Q4 calculation. Its $416.62B result is financially defensible, as both refusal reviewers found. The way it was written still triggers the existing check. I replayed two guidance-reset examples in memory. The current check refused both, and B published both. The “one quantity, several figures” note also appears when guidance legitimately supports an estimate, so it cannot tell an analyst which happened. That trade favors A′ under D737 and D738. [D737 and D738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11236) · [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11346) · [Saved answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/claude.json:1) · [Refusal review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json:3) · [Reader note](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:752)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — The proposed wording still misses the boundary.** “Never as an input of a computed figure” bans more than the code does. “For a shared quantity” can also point writers at the wrong quantity. I checked all three cases in memory. [S4 implementation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:594)\n     6\t\n     7\t   - **Unlisted quantity.** Removing Q3 from the shared list made the identical arithmetic pass. The separate check for two adopted forecasts still applies to unlisted quantities. [Shared-quantity check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:615)\n     8\t   - **Outside Q3 figure.** Replacing guidance with the outside $113.6B produces the same refusal when the rate feeds Q4 directly. It passes when the outside rate informs a stated Q4 growth estimate. Readers still judge whether adopting that pace is supported. D741 specifically approves the guidance example. [Outside figure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:258) · [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348)\n     9\t   - **Data Center.** The direct rate is refused when the segment outcome reaches the listed **company** Q3 guidance through that rate. The same arithmetic passes when the writer states and supports an estimated **segment** growth rate using the company rate as a benchmark. The segment’s own Q3 forecast supplies the dollar base. [Joint fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:58) · [Joint request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:8)\n    10\t\n    11\t   **Wording — Change the header too.** Say “Follow every arithmetic path from each outcome, including through rates and multiples. For each listed shared quantity and period, the first figure of that quantity on every path that reaches it must be the same forecast.” In the system text, ask writers to *use guidance to inform the pace they forecast*. “At the guided pace” reads as requiring the exact rate. In the schema, say to record the chosen future pace as an estimate, explain why it applies, and name the benchmark in `rests_on`. The chosen rate can then feed the dollar calculation. That version passed with the same $416.62B and preserved recomputable arithmetic. Give readers the proposed instruction to look through rates for a second dollar value, including inside stated assumptions. [System instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Schema and header](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:112) · [Reader brief](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169)\n    12\t\n    13\t3. **Serious — Keep the stop line and register how readers apply it.** Record two judgments independently. Was the forecast and its reasoning financially sound? Did the exact request clearly state the condition that triggered refusal? A sound answer refused under a clear condition still stops the run. Calling it wrong solely because it broke the format would remove the protection the second half of the stop line provides. Registering those two questions up front preserves the bar and the previous STOP. Use the registered third reader to settle disagreements on the specific finding. Give the wording reviewer the system text, user text, and schema. The current adjudication packet omits the system text, which contains the instruction at issue here. [Registered stop and reader procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [Adjudication packet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:71) · [Prior STOP](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md:3)\n    14\t\n    15\t4. **Minor — Agree to one fresh $0 run with 12 writers after the wording and the procedure are registered.** First replay the saved refusal and the two reset examples, and show that the stated-estimate version passes. Keep the saved refusal as a test of the stated format, with its financial correctness still open to blind review. A fresh, financially sound refusal sends the format back to council. An accepted answer confirmed to carry two quarterly dollar values sends the publication decision back to council. Only a complete run that meets every registered line, including a usable answer for each case and both supported forecasts in one joint reply, goes to Alex for the paid-run decision. No files were edited. [Registered cases and next step](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:255)\n    16\t\n    17\t**Verdict — A′ with revised wording and the existing stop line.**     1\t1. **Serious — The third run stopped under the rule as registered.** R01 forecast $679.25–745 billion and chose rung 4, which starts at $700 billion. Both readers marked the same defect on F14. That independently triggers the stop at §5 line 252. D742 kept that line and assigned adjudication to disagreements. The adjudicators reviewed F14 because the packet also contained disagreements, and both correctly rejected the F14 mark. Their finding explains the stop without reversing it. The reader brief omitted the range exception. The writer’s schema allowed an overlapping band with a stated reason, and R01 gave one. I replayed the reply in memory. Rung 4 published, and changing only the selection to the disjoint rung 5 triggered S6. [Registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [D742 procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:279) · [Reader brief](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169) · [Writer’s schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:329) · [S6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:541) · [R01 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.claude.json)\n     2\t\n     3\t2. **Wording — The counts reconcile with two corrections to the report.** All 12 replies passed the schema and published on replay, with zero refusals. There were 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Both adjudicators rejected all eight individual defect marks, covering seven distinct allegations because F14 was marked twice. R02 and R09 account for **two** midpoint marks. Five replies used the permitted midpoint form. “No split remains” applies to defect marks only. Across 14 forecasts, 12 were supported, R07 was unsupported, and R06 stayed split. Every case had a usable reply on those readings, and both joint replies supported both forecasts. The 109 Part A checks also passed on replay. [Checker results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json) · [Tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:382) · [Report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:13) · [Part A results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-cases.log:110)\n     4\t\n     5\t3. **Serious — R07 exposes a forecast that can publish without enough evidence. It adds zero registered stops.** The arithmetic is right. At $745 billion of total spending, the 90% share gives $670.5 billion, and a share of about 94% reaches rung 4. Both adjudicators found the evidence too weak to justify the cut to 90%. I agree with that narrower conclusion. Meta’s figures are for one company, the nearly $800 billion estimate covers five, and the four-company $730 billion figure measures AI infrastructure. Those are reasons to question 90%, with no firm lower bound for the exact four-company data-center share. R01 supplied the usable reply that S needed. Carry R07 forward as a disclosed example of what the eventual paid measurement needs to reveal. [R07 calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:213) · [Source passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:29) · [R07 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:3) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json:1) · [Usability rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:263)\n     6\t\n     7\t4. **Serious — R01 has a separate problem that the range ruling leaves open.** It labels Zacks’s $720–745 billion of **all** capital spending as the market reference for **data-center** spending. The writer’s instructions ask for a reference for the question itself. Claude’s first reading noticed the mismatch in its explanation, and neither reader filed it as a named defect. The same outside tally also helps set the Microsoft estimate, so agreement with the tally adds little confidence. The adjudicators addressed that dependence when they rejected the two-forecasts mark. The reference mismatch adds zero stops to the recorded vote, but it matters because R01 is S’s sole usable reply. Fresh readers need to judge its substance as well as its range. [R01 reference](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:137) · [Zacks passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Writer’s reference instruction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt:4) · [First reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.claude.json:1) · [Adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json:17)\n     8\t\n     9\t5. **Minor — Two disclosures deserve more precise wording.** Both O replies depend on reading the $18 billion as funding from the July balance. R04 states the overlap and payment timing as estimates. R06 presents the $18 billion as the company’s schedule, and the adjudicators split on whether that reading supports the forecast. R11 also contains a prose error that a reader did catch. A $138 billion fourth quarter gives $428.32 billion for the year, below the $431.2 billion cutoff it was offered to illustrate. Its actual forecast still sits in rung 2, and the reader filed no named defect. I found no other confirmed registered stop. [O source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:36) · [R04 assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/answers/codex.json:172) · [R06 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.codex.json:3) · [R11 reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R11.claude.json:1)\n    10\t\n    11\t6. **Recommendation — Take the recorded STOP through Council B and ask Alex to authorize option (a).** Register the schema’s exact range rule in both reading instructions, then have fresh readers and adjudicators review all 12 original replies. Give them reshuffled IDs and access only to their packets. Keep prior votes, the answer key, and the expected result from them. Register the wording and scoring before release, with blinded controls for an explained overlap and a disjoint band. Reusing the replies can bias the people choosing the correction. Those controls and the full reread test whether the correction works beyond the case that prompted it. Option (b) would introduce fresh writer variation into a question about how the same answers were read. Option (c) is the right approval step, with the original STOP on the record. A clean reread can support a separate paid-run request to Alex. [Council B requirement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11318) · [Authority after a registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:339) · [Paid-run decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n    12\t\n    13\t   **Falsifier.** If the corrected reading rejects the valid overlap or accepts the disjoint control, the repair fails. If the reread hits any registered stop, including S losing its sole usable reply, the result stays with the council.     1\t**The registered STOP stands. Both range controls passed their range check, and both adjudicators agreed that Q10’s $108 billion midpoint was valid. Ask Alex to authorize one more blinded read with the full instructions the writers saw.**\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — Q10 was scored correctly.** Both first readers marked the same figure as misread. Under the registered rule, that alone stops the run. Both adjudicators cleared the mark because the writer quoted “$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%” in full and used the permitted midpoint. The two range controls also behaved as intended. Both readers flagged the $36–43 billion range when it was placed in the disjoint $20–30 billion band. Neither flagged the overlapping band or Q07’s range. Across the 12 original replies, the re-read left 12 of 14 forecasts supported and two split. Every case still had a usable reply, and both joint replies supported both forecasts. [Registered rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [Q10 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:29) · [Re-read tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:190)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Minor — The overlap control passed a narrower test than the report’s wording suggests.** The builder added the new $36–43 billion forecast but kept the old 85% growth assumption and $37 billion forecast in the explanation. Both readers correctly flagged the competing figures. That leaves the range result intact, though “must not be marked” is broad enough to suggest a clean answer was expected. The disjoint control also retained prose naming the correct band, giving readers a second clue. Clean both explanations and render the controls like the original replies. Also fix the control adjudication path before relying on it. If readers split, the builder would look for an original writer file named `C-overlap.json` or `C-disjoint.json`. Neither control split on its range mark this time. [Control registration](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:306) · [Control builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread.py:23) · [Overlap reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:14) · [Adjudication builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:84)\n     6\t\n     7\t3. **Serious — Give readers the same rule text as adjudicators.** Use one shared source for the writer’s system text, all schema descriptions, and the definitions readers apply. The current readers got the passages and answer. The adjudicators also got the writer’s rules, which resolved both the midpoint mark and Q10’s separate rounding mark. I counted **11 midpoint marks** across the four readings, in groups of 4, 3, 2, and 2. Both adjudicators rejected every one. The risk runs the other way too. A format permission can make readers overlook a wrong value or give too much credit to a weak assumption. Have readers check the quote and the calculation against the format, and separately say which evidence supports the assumption that puts the forecast in its chosen band. [Reader packet builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread.py:63) · [Adjudication rules and writer instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:24) · [Q10 rounding decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:16)\n     8\t\n     9\t4. **Serious — The spending forecast still shows how much the result can depend on who reads it.** The first adjudicators rejected the 90% share as unsupported. Both fresh readers accepted the identical reply. At $745 billion of total spending, 90% gives $670.5 billion, while about 94% reaches $700 billion and changes the band. The cited Meta figure covers one company, and the Zacks passage offers only “a large portion” for AI infrastructure. The other spending reply, Q07, stayed split even with the full format rules in adjudication. Its reference also still measures total spending. A second re-read can test whether readers apply the format consistently. A fresh vote on these same replies gives limited evidence about whether the spending forecast is reliable. [First ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:3) · [Fresh reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.claude.json:1) · [Q07 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:3) · [Council 57](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r57.md:7)\n    10\t\n    11\t**Recommendation.** Take the STOP and the spending disagreement to Alex now. Ask him to authorize one bounded, blinded re-read of the same 12 replies, with the shared instructions and controls agreed before release. A fourth writer run would change the answers while the open question is how readers judge them. D743 authorized the range repair and one re-read. [Alex’s authorization](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11434)\n    12\t\n    13\t**Falsifier.** The revised reading must accept the full quote recorded as $108 billion and catch the identical quote recorded as $108.5 billion. I checked that changing only that value triggers the number check. It must also distinguish clean overlapping and disjoint bands, and flag a format-correct forecast whose decisive assumption lacks support. A failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read. Report both spending replies’ support votes and reasons alongside the verdict. [Source-number check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:477) · [Registered scoring](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:319)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
