{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=10950&&NR<=10993 || NR>=11305&&NR<=11369 || NR>=11449&&NR<=11489 {printf \\\"%d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\",NR,\"'$0}'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '319,373p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '437,529p'; tail -n 12 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=10950&&NR<=10993 || NR>=11305&&NR<=11369 || NR>=11449&&NR<=11489 {printf \\\"%d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\",NR,\"'$0}'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '319,373p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '437,529p'; tail -n 12 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"10950\t    is unmeasured, and no change is made.\n10951\t  - A parallel replay left unfinished before 3ba40d8f cannot resume after it. The saved settings key changed with\n10952\t    `Setting.link_questions`, as D706 recorded. None is unfinished now.\n10953\t- **3ba40d8f does not change poc-v118's judging or filing.** The v7 request matched byte for byte on 43 stored views. `maxItems` applies\n10954\t  only where a format declares it, and v118 proposes no links.\n10955\t- **Checks:** suite 389 passed; frozen-piece tests 21 passed.\n10956\t\n10957\t## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n10958\t\n10959\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-22): \"work with counsel and have a look at the traces of this problem … what is wrong with the system design\n10960\t  overall … look at the code, look at the traces, deeply battle it out with counsel … because I think it's the most important thing\n10961\t  that we don't hallucinate with the numbers, and I want you to get this right\".\n10962\t- **The record:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` (`RESULT.md`).\n10963\t  - Claude's reflection was written before Codex's.\n10964\t  - Codex ran an independent investigation (fresh thread `01a20501…`) and a challenge round.\n10965\t  - Consensus. One wording point stays open: the $146B is arithmetically a residual, but the order of the judge's choices is unknown.\n10966\t- **Diagnosis:**\n10967\t  1. Answers are prose; code ties no figure to a source and recomputes nothing. The production check passes contradictory or invented\n10968\t     figures.\n10969\t  2. Related questions compute the same quantities separately. The unit is one question, with 0 dependencies.\n10970\t  3. Filing and the evidence in view split their inputs. The previous conclusion carries figures without provenance.\n10971\t  4. Opening's bands amplify an error.\n10972\t- **Measured:**\n10973\t  - Data Center Q4 above company Q4 on at least 13 of 26 days in old-1;\n10974\t  - Data Center's full year above the total's on 20 consecutive days in old-2;\n10975\t  - different Q3 bases on 17 of 18 paired days;\n10976\t  - shared evidence between total and Data Center 8% in old-2;\n10977\t  - 17 of the 24 double-rejected answers from unchecked arithmetic.\n10978\t- **Recommended design** (a new judge version, Alex's to open):\n10979\t  - one judging call per related family, each question keeping its answer from one calculation;\n10980\t  - figures labelled by the judge (source, calculation, assumption; scope and period);\n10981\t  - code checks only identity and arithmetic (figure in quote, recompute, part within whole, one value per shared quantity, a sourced\n10982\t    reference);\n10983\t  - unreconciled forecasts shown as unresolved;\n10984\t  - extraction unchanged;\n10985\t  - families declared for the test.\n10986\t- **Interim for a first release:** number questions shown as \"Forecast unavailable\" until they pass the checks.\n10987\t- **The test:** a $0 gate (hand reconstructions of five stored cases, four gold controls, three planted errors), then a paid gate\n10988\t  (36 calls; joint against separate on the three draws' 09-20 pair, plus O, S and E singletons). Pass and stop lines are in `RESULT.md`.\n10989\t- **Not done:** nothing built, sent or changed.\n10990\t\n10991\t## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n10992\t\n10993\t- **The questions** (after D725):\n11305\t  - **S5/S7:** `rests_on` is enforced for estimates only and `inputs` for computed figures only, and numbers must be finite.\n11306\t  - **The runner:** it now refuses a reply that is not exactly one JSON object, an incomplete inventory, and a ladder out of order.\n11307\t    A checker error is recorded per reply.\n11308\t- **Checks:**\n11309\t  - `small_cases.py`: 100 of 100, each an exact rule set;\n11310\t  - `small_mutants.py`: 38 mutants caught, and the control passes;\n11311\t  - `small_rehearsal.py`: 19 of 19, including a defective and a correct answer for each named defect through the gate path, and the old\n11312\t    run reproduced byte for byte;\n11313\t  - the old harness unchanged: rehearsal 63 of 63, the $0 gate 87 of 87, the old plan's bytes identical.\n11314\t- **Size:**\n11315\t  - The v13s schema is about 14,300 characters against about 9,600, and a whole pair request about 140,000 characters against\n11316\t    134,000. It is larger, not smaller as SMALL-DESIGN.md §4 expected.\n11317\t  - The reason: the fields the old request left undefined now carry their rules (AGENTS §6).\n11318\t- **Decided:**\n11319\t  - The blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5) runs now, without a further design round. It costs $0 at the provider.\n11320\t  - Its stop conditions measure the same classes empirically: readers mark the five defects on every reply, and an accepted reply with\n11321\t    a marked defect stops the test.\n11322\t  - Its result goes to a council round (Council B) before anything goes to Alex.\n11323\t- **Output:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`.\n11324\t\n11325\t## D740 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s blind test stops on a checker error and a reader-marked defect; council round 55 sends three reader rulings to Alex\n11326\t\n11327\t- **Result** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`; $0 at the\n11328\t  provider):\n11329\t  - All 12 first answers (six cases, Claude and Codex writers) were schema-valid, and 11 passed every check.\n11330\t  - **Stop 1:** S1 refused a right answer. \"From $35 billion to $170 billion\" was read as a range, through round 54's \"to\" range form\n11331\t    (Claude's mistake). The old checker had excluded that form for exactly this ambiguity.\n11332\t  - **Stop 2:** both readers and both adjudicators marked R06, a guidance-implied Q3 growth benchmark used while forecasting Q3 above\n11333\t    guidance, as \"two forecasts of one quarter\". Both adjudicators rejected R12's claim, which has the same pattern.\n11334\t  - The reader families split on a computed market reference (consensus total × last quarter's segment share; R02, R05, R08, R12) and\n11335\t    on total capex used as data-center capex (R09).\n11336\t- **Council round 55** (`codex-r55.md`):\n11337\t  - STOP is correct as registered, and the counts reconcile (three wordings corrected).\n11338\t  - R06 and R12 are the same method, so the readers' boundary is unstable.\n11339\t  - The three rulings go to Alex.\n11340\t  - **Next:**\n11341\t    - fix the S1 range;\n11342\t    - apply the rulings, in the reader brief and in code only where the structure decides them;\n11343\t    - verify both reader families on the stored boundary replies;\n11344\t    - re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers, then the paid re-send request.\n11345\t\n11346\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n11347\t\n11348\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\n11349\t  - 【增速参考】\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\n11350\t  - 【市场参照】\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\n11351\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\n11352\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"不算错 (推荐)\"; \"不能 (推荐)\"; \"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\".\n11353\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n11354\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\n11355\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\n11356\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\n11357\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\n11358\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\n11359\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\n11360\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\n11361\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\n11362\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\n11363\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\n11364\t  - Then three steps:\n11365\t    1. fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range (D740);\n11366\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n11367\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\n11368\t\n11369\t## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\n11449\t## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n11450\t\n11451\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §4; `REREAD-TALLY.json`):\n11452\t  - The range repair works on both controls, and R01/Q07 is no longer marked.\n11453\t  - **STOP as written on Q10** (P-v8, Codex writer). Both readers marked \"number misread\" on \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as\n11454\t    its midpoint. Both adjudicators rejected it under the schema rule. The same mark has appeared 11 times across four readings, and\n11455\t    every adjudication rejected it.\n11456\t  - No settled defect. Q07 (S, Claude writer) is split at both levels, and Q09 (O, Claude writer) is split again.\n11457\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) is \"supported\" by both fresh readers after being \"not supported\" by both first adjudicators: reader\n11458\t    judgement noise on a decisive assumption.\n11459\t- **The mechanism.** Readers get the passages and the answer but not the writer's format rules, while adjudicators get both. Both stops\n11460\t  caused by the instrument come from that gap.\n11461\t  - C-overlap's shared \"two forecasts\" mark is Claude's control text: it kept the original \"85% / $37 billion\" beside the new range.\n11462\t  - C-disjoint kept prose naming the right band.\n11463\t- **Council round 58** (`codex-r58.md`):\n11464\t  - It confirmed the STOP.\n11465\t  - **Structural rule:** readers get the same rule text as adjudicators, from one shared source: the writer's system text, every schema\n11466\t    description, and the defect definitions. They check quote and calculation against the format, and separately name the evidence for\n11467\t    the assumption that sets the band.\n11468\t  - **Controls, rebuilt clean:**\n11469\t    - a permitted midpoint must pass;\n11470\t    - 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\" must be marked;\n11471\t    - a clean overlap must pass;\n11472\t    - a clean disjoint band must be marked;\n11473\t    - a format-correct forecast with an unsupported decisive assumption must be flagged.\n11474\t  - The control adjudication path must resolve control answers.\n11475\t  - Recommendation: take the STOP and the spending disagreement to Alex, and ask him to authorize one bounded blinded re-read of the\n11476\t    same 12 replies. A fourth writer run would change the answers while the open question is the reading.\n11477\t  - Falsifier: any failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read. Both spending replies' votes and reasons are reported beside\n11478\t    the verdict.\n11479\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22) to \"要不要批准再重读一次？\": \"批准再重读一次 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不再重读，直接议花钱重发\" and\n11480\t  \"先停在这里\".\n11481\t- **Decided (Alex):** one bounded blinded re-read under the shared rule text, with the five controls. Any failed control or registered\n11482\t  stop ends it. A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n   319\t- Pair every structural check with its semantic one. Run a data rule over its whole input range and on cases it was not tuned on. Weigh\n   320\t  a case by what it carries in the product, not by count.\n   321\t- A claim about a population (\"the data is clean\") rests on a code sweep of the whole population and an independent read of a fresh\n   322\t  random sample, never on the pages the author looked at.\n   323\t- Cost is priced from tokens at the published rates (`hivemind.model.PRICES`, cached input at `model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE`), never from a\n   324\t  stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 7.3 Iterating\n   327\t\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   346\t- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n   347\t  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n   348\t  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   349\t  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n   350\t  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n   351\t  the next test goes straight ahead, held to the PRD and its quantified goals, with every trace read by you yourself and not only\n   352\t  by readers (Alex, 2026-11-10: \"你应该跑council loop 来reflect 你每一轮的思路还有决策，需要你打成共识，记录决策，向前直行测试。不要drift,我们有PRD和量化目标，\n   353\t  你自己作为llm 下去看所有trace\").\n   354\t- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n   355\t  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n   356\t  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n   357\t  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n   358\t  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n   359\t  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n   360\t  control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the model's own account and the\n   361\t  request locate where the decision is made (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你跑这么多次真的有意义吗\"; \"我感觉你在为了跑而跑\"). Several fixes on one\n   362\t  base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02: \"快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整\"; D266).\n   363\t- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n   364\t  rounds of review (Claude's own, Codex's, an independent reader's) on the traces until a round finds nothing serious; never a menu of\n   365\t  arms or options, never a conclusion the next probe overturns (a choice that changes what the system does still goes to him with its\n   366\t  options and a recommendation, Authority and scope) (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"就测试一种，一次 … 你就没有自己看5轮review，能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议？\").\n   367\t  An agreed recommendation is first challenged by an independent reader on the concrete evidence behind it (\"why you give\n   368\t  recommendations for this. What did you see exactly?\"). A review round reads a rule as its author meant it; only a send shows how the\n   369\t  model applies it: before a registered test, the changed request is sent on the cases that motivated it, a few cents.\n   370\t- **A failed arm is diagnosed before the next one.** When an arm fixes its class and regresses another, write the root cause first: the\n   371\t  process error (what grew, what the small sample did not guard) and the mechanism, traced to the raw answers and the model's account,\n   372\t  never a guess stated as the cause. A small sample guards every class the change can move, not only its own. When a fix keeps moving\n   373\t  another class, the call is doing several jobs: move the rule to its owner (a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field)\n   437\t  - delivery: the decisive statements reached the judge on 4/4 primary questions in both draws;\n   438\t  - talks stayed talks;\n   439\t  - both blind readers supported all 4 hybrid answers;\n   440\t  - no five-company or 2027 figure was taken as the answer;\n   441\t  - c8 proposed no NVIDIA-figure question.\n   442\t- **Missed:**\n   443\t  - P2's proxy clause: total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy, which old also misses;\n   444\t  - V: the rebuilt control's v47 was unsupported, and a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n   445\t- **Not shown:** that the merge answers better. The old answers were already supported, and the capex band was set by 09-06 history.\n   446\t- **Next:** superseded by D681, below.\n   447\t\n   448\t**The one next task: Alex chooses whether to authorize one more bounded blinded re-read (D744).**\n   449\t\n   450\t- **Why it is needed.** The D743 re-read fixed the range rule, but stopped as written on Q10: both readers marked the permitted \"plus or\n   451\t  minus 2%\" midpoint, and both adjudicators rejected the mark.\n   452\t- **Council 58** recommends readers get the same rule text as adjudicators (system text, schema descriptions, definitions; one shared\n   453\t  source), plus five clean controls (midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported decisive assumption).\n   454\t- **Reported beside the verdict:** both S replies' support votes, because the 90% share reply flipped between reader sets.\n   455\t\n   456\tThe record of the D743 re-read's setup:\n   457\t\n   458\t**Earlier next task (done, D744): Alex chooses how to proceed after the third blind run's STOP (D743). Council 57 recommends a blinded\n   459\tre-read of the same 12 replies with the reader brief corrected to the schema's range rule, plus overlap and disjoint-band controls.**\n   460\t\n   461\t- **The third run** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md`):\n   462\t  - The writer side is clean: 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, and D742's rule followed by all six P-family writers.\n   463\t  - The run stopped as written because both readers marked R01's range across a rung edge.\n   464\t  - Both adjudicators rejected that mark under the schema's range clause, which the reader brief lacks (Claude's error).\n   465\t- **Disclosed:**\n   466\t  - R07's unsupported 90% share (accepted, rung 3 against passages near 100%);\n   467\t  - O's $18B reading, split in three runs;\n   468\t  - R01's total-capex reference for a data-center question;\n   469\t  - R11's prose example missing its edge.\n   470\t\n   471\tThe record of the third run's setup (D742):\n   472\t\n   473\t**Earlier next task (done, D743): run the third v13s blind test (12 fresh writers, then readers, reviewers and adjudicators) on the\n   474\trequest D742 fixed, and score it on the registered lines (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D742]).**\n   475\t\n   476\t- **The re-run stopped on one line** (D742, `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md`).\n   477\t  - S4 refused a sound answer that computed a guidance growth rate straight into Q4.\n   478\t  - 12 of 12 were schema-valid and 11 of 12 accepted. Every case was usable. No defect was settled.\n   479\t- **The cause was the request.** The system text invited carrying the guided growth forward directly, while the schema and S4 allow a\n   480\t  guidance rate only as an estimate's benchmark (Alex's ruling 1 as written).\n   481\t- **Council 56–56c.** A blanket exemption and a narrowed exemption each let a reset to guidance publish. So S4 stays, and the request\n   482\t  states one rule in three places: guidance informs the pace; the pace is an estimate naming its benchmark; the quarter's dollar value\n   483\t  is the one forecast figure.\n   484\t- **Checks:** cases 109 of 109, mutants 42 of 42, rehearsal 19 of 19. The v13 plan hash is unchanged. `s4_replay.py` refuses the saved\n   485\t  reply and both council resets, and passes the stated-estimate form.\n   486\t- **Two reader splits are disclosed, not stops:**\n   487\t  - O's \"$18 billion committed for the remainder of its fiscal year\" reading, which decides O's rung;\n   488\t  - wrong numbers in prose that no figure carries.\n   489\t- **Run folder:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`.\n   490\t- **What each outcome sends where:**\n   491\t  - a fresh sound refusal, or an accepted reply confirmed to carry two values of a quarter, goes back to council;\n   492\t  - a run meeting every line goes to Alex as the paid re-send request.\n   493\t\n   494\tThe record before D742:\n   495\t\n   496\t**Earlier next task (done, D742): re-run the v13s blind test once with fresh writers and readers, after the reader calibration on\n   497\tAlex's rulings (D740, D741).**\n   498\t\n   499\t- **The first blind test stopped** (D740, `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`):\n   500\t  - 12 of 12 first answers were schema-valid, and 11 of 12 passed every check.\n   501\t  - **Stop 1:** S1 read \"from $35 billion to $170 billion\" as a range. This was Claude's change, now fixed: an \"X to Y\" after \"from\" is a\n   502\t    change between two values.\n   503\t  - **Stop 2:** the readers marked a guidance-implied growth benchmark as \"two forecasts of one quarter\".\n   504\t- **Alex ruled the readers' boundary questions** (D741):\n   505\t  - a guidance-implied growth benchmark is not a defect;\n   506\t  - consensus total × a past quarter's share is not the market's reference, and S5 now refuses it;\n   507\t  - a total used for a narrower quantity must state its deduction or a supported bound.\n   508\t- **Checks:** cases 104 of 104, mutants 40 of 40, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the old harness unchanged.\n   509\t- **The reader calibration on six stored boundary replies** (`calibration-D741/`) runs before the re-run.\n   510\t- **A clean re-run** goes to Alex as the paid re-send request.\n   511\t\n   512\tThe record before D740:\n   513\t\n   514\t- **The format shrank** (D737). Alex chose to shrink it after round 51 (D736) found the full restatement unbounded.\n   515\t- **What code refuses** (D738): only the five named defects, where the calculation's own structure decides them, plus broken\n   516\t  structure. Indirect forms are recorded for readers.\n   517\t- **The small format is built and rehearsed at $0** (D739):\n   518\t  - `calc_small.py`, the v13s schema and header, and format selection in `paid_gate.py`;\n   519\t  - cases 100 of 100, mutants 38 of 38, rehearsal 19 of 19;\n   520\t  - the old harness unchanged.\n   521\t- **The blind test** (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5): six registered requests, each written by a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only\n   522\t  Codex session, in `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`. Two readers then grade every reply. A clean result goes to Alex as the\n   523\t  request for the paid re-send. Nothing paid is sent before that.\n   524\t\n   525\tThe record before D737 (superseded as the next task):\n   526\t\n   527\t**Earlier next task: finish judging-v13's repair to the council's line, then run the $0 blind test (D734, D735).**\n   528\t\n   529\t- **Alex chose the repair** (D734; his words are in the decision log).\n| 30 | 11-05 | Alex：\"你充分验证\"。乙的全部 22 道题验证：设计（`baseline-design.md` 第 8 轮）——文件由代码选（开始跟踪前最近一个财报日的财报类材料：5 月 20 日新闻稿和 CFO 说明）；读法和第二版一字不差，每题每份文件 3 次；判断照 PRD 10 §4 当天原样重发：三遍各自拿窗口末最后一次判断的存档请求，对照原样 3 次，起点组只在证据块最前面加上读到的数；过关三条事先写下（v10、v12 各 ≥ 6/9；不弄坏：同一遍里重排的精确检验显著少、或少 2 次以上且两人读理由认定是起点数带偏；读到的数五类分类，读错、别的量、不是数一个都不能有） | 发之前修了两处：读法的检查原来要求\"引用的最后一个数就是读到的数\"，照抄整句的正常引用会被拒（4 句全拒），改成\"读到的数印在引用里\"（对照 32 例全对，两个弄坏的版本分别抓出 5、1 个错；试点重放只有 v10 第 3 次那两条整行变成收下，v10 变 3/3）；发送脚本按冻结尺子补上是非题判法、\"档动了\"对着请求里那次上一次结论查（三遍存档 57 个结论和 `score_judgement.py` 逐题一致，弄坏的判法被抓出）。审查：Codex 4 轮（第 1 轮 5 条严重、第 2 轮 3 条严重，都属实都改；第 3、4 轮无严重问题），独立读者 1 次（请求干净、没有金标泄漏；挪档看不见、单位后缀、口径错等照改） | 设计和 8 个脚本锁定（`baseline-all.sha256`）；发读数 132 次 |\n| 31 | 11-05 | 乙的全部 22 道题验证，照锁定的设计测一次：读 132 次（0.083 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、判断 144 次（0.344 美元），三遍各自和当天原样重发的对照比 | **第 1 条过**：v10、v12 各 9/9 判对。**第 2 条过**：读到数的 7 道判过的题两组选档逐次一样，只有 v22 第 2 遍少 1 次（7/9 对 6/9，理由显示是本来的抽样起伏，起点组写明\"没有把一季度乘 4\"）。**第 3 条没过**：53 个收下的数里 5 个是别的量——v19（Vera CPU 收入）两次收了\"数据中心计算收入 604 亿\"，v20（Blackwell 和 Rubin 收入）两次收了\"数据中心收入 752 亿\"和\"网络收入 148 亿\"；判断都没被带偏（v19 理由写明计算收入里没有单列 Vera CPU） | 照登记停，交 Alex |\n| 32 | 11-05 | 问模型自己为什么收那 5 个数（3 次，约 0.3 美分，`probe-baseline-account.md`） | 3 次都指向读法任务里那一句\"报告没用题目的名字写这个量时，写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"，并自己说这些数不是这道题的量、不该写。这一句是 v10、v12 要用的，在报告根本没有这个量的题上把模型推去写更大或旁边的行 | 建议：只改这一句的边界（只收\"由它算出的那几行\"和\"换了名字的同一个量\"，更大的、旁边的行不算），按提示词审计改，再在 22 道题上测一次；等 Alex 的话 |\n| 33 | 11-05 | Alex：\"改\"。只改读法里那一句：原句\"写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"→ 新句\"只写算出它的那几行，或换了名字的同一个量；更大的行、旁边的行都不写\"（任务 790 → 889 字符，理由：抽象说法换成闭合两类加边界）。提示词审计、Codex 只读审 1 轮（1 条严重：发送失败会被当成白卷，已改成\"不完整、不下结论\"）；先在 v19、v20、v10、v12 上试发 24 次（1.6 美分） | 试发过：v19、v20 三次都白卷，v10 3/3；v12 2/3——问模型（2 次）：新句的\"更大的行\"半句让它把\"购置固定资产和无形资产\"当成比资本开支宽的一行，其中一次重想后改口\"就是资本开支\" | 照登记测全部 22 道题 |\n| 34 | 11-05 | 第三版的登记测试：和第 8 轮一样，只换这一句；读 132 次（0.078 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、读到数的 7 道题三遍重判、当天对照（108 次，0.268 美元） | **三条全过**：v10、v12 各 9/9；判对的题一道没变错（只有 v22 第 2 遍反过来对照 0/3、起点组 3/3，理由是在 41% 和 32% 两个增长估计间摇摆，和第 13 轮方向相反，与起点数无关）；46 个数全是自己的量（2 条是标明的二季度指引），两人分类全一致；v19、v20 的错数全没了，其余 15 道白卷、没冒出新错。没盖住：资本开支那一行试发时漏过 1 次；指引也被写成\"事实\"；一路判下来的效果和跨题效果要端到端才量得到 | 交 Alex：要不要做进生产、跑 14 天三遍端到端 |\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '9,40p;70,155p;375,405p;437,505p' && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '75,160p' && nl -ba blind_rules.py && nl -ba blind_reread2.py | sed -n '1,187p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '9,40p;70,155p;375,405p;437,505p' && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '75,160p' && nl -ba blind_rules.py && nl -ba blind_reread2.py | sed -n '1,187p'\"","aggregated_output":"     9\timport itertools\n    10\timport math\n    11\timport re\n    12\t\n    13\tKINDS = {'source', 'computed', 'estimate'}\n    14\tBASES = {'reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'}\n    15\tEXTERNAL = {'consensus', 'guidance', 'third_party'}\n    16\tUNITS = {'USD bn', '%', 'x'}\n    17\tARITY = {'sum': (2, None), 'product': (2, None), 'difference': (2, 2), 'ratio': (2, 2), 'share': (2, 2), 'growth': (2, 2),\n    18\t         'apply_growth': (2, 2)}\n    19\tQUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\n    20\t                            'roughly', 'nearly', 'almost', 'below', 'under', 'near'], key=len, reverse=True)\n    21\tQUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\n    22\tQUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\n    23\tSCALE = {'trillion': 1000.0, 'tn': 1000.0, 't': 1000.0, 'billion': 1.0, 'bn': 1.0, 'b': 1.0, 'million': 0.001, 'mn': 0.001, 'm': 0.001}\n    24\tLIMIT = 1e9                                              # [54] a number beyond this is refused, not computed\n    25\tMAX_RANGE_LEAVES = 12\n    26\t\n    27\t_NUM = r'(\\d[\\d,]*(?:\\.\\d+)?)'\n    28\t_CUR = r'(?:US)?\\$\\s?'\n    29\t_SC = r'(?:\\s*(trillion|billion|million|bn|tn|mn|t|b|m)\\b)?'\n    30\t_MINUS = r'([-−–]\\s?)?'\n    31\t_PCT = r'\\s*(?:%|percent)'\n    32\t_BODIES = [   # [54] the complete numeric expressions, longest forms first; a quote_number must be one of them, whole\n    33\t    ('relative', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*,?\\s*\\(?\\s*(?:plus or minus|\\+/-|±)\\s*' + _NUM + r'\\s*%\\s*\\)?'),\n    34\t    ('range', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*(?:–|—|-|to)\\s*(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n    35\t    ('range', r'between\\s+' + _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s+and\\s+(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n    36\t    ('percent_range', r'between\\s+' + _NUM + r'\\s*%?\\s+and\\s+' + _NUM + _PCT),\n    37\t    ('percent_range', _NUM + r'\\s*%?\\s*(?:–|—|-|to)\\s*' + _NUM + _PCT),\n    38\t    ('money', _MINUS + _CUR + _NUM + _SC),\n    39\t    ('percent', _MINUS + _NUM + _PCT),\n    40\t    ('multiple', r'(double|triple|twice)'),\n    70\t        if form == 'percent_range':\n    71\t            return {'unit': '%', 'low': _num(g[0]), 'high': _num(g[1])}\n    72\t        if form == 'money':\n    73\t            return {'unit': 'USD bn', 'value': (-1.0 if g[0] else 1.0) * _money(g[1], g[2])}\n    74\t        if form == 'percent':\n    75\t            return {'unit': '%', 'value': (-1.0 if g[0] else 1.0) * _num(g[1])}\n    76\t        return {'unit': 'x', 'value': 3.0 if g[0].lower() == 'triple' else 2.0}\n    77\t    return None\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t_CHANGE = re.compile(r'(?<!rang)(?<!range)(?<!ranges)(?<!ranging)\\bfrom\\s*$', re.I)\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\tdef expressions(text):\n    84\t    \"\"\"[54] Every complete numeric expression in a quote, as (start, end), longest forms first and never overlapping. [D740] An\n    85\t    'X to Y' written after 'from' (not 'range(s) from' or 'ranging from') is a change between two values, so its two amounts are\n    86\t    separate expressions.\"\"\"\n    87\t    taken = []\n    88\t    for form, pattern in _SCAN:\n    89\t        for m in pattern.finditer(text):\n    90\t            if form == 'range' and re.search(r'\\bto\\b', m.group(0), re.I) and not m.group(0).lower().startswith('between'):\n    91\t                before = text[:m.start()].rstrip().lower()\n    92\t                if before.endswith('from') and not re.search(r'\\b(range|ranges|ranged|ranging)\\s+from$', before):\n    93\t                    continue\n    94\t            if not any(m.start() < e and s < m.end() for s, e in taken):\n    95\t                taken.append((m.start(), m.end()))\n    96\t    return sorted(taken)\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\tdef _key(what):\n   100\t    return (what['population'], what['measure'], what['scope'], what['period'])\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\tdef _quantity(what):\n   104\t    return (what['population'], what['measure'], what['scope'])\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\tdef _same(a, b):\n   108\t    return a is not None and b is not None and abs(a - b) <= 1e-9 * max(1.0, abs(a), abs(b))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\tdef _close(declared, expected, unit):\n   112\t    return abs(declared - expected) <= max(0.005 * abs(expected), 0.05 if unit == '%' else 0.1)\n   113\t\n   114\t\n   115\tdef _is_range(fig):\n   116\t    return isinstance(fig.get('low'), (int, float)) and isinstance(fig.get('high'), (int, float))\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\tdef _interval(fig):\n   120\t    if _is_range(fig):\n   121\t        return float(fig['low']), float(fig['high'])\n   122\t    return float(fig['value']), float(fig['value'])\n   123\t\n   124\t\n   125\tdef _period(text):\n   126\t    m = _PERIOD.match((text or '').strip())\n   127\t    if not m:\n   128\t        return None\n   129\t    year, part = m.group(1) + m.group(2), m.group(3)\n   130\t    if part is None:\n   131\t        quarters = {1, 2, 3, 4}\n   132\t    elif part.startswith('H'):\n   133\t        quarters = {1, 2} if part == 'H1' else {3, 4}\n   134\t    else:\n   135\t        a, _, b = part.partition('-')\n   136\t        lo, hi = int(a[1]), int((b or a)[1])\n   137\t        if lo > hi:\n   138\t            return None\n   139\t        quarters = set(range(lo, hi + 1))\n   140\t    return year, quarters\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\tdef _overlap(p1, p2):\n   144\t    \"\"\"[54] Two periods overlap: the same label, or parsed fiscal periods of one year that share a quarter.\"\"\"\n   145\t    if p1 == p2:\n   146\t        return True\n   147\t    a, b = _period(p1), _period(p2)\n   148\t    return bool(a and b and a[0] == b[0] and a[1] & b[1])\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t# ---------- decoding the wire shape ----------\n   152\tFIGURE_FIELDS = ('id', 'what', 'kind', 'basis', 'value', 'low', 'high', 'unit', 'quote_number', 'qualifier', 'inputs', 'operation',\n   153\t                 'basis_text', 'rests_on')\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   375\t        errors.append(('S7', f'{fid} is defined twice'))\n   376\t    for f in calc['figures']:\n   377\t        fid = f.get('id')\n   378\t        if not isinstance(fid, str) or not fid:\n   379\t            errors.append(('S7', 'a figure has no id'))\n   380\t            continue\n   381\t        if fid in passages:\n   382\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure id must not be a passage id'))\n   383\t        if f.get('kind') not in KINDS or f.get('basis') not in BASES or f.get('unit') not in UNITS:\n   384\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: kind, basis or unit outside the closed set'))\n   385\t            continue\n   386\t        numbers = [f.get(k) for k in ('value', 'low', 'high') if isinstance(f.get(k), (int, float))]\n   387\t        if any(not math.isfinite(n) or abs(n) > LIMIT for n in numbers):\n   388\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a number that is not finite or beyond {LIMIT:g}'))\n   389\t            continue\n   390\t        has_value = isinstance(f.get('value'), (int, float))\n   391\t        has_low, has_high = isinstance(f.get('low'), (int, float)), isinstance(f.get('high'), (int, float))\n   392\t        if has_value == (has_low or has_high) or has_low != has_high:\n   393\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure has value, or low and high, never both and never one end alone'))\n   394\t        elif has_low and has_high and f['low'] > f['high']:\n   395\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: low is above high'))\n   396\t        kind = f['kind']\n   397\t        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\n   398\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n   399\t        if kind != 'computed' and (f.get('inputs') or f.get('operation')):\n   400\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs and operation are for computed figures only'))\n   401\t        if kind != 'estimate' and f.get('rests_on'):\n   402\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n   403\t        if kind == 'source':\n   404\t            if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   405\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source cannot have basis forecast'))\n   437\t        return hit\n   438\t    if any(cyclic(fid) for fid in figs):\n   439\t        errors.append(('S7', 'a figure depends on itself (through inputs or rests_on)'))\n   440\t    members = family['members']\n   441\t    seen = {}\n   442\t    for a in calc['answers']:\n   443\t        seen[a.get('view_id')] = seen.get(a.get('view_id'), 0) + 1\n   444\t    for v in members:\n   445\t        if seen.get(v, 0) != 1:\n   446\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} has {seen.get(v, 0)} answers; exactly one is required'))\n   447\t    for v in seen:\n   448\t        if v not in members:\n   449\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} is not a member of the family'))\n   450\t    return errors\n   451\t\n   452\t\n   453\tdef _sources(passages, figs):\n   454\t    errors = []\n   455\t    for f in figs.values():\n   456\t        if f['kind'] != 'source':\n   457\t            continue\n   458\t        fid, qn, ev = f['id'], f['quote_number'], f['evidence']\n   459\t        if ev not in passages:\n   460\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: evidence_id {ev} is not a shown passage'))\n   461\t            continue\n   462\t        text = passages[ev]['text']\n   463\t        spans = expressions(text)\n   464\t        starts = [(s, e) for s, e in spans if text[s:e] == qn]\n   465\t        if not starts:\n   466\t            inside = [text[s:e] for s, e in spans if qn in text[s:e]]\n   467\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\n   468\t                           f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not written in the quote of {ev} as a complete number'))\n   469\t            continue\n   470\t        parsed = parse_number(qn)\n   471\t        if parsed is None:\n   472\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not a US-dollar amount, a percentage or double, triple or twice, as written'))\n   473\t            continue\n   474\t        if f['unit'] != parsed['unit']:\n   475\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   476\t            continue\n   477\t        if 'low' in parsed:\n   478\t            ok = (_same(f.get('low'), parsed['low']) and _same(f.get('high'), parsed['high'])) or \\\n   479\t                 ('mid' in parsed and _same(f.get('value'), parsed['mid']))\n   480\t        else:\n   481\t            ok = _same(f.get('value'), parsed['value'])\n   482\t        if not ok:\n   483\t            want = (f'low {parsed[\"low\"]:g} and high {parsed[\"high\"]:g}' + (f', or value {parsed[\"mid\"]:g}' if 'mid' in parsed else '')\n   484\t                    if 'low' in parsed else f'value {parsed[\"value\"]:g}')\n   485\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\n   486\t            continue\n   487\t        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\n   488\t        written = []\n   489\t        for start, end in starts:\n   490\t            before, after = text[:start], text[end:]\n   491\t            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\n   492\t            if found is None:\n   493\t                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\n   494\t            written.append(found or 'none')\n   495\t        if q not in written:\n   496\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n   497\t    return errors\n   498\t\n   499\t\n   500\tdef _arithmetic(figs, calc):\n   501\t    errors = []\n   502\t    for f in figs.values():\n   503\t        if f['kind'] != 'computed':\n   504\t            continue\n   505\t        fid, op, ins = f['id'], f['operation'], f['inputs']\n    75\t    return obj({'figures': {'type': 'array', 'items': figure}, 'answers': {'type': 'array', 'items': answer}})\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\tQUALIFIERS = list(calc_small.QUALIFIERS)                 # one owner: the checker's closed list\n    79\t\n    80\t\n    81\tdef v13s_schema(member_ids, evidence_ids, theme_ids):\n    82\t    \"\"\"The small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §3 [52], D737, D738): each rule the checks enforce stated beside its field.\"\"\"\n    83\t    members = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids}}\n    84\t    what = obj({'population': S,\n    85\t                'measure': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.'},\n    86\t                'scope': S,\n    87\t                'period': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.'}},\n    88\t               'What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.')\n    89\t    figure = obj({\n    90\t        'id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.'},\n    91\t        'what': what,\n    92\t        'kind': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['source', 'computed', 'estimate'], 'description': \"source: a number written in a shown passage's \"\n    93\t                 'quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.'},\n    94\t        'basis': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'], 'description': \"reported: an \"\n    95\t                  \"actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this \"\n    96\t                  \"family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"},\n    97\t        'value': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.'}),\n    98\t        'low': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n    99\t        'high': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n   100\t        'unit': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['USD bn', '%', 'x'], 'description': 'USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = '\n   101\t                 '1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).'},\n   102\t        'evidence_id': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids, 'description': 'Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.'}),\n   103\t        'quote_number': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a \"\n   104\t                                  \"piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent \"\n   105\t                                  \"sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', \"\n   106\t                                  \"'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written \"\n   107\t                                  \"range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change \"\n   108\t                                  \"written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, \"\n   109\t                                  \"plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"}),\n   110\t        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\n   111\t                               \"the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"}),\n   112\t        'inputs': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"}),\n   113\t        'operation': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': ['sum', 'difference', 'product', 'ratio', 'share', 'growth', 'apply_growth'],\n   114\t                               'description': 'Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); '\n   115\t                               'product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units '\n   116\t                               'give x; a ÷ x keeps a\\'s unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; '\n   117\t                               'growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and '\n   118\t                               'product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range '\n   119\t                               'input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the '\n   120\t                               'midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds '\n   121\t                               \"a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the \"\n   122\t                               'part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds '\n   123\t                               'one quantity for the same period twice. When '\n   124\t                               'a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 '\n   125\t                               \"Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"}),\n   126\t        'basis_text': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Estimate only: the assumption, in words.'}),\n   127\t        'rests_on': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': 'Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.'}),\n   128\t        'outcome_for': dict(members, description='The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure '\n   129\t                            'with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, '\n   130\t                            \"none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"),\n   131\t        'reference_for': dict(members, description=\"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath \"\n   132\t                              'it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted '\n   133\t                              \"consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period \"\n   134\t                              \"(a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"),\n   135\t    })\n   136\t    push = obj({'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   137\t    theme_push = obj({'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': theme_ids or ['none']},\n   138\t                      'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   139\t    answer = obj({\n   140\t        'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids},\n   141\t        'shape': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['forecast', 'unresolved'], 'description': 'forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and '\n   142\t                  'a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable '\n   143\t                  'null; no figure marked for it.'},\n   144\t        'rung': nullable({'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it '\n   145\t                          '(a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.'}),\n   146\t        'reference_unavailable': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.'}),\n   147\t        'reasons': S,\n   148\t        'why': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.'}),\n   149\t        'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}},\n   150\t        'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': push}, 'theme_pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': theme_push},\n   151\t    })\n   152\t    return obj({'figures': {'type': 'array', 'items': figure, 'description': 'The family\\'s calculation. Use one forecast figure for each '\n   153\t                            'quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a '\n   154\t                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why '\n   155\t                            'it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared '\n   156\t                            'quantity\\'s guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. '\n   157\t                            'To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the '\n   158\t                            'deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. '\n   159\t                            'Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.'},\n   160\t                'answers': {'type': 'array', 'items': answer}})\n     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     8\t\n     9\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    10\tnot a defect):\n    11\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    12\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    13\t  format rules permit.\n    14\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    15\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    16\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    17\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    18\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    19\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    20\t  quarter.\n    21\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    22\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    23\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    24\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    25\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    26\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    27\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    28\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    32\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    33\t    found = {}\n    34\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    35\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    36\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    37\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    38\t            if key != 'description':\n    39\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    40\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    41\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    42\t        for value in schema:\n    43\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    44\t    return found\n    45\t\n    46\t\n    47\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    48\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    49\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    50\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    51\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    52\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    53\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)])\n     1\t\"\"\"The D744 second blinded re-read of a v13s blind run (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D744]): the same first replies, read with the same rule text\n     2\tadjudicators get (blind_rules: system text, every schema description, the definitions), plus five controls built from fresh copies:\n     3\tK-midpoint and K-misread (P's Claude reply, its \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance as the midpoint 108 or as 108.5), K-overlap and K-disjoint\n     4\t(E's Claude reply with a growth range giving $36-43B, rung 3 with its reason or rung 4, text made consistent), and K-unsupported (E's\n     5\tClaude reply with a 140% growth estimate its own reasons argue against). Each control is checked by the code before it is used.\n     6\t\n     7\tUsage: python3 blind_reread2.py <blind run folder>\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport copy\n    10\timport hashlib\n    11\timport json\n    12\timport random\n    13\timport sys\n    14\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    15\t\n    16\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    17\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    19\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\n    23\t\n    24\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n    25\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n    26\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n    27\t\n    28\tJudge in two steps:\n    29\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n    30\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    31\t2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.\n    32\t\n    33\tReturn JSON:\n    34\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    35\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    36\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    37\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    38\t\n    39\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    40\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    41\t\"\"\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    45\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef _replace(text, old, new):\n    49\t    if text.count(old) != 1:\n    50\t        raise SystemExit(f'control text: \"{old[:60]}\" occurs {text.count(old)} times')\n    51\t    return text.replace(old, new)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef controls(out):\n    55\t    made = {}\n    56\t    p = json.loads((out / 'P' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    57\t    guide = next(f for f in p['figures'] if f.get('quote_number') == '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%')\n    58\t    for name, value in (('K-midpoint', 108.0), ('K-misread', 108.5)):\n    59\t        a = copy.deepcopy(p)\n    60\t        _fig(a, guide['id']).update(value=value, low=None, high=None)\n    61\t        made[name] = ('P', a, guide['id'])\n    62\t    e = json.loads((out / 'E' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    63\t    old_reason = 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line.'\n    64\t    for name, rung, reason in (\n    65\t            ('K-overlap', 3, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8). Its midpoint, '\n    66\t             'near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3 (Neutral), so I choose rung 3; the top of the range reaches rung 2.'),\n    67\t            ('K-disjoint', 4, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8): rung 4.')):\n    68\t        a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    69\t        f7, f8 = _fig(a, 'F7'), _fig(a, 'F8')\n    70\t        f7.update(value=None, low=80.0, high=115.0, basis_text=_replace(f7['basis_text'], 'gives 85%.', 'gives 80% to 115%.'))\n    71\t        f8.update(value=None, low=36.0, high=43.0)\n    72\t        a['answers'][0].update(rung=rung, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason, reason))\n    73\t        made[name] = ('E', a, 'F8')\n    74\t    a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    75\t    _fig(a, 'F7').update(value=140.0, basis_text='My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth: 140%, because AI demand is strong.',\n    76\t                         rests_on=['F2'])\n    77\t    _fig(a, 'F8').update(value=48.0)\n    78\t    a['answers'][0].update(rung=2, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason,\n    79\t                                                    'I forecast 140% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $48 billion (F8): rung 2 (Bullish).'))\n    80\t    made['K-unsupported'] = ('E', a, 'F7')\n    81\t    expected = {'K-midpoint': [], 'K-misread': ['S1'], 'K-overlap': [], 'K-disjoint': ['S6'], 'K-unsupported': []}\n    82\t    for name, (case, answer, _) in made.items():\n    83\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n    84\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n    85\t        if codes != expected[name]:\n    86\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the code gives {codes}, expected {expected[name]}; the control is not what it claims')\n    87\t    return made\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\tdef build(out):\n    91\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n    92\t    items = [{'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'], 'answer': f'{r[\"case\"]}/answers/{r[\"writer\"]}.json'} for r in rows]\n    93\t    folder = out / 'reread2-controls'\n    94\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n    95\t    for name, (case, answer, figure) in controls(out).items():\n    96\t        (folder / f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(answer, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        items.append({'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': f'reread2-controls/{name}.json', 'figure': figure})\n    98\t    seed = hashlib.sha256(b'D744 second re-read ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    99\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   100\t    random.Random(seed).shuffle(order)\n   101\t    packets = out / 'reread2-packets'\n   102\t    packets.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   103\t    key = {}\n   104\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   105\t        item = items[i]\n   106\t        it = json.loads((out / item['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   107\t        pid = f'T{n:02d}'\n   108\t        key[pid] = item\n   109\t        text = '\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(out / item['case']), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '', it['user'], '',\n   110\t                          '# The answer', '', (out / item['answer']).read_text(), ''])\n   111\t        (packets / f'{pid}.md').write_text(text)\n   112\t    (out / 'reread2-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   113\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {packets}; key in reread2-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   117\t    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\n   118\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n   119\t    build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve())\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && nl -ba S/user.txt && rg -n '\\\"F11\\\"|\\\"F14\\\"|95%|90%|750 billion|reference|share' S/answers/*.json reread2-packets/T10.md reread2-adjudication/T10.md && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.codex.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && nl -ba S/user.txt && rg -n '\\\"F11\\\"|\\\"F14\\\"|95%|90%|750 billion|reference|share' S/answers/*.json reread2-packets/T10.md reread2-adjudication/T10.md && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.codex.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    19\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n    20\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    49\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    55\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n    56\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    58\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    60\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    65\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n    66\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n    67\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n    68\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    69\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n    70\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n    79\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n    80\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.reread2-adjudication/T10.md:21:- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:22:  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:35:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F11']): e1416 says “an estimated $750 billion.” F11 gives the correct amount but omits the qualifier.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:36:- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: Build of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure from the shown guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2), Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3), plus a stated estimate for Microsoft of $175bn (F6), set between a run-rate build from its January-March 2026 quarter of $30.88bn up 84% (e922; 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn, checked) and the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 $745bn, e2458 $760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn); combined $715-745bn (F7, sum checked). The stated 95-100% data-center share (F13) rests on outside sources labelling the same totals as data-center spending (NVIDIA's top-five nearly $800bn is called capex in e2759 and data-center capex in e4766/e5376; e4082 around $750bn of AI data-center spending); share gives $679.25-745bn (F14, checked), midpoint about $712bn, rung 4 chosen as a band the range overlaps with the reason given. Reference e5014 ($720-745bn, four companies) also sits in rung 4. A reader can check every step; the rung-4 call leans on two stated assumptions (Microsoft at $175bn rather than the ~$155bn run-rate, and a near-total data-center share, against e5014's weaker 'large portion'), and the basis text's '$175-190bn at their centres' understates the top of the residual range (the $760bn tally gives about $205bn), which does not weaken the chosen value.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:43:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:46:- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:47:- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:53: \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:55: \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:68: \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:72: \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:73: \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:75: \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:96:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:97:    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:126:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:127:    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:235:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:254:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:273:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:292:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:311:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:330:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:349:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:368:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:387:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:406:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:409:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:418:      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:425:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:444:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:448:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:460:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:461:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:463:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:466:      \"id\": \"F14\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:478:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:482:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:490:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:491:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:16:               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\nreread2-packets/T10.md:30:- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\nreread2-packets/T10.md:31:  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\nreread2-packets/T10.md:44:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:47:- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:48:- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:54: \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:56: \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:69: \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:73: \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:74: \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:76: \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:98:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:99:    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:128:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:129:    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\nreread2-packets/T10.md:238:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:257:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:276:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:295:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:314:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:333:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:352:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:371:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\nreread2-packets/T10.md:390:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:409:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:412:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:421:      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:428:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:447:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:451:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nreread2-packets/T10.md:463:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:464:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\nreread2-packets/T10.md:466:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:469:      \"id\": \"F14\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:481:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:485:      \"reference_for\": []\nreread2-packets/T10.md:493:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\nreread2-packets/T10.md:494:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\nS/answers/codex.json:20:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:39:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:58:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:77:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:96:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:115:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:134:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:153:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:172:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:191:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:194:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nS/answers/codex.json:210:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:214:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of total capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nS/answers/codex.json:226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split.\",\nS/answers/codex.json:229:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:243:      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F12\"],\nS/answers/codex.json:244:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nS/answers/codex.json:248:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:256:      \"reference_unavailable\": \"The packet gives four-company estimates for all capital expenditure and for broadly defined AI infrastructure. NVIDIA's data-center figure includes a fifth company. The sources provide no quoted allocation that isolates data-center capex for the four companies.\",\nS/answers/codex.json:257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\",\nS/answers/claude.json:20:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:39:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:58:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:77:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:96:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:115:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:134:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:153:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\nS/answers/claude.json:172:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:191:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:194:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nS/answers/claude.json:203:      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\nS/answers/claude.json:210:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:229:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:233:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nS/answers/claude.json:245:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\nS/answers/claude.json:246:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\nS/answers/claude.json:248:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:251:      \"id\": \"F14\",\nS/answers/claude.json:263:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nS/answers/claude.json:267:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:275:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\nS/answers/claude.json:276:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The $679.25–745 billion range is calculated correctly and overlaps rung 4, but it depends on an unsupported assumption that at least 95% of the four companies’ capital spending goes to data centers. e5014 says only that 'a large portion' goes toward AI infrastructure. e1947 gives a figure for AI infrastructure without isolating data centers, and e5376 covers five companies. F8 measures total capital spending, so it does not establish a market reference for the data-center portion.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F11\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e1416 says 'an estimated $750 billion.' F11 copies the dollar amount correctly but records the qualifier as 'none.' The format requires 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number.'\"\n    15\t    }\n    16\t  ]\n    17\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every step can be checked against the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. The build uses the quoted guidance: Alphabet '$195 billion to $205 billion' (e1254), Meta '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549) and Amazon '$220 billion' (e1649). Microsoft's $175bn (F6) is a stated estimate placed between two checkable anchors. The first is a run-rate from '$30.88 billion, up 84%' (e922): at 15% a quarter, 30.88 + 35.5 + 40.8 + 47.0 is about $154bn. The second is the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 '$720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 '$745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion') after the other three companies' $540-570bn, which gives roughly $150-205bn and about $178-205bn at the tallies' centres. The basis text's '$175-190bn' understates the top of that span, but this does not move the chosen value outside it. F7's sum checks: 195+125+220+175 = 715 and 205+145+220+175 = 745. The 95-100% data-center share (F13) is written as its own estimate figure, as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), and the passages back it. NVIDIA's top-five figure is called 'CapEx ... nearly $800 billion' in e2759 and 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures' in e4766 and e5376: the same total under both labels. e4082 also puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year', roughly the size of the four companies' total capital expenditure. Reader 1 is right that no passage proves other spending is at most 5%, but 'supported' asks that the forecast follow from the passages plus the stated assumptions, not that a passage prove the assumption. F14's share checks: 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 and 745 x 1.00 = 745. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and rung 4 is chosen as a band the range overlaps, with the reason given, which the format permits. Reader 1's point that F8 measures total capital expenditure rather than the data-center part concerns the reference, not whether the forecast is supported, and neither reader marked it as a defect.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F11\"], \"decision\": \"uphold\", \"why\": \"e1416's quote reads 'Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The format rule asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number', and the words just before the number are 'an estimated'. F11 records qualifier 'none', so its qualifier differs from what the quote writes. The number and scale (750, USD bn) are correct. F11 appears only in F13's rests_on and enters no calculation, so the defect does not change the outcome or the rung.\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_rules.py && nl -ba blind_reread2.py && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '1,220p' && nl -ba judging-v13s.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_rules.py && nl -ba blind_reread2.py && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '1,220p' && nl -ba judging-v13s.txt\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     8\t\n     9\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    10\tnot a defect):\n    11\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    12\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    13\t  format rules permit.\n    14\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    15\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    16\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    17\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    18\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    19\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    20\t  quarter.\n    21\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    22\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    23\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    24\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    25\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    26\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    27\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    28\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    32\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    33\t    found = {}\n    34\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    35\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    36\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    37\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    38\t            if key != 'description':\n    39\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    40\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    41\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    42\t        for value in schema:\n    43\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    44\t    return found\n    45\t\n    46\t\n    47\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    48\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    49\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    50\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    51\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    52\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    53\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)])\n     1\t\"\"\"The D744 second blinded re-read of a v13s blind run (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D744]): the same first replies, read with the same rule text\n     2\tadjudicators get (blind_rules: system text, every schema description, the definitions), plus five controls built from fresh copies:\n     3\tK-midpoint and K-misread (P's Claude reply, its \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance as the midpoint 108 or as 108.5), K-overlap and K-disjoint\n     4\t(E's Claude reply with a growth range giving $36-43B, rung 3 with its reason or rung 4, text made consistent), and K-unsupported (E's\n     5\tClaude reply with a 140% growth estimate its own reasons argue against). Each control is checked by the code before it is used.\n     6\t\n     7\tUsage: python3 blind_reread2.py <blind run folder>\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport copy\n    10\timport hashlib\n    11\timport json\n    12\timport random\n    13\timport sys\n    14\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    15\t\n    16\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    17\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    19\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\n    23\t\n    24\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n    25\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n    26\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n    27\t\n    28\tJudge in two steps:\n    29\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n    30\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    31\t2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.\n    32\t\n    33\tReturn JSON:\n    34\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    35\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    36\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    37\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    38\t\n    39\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    40\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    41\t\"\"\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    45\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef _replace(text, old, new):\n    49\t    if text.count(old) != 1:\n    50\t        raise SystemExit(f'control text: \"{old[:60]}\" occurs {text.count(old)} times')\n    51\t    return text.replace(old, new)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef controls(out):\n    55\t    made = {}\n    56\t    p = json.loads((out / 'P' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    57\t    guide = next(f for f in p['figures'] if f.get('quote_number') == '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%')\n    58\t    for name, value in (('K-midpoint', 108.0), ('K-misread', 108.5)):\n    59\t        a = copy.deepcopy(p)\n    60\t        _fig(a, guide['id']).update(value=value, low=None, high=None)\n    61\t        made[name] = ('P', a, guide['id'])\n    62\t    e = json.loads((out / 'E' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    63\t    old_reason = 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line.'\n    64\t    for name, rung, reason in (\n    65\t            ('K-overlap', 3, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8). Its midpoint, '\n    66\t             'near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3 (Neutral), so I choose rung 3; the top of the range reaches rung 2.'),\n    67\t            ('K-disjoint', 4, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8): rung 4.')):\n    68\t        a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    69\t        f7, f8 = _fig(a, 'F7'), _fig(a, 'F8')\n    70\t        f7.update(value=None, low=80.0, high=115.0, basis_text=_replace(f7['basis_text'], 'gives 85%.', 'gives 80% to 115%.'))\n    71\t        f8.update(value=None, low=36.0, high=43.0)\n    72\t        a['answers'][0].update(rung=rung, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason, reason))\n    73\t        made[name] = ('E', a, 'F8')\n    74\t    a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    75\t    _fig(a, 'F7').update(value=140.0, basis_text='My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth: 140%, because AI demand is strong.',\n    76\t                         rests_on=['F2'])\n    77\t    _fig(a, 'F8').update(value=48.0)\n    78\t    a['answers'][0].update(rung=2, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason,\n    79\t                                                    'I forecast 140% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $48 billion (F8): rung 2 (Bullish).'))\n    80\t    made['K-unsupported'] = ('E', a, 'F7')\n    81\t    expected = {'K-midpoint': [], 'K-misread': ['S1'], 'K-overlap': [], 'K-disjoint': ['S6'], 'K-unsupported': []}\n    82\t    for name, (case, answer, _) in made.items():\n    83\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n    84\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n    85\t        if codes != expected[name]:\n    86\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the code gives {codes}, expected {expected[name]}; the control is not what it claims')\n    87\t    return made\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\tdef build(out):\n    91\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n    92\t    items = [{'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'], 'answer': f'{r[\"case\"]}/answers/{r[\"writer\"]}.json'} for r in rows]\n    93\t    folder = out / 'reread2-controls'\n    94\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n    95\t    for name, (case, answer, figure) in controls(out).items():\n    96\t        (folder / f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(answer, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        items.append({'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': f'reread2-controls/{name}.json', 'figure': figure})\n    98\t    seed = hashlib.sha256(b'D744 second re-read ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    99\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   100\t    random.Random(seed).shuffle(order)\n   101\t    packets = out / 'reread2-packets'\n   102\t    packets.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   103\t    key = {}\n   104\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   105\t        item = items[i]\n   106\t        it = json.loads((out / item['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   107\t        pid = f'T{n:02d}'\n   108\t        key[pid] = item\n   109\t        text = '\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(out / item['case']), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '', it['user'], '',\n   110\t                          '# The answer', '', (out / item['answer']).read_text(), ''])\n   111\t        (packets / f'{pid}.md').write_text(text)\n   112\t    (out / 'reread2-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   113\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {packets}; key in reread2-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   117\t    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\n   118\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n   119\t    build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve())\n     1\t\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n     2\t\n     3\tAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\n     4\tthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\n     5\tand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n     6\t\n     7\tRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\n     8\tanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\n     9\tclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n    10\t\n    11\tUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n    12\t       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n    52\t        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    53\t    return lines\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\tdef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    57\t    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    58\t    out = folder / out_name\n    59\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    60\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    61\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    62\t        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    63\t        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    64\t        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n    65\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    66\t                          format_material(folder / case), '',\n    67\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    68\t                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n    69\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    70\t        if target.exists():\n    71\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    72\t        target.write_text(text)\n    73\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    74\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    75\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\tREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n    79\t\n    80\tA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\n    81\talone:\n    82\t1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n    83\t   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n    84\t2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n    85\t   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\n    86\tReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n    87\t\"\"\"\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\tdef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    91\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    92\t    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    93\t    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n    94\t        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    95\t    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    96\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    97\t    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    98\t                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    99\t                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n   100\t                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n   101\t                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n   102\t    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n   103\t    if target.exists():\n   104\t        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n   105\t    target.write_text(text)\n   106\t    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n   107\t                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n   108\t    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   112\t    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n   113\t        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   114\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n   115\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   116\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread2':       # D744: the second re-read under the shared rule text\n   117\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread2-key.json', 'reread2-packets', 'reread2-adjudication')\n   118\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 3 and sys.argv[1] != 'refusal':\n   119\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n   120\t    else:\n   121\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && rg -n '\"'^#|e1947|e1416|e2759|e5376|e2880|e1732|e4353|e2971|Meta|Google|Amazon|Microsoft'\"' S/user.txt | cut -c 1-310 && rg -n '\\\"F11\\\"|\\\"F14\\\"|95%|90%|750 billion|reference|share|679.25' S/answers/*.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.codex.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && rg -n '\"'^#|e1947|e1416|e2759|e5376|e2880|e1732|e4353|e2971|Meta|Google|Amazon|Microsoft'\"' S/user.txt | cut -c 1-310 && rg -n '\\\"F11\\\"|\\\"F14\\\"|95%|90%|750 billion|reference|share|679.25' S/answers/*.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.codex.json && nl -ba reread2-adjudication/T10.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n12:- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n13:    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n17:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n21:- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n22:    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n24:- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n25:    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar \n30:    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n32:- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, \n33:    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n34:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n39:- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n45:- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n46:    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n50:- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n51:    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n52:- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n53:    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n54:- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n55:    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n57:- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments no\n58:    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n66:- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n67:    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to A\n84:    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n86:- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n87:    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n103:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n110:- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n115:- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infras\n116:    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or sugg\n117:- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connection\n118:    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in re\n128:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\nS/answers/codex.json:20:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:39:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:58:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:77:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:96:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:115:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:134:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:153:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:172:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:191:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:194:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nS/answers/codex.json:210:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:214:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of total capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nS/answers/codex.json:226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split.\",\nS/answers/codex.json:229:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:243:      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F12\"],\nS/answers/codex.json:244:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nS/answers/codex.json:248:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/codex.json:256:      \"reference_unavailable\": \"The packet gives four-company estimates for all capital expenditure and for broadly defined AI infrastructure. NVIDIA's data-center figure includes a fifth company. The sources provide no quoted allocation that isolates data-center capex for the four companies.\",\nS/answers/codex.json:257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\",\nS/answers/claude.json:20:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:39:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:58:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:77:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:96:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:115:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:134:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:153:      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\nS/answers/claude.json:172:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:191:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:194:      \"id\": \"F11\",\nS/answers/claude.json:203:      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\nS/answers/claude.json:210:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:229:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:233:      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\nS/answers/claude.json:245:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\nS/answers/claude.json:246:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\nS/answers/claude.json:248:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:251:      \"id\": \"F14\",\nS/answers/claude.json:256:      \"low\": 679.25,\nS/answers/claude.json:263:      \"operation\": \"share\",\nS/answers/claude.json:267:      \"reference_for\": []\nS/answers/claude.json:275:      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\nS/answers/claude.json:276:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The $679.25–745 billion range is calculated correctly and overlaps rung 4, but it depends on an unsupported assumption that at least 95% of the four companies’ capital spending goes to data centers. e5014 says only that 'a large portion' goes toward AI infrastructure. e1947 gives a figure for AI infrastructure without isolating data centers, and e5376 covers five companies. F8 measures total capital spending, so it does not establish a market reference for the data-center portion.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F11\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e1416 says 'an estimated $750 billion.' F11 copies the dollar amount correctly but records the qualifier as 'none.' The format requires 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number.'\"\n    15\t    }\n    16\t  ]\n    17\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every step can be checked against the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. The build uses the quoted guidance: Alphabet '$195 billion to $205 billion' (e1254), Meta '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549) and Amazon '$220 billion' (e1649). Microsoft's $175bn (F6) is a stated estimate placed between two checkable anchors. The first is a run-rate from '$30.88 billion, up 84%' (e922): at 15% a quarter, 30.88 + 35.5 + 40.8 + 47.0 is about $154bn. The second is the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 '$720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 '$745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion') after the other three companies' $540-570bn, which gives roughly $150-205bn and about $178-205bn at the tallies' centres. The basis text's '$175-190bn' understates the top of that span, but this does not move the chosen value outside it. F7's sum checks: 195+125+220+175 = 715 and 205+145+220+175 = 745. The 95-100% data-center share (F13) is written as its own estimate figure, as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), and the passages back it. NVIDIA's top-five figure is called 'CapEx ... nearly $800 billion' in e2759 and 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures' in e4766 and e5376: the same total under both labels. e4082 also puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year', roughly the size of the four companies' total capital expenditure. Reader 1 is right that no passage proves other spending is at most 5%, but 'supported' asks that the forecast follow from the passages plus the stated assumptions, not that a passage prove the assumption. F14's share checks: 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 and 745 x 1.00 = 745. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and rung 4 is chosen as a band the range overlaps, with the reason given, which the format permits. Reader 1's point that F8 measures total capital expenditure rather than the data-center part concerns the reference, not whether the forecast is supported, and neither reader marked it as a defect.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F11\"], \"decision\": \"uphold\", \"why\": \"e1416's quote reads 'Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The format rule asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number', and the words just before the number are 'an estimated'. F11 records qualifier 'none', so its qualifier differs from what the quote writes. The number and scale (750, USD bn) are correct. F11 appears only in F13's rests_on and enters no calculation, so the defect does not change the outcome or the rung.\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\nkeys=json.load(open(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\"))\nfor i,v in keys.items():\n print(i,v[\\\"case\\\"],v[\\\"writer\\\"])\n for sub in (\\\"reread2-packets\\\",\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\"):\n  for reader in (\\\"codex\\\",\\\"claude\\\"):\n   f=pathlib.Path(f\\\"{sub}/{i}.{reader}.json\\\")\n   if f.exists():print(sub,reader,json.load(f.open()))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\nkeys=json.load(open(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\"))\nfor i,v in keys.items():\n print(i,v[\\\"case\\\"],v[\\\"writer\\\"])\n for sub in (\\\"reread2-packets\\\",\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\"):\n  for reader in (\\\"codex\\\",\\\"claude\\\"):\n   f=pathlib.Path(f\\\"{sub}/{i}.{reader}.json\\\")\n   if f.exists():print(sub,reader,json.load(f.open()))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"T01 P K-midpoint\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue gives $177.82 billion for H1 (e111, e2649). Q3 guidance and the history of guidance beats support a $112.5 billion Q3 estimate after allowing for supply constraints (e2652, e5054, e5055, e3200, e3115, e4911). Expected hyperscale acceleration as Vera Rubin supply expands supports using the 12% Q3 guidance pace as a benchmark for Q4 growth despite continuing supply constraints (e4899, e3406, e4283, e2759, e5067). That gives $416.32 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.'}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue gives $164.25 billion for H1 (e109, e2650). The segment's rising share through Q2, management's expectation that Data Center will drive most Q3 growth, and slower Edge Computing growth support a modest increase from 92.5% in Q2 to an assumed 93% in H2 (e3399, e5223, e4283, e5059). Applying that share to the same $238.5 billion company H2 forecast gives $386.06 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported H1 of $177.82bn (F1 e111 $81.62bn + F2 e2649 $96.2bn; matches e4992's $177.8bn) plus the stated assumptions F9 (Q3 $112.5bn: above the $108.0bn +/-2% guide, e2652, on the 12-quarter record of beating the high end, e5054, by 3.1% on average, e5055, and the Q2 $5.2bn beat, e3200, trimmed for the supply caps in e3115/e4911) and F11 (Q4 +12% sequential, benchmarked on the guided Q3 pace F10/e4899, with Q4 hyperscale reacceleration e3406/e4283 and supply constraint e5067). FY2027 = $416.32bn, inside rung 2 ($392bn to $431.2bn). The band is robust: consensus $411bn (e4333), the Zacks growth rate on FY2026 (~$405bn), the analysts' quarterly path (e5161/e5162, ~$398bn) and the guidance-midpoint/5% sensitivity (~$399bn) all sit in rung 2. Minor prose slips that do not touch any figure or the band: the rung-1 example 'Q4 near $138bn (+20%)' would need about $141bn (+25%) on the writer's own $112.5bn Q3, and e5161/e5162 are dated after the Q3 guide, so calling them earlier than it is not supported.\", 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported H1 Data Center $164.25bn (F4 e109 $75.25bn + F5 e2650 $89.0bn; e5309 $164bn) plus the stated assumption F16 (H2 Data Center share 93.0%, a small step above Q2's 92.5%, e3399, extending a share that has risen every quarter with narrowing gains, with Q3 growth led by the data center business, e5223/e4283, and the non-Data Center remainder, Edge Computing $7.2bn, growing a slower 13%, e5059), applied to the family's H2 total F13 ($238.5bn). FY2027 = $386.06bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to $400bn), below the total's $416.32bn. The band holds under the guidance-midpoint/5%/92.5% sensitivity (~$369bn) and matches the outside reference (Morningstar $361bn, e2295, rung 2).\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT02 O claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"The $9–20 billion forecast assumes that e2889's $18 billion will be funded by fiscal year-end entirely from e2620's $25 billion July 26 balance, leaving $7 billion. It adds an estimated $2–13 billion in new commitments still unfunded at year-end, based mainly on e1970, e1919, and e4648.\", 'why_not': 'e2889 says $18 billion was committed for the rest of the fiscal year. It does not establish that the full amount was included in the July 26 balance or will be paid by year-end. The deal passages describe possible or conditional investments without establishing when they would be signed or funded. They do not support the $13 billion upper estimate for new unpaid commitments. e2643 concerns whether large AI-lab investments will repeat next year and does not bound commitments made before this fiscal year ends.'}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"e2620 (NVIDIA reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026, F1) less e2889 (NVIDIA's same-day statement that $18 billion is committed for the rest of the fiscal year, F2, read as the part of the July book funded by year end) gives about $7 billion still owed from the July book (F3); plus the stated estimate F4 of $2-13 billion of post-July commitments still unfunded at year end, built from the staged or unclosed amounts in e1970 (SB Energy second $1.5 billion at IPO), e1919 (Lancium milestone $1 billion), e4648 (Anthropic IPO anchor up to $10 billion) and e3934 (Thinking Machines about $2.5 billion), on the stated assumption that Poolside (e2207), MediaTek (e3474) and SB Energy's first tranche (e1957) fund at closing, with e2643 and e4487 arguing against further large staged commitments. F5 = $9-20 billion, wholly below the $25 billion rung 1 boundary.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-adjudication codex {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'no', 'why': \"e2620 reports '$25 billion as of July 26, 2026,' while e2889 says NVIDIA has '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' The latter does not say how much of the $18 billion came from the July balance or when it is expected to be paid. Subtracting all $18 billion therefore does not support a $7 billion year-end remainder. The named deals explain how the estimate reaches about $13 billion in new unpaid commitments under one scenario, but the passages give little basis for expecting those deals to be signed by year-end and still unpaid then. In particular, e4648 says NVIDIA 'could invest as much as $10 billion' in Anthropic, with no signing or funding date. The $9–20 billion forecast rests on timing and overlap assumptions the passages do not support.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-adjudication claude {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'yes', 'why': \"The forecast can be checked step by step against the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. F1 = $25 billion is e2620's 'equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026'. F2 = $18 billion is e2889, NVIDIA's same-day statement that it 'has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'. That quote ties the $18 billion to payment in the rest of the fiscal year, and $18 billion is less than the $25 billion book reported the same day. Reading it as the part of the July book due by year end is a plain reading of the quote, and the reasons state it ('NVIDIA said $18 billion (F2) of its commitments is to be funded in the rest of FY2027'). So F3 = 25 - 18 = $7 billion follows. F4 ($2-13 billion) is labelled as the writer's own estimate and its assumption is written out. Deals that fund at closing are left out: Poolside $1 billion (e2207), MediaTek $3.5 billion (e3474) and SB Energy's first $1.5 billion (e1957). Staged or unclosed amounts are counted: SB Energy's second '$1.5 billion tranche ... expected to land' at the IPO (e1970), Lancium's 'additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go[als]' (e1919), and Anthropic's 'could invest as much as $10 billion' (e4648). Those amounts are checkable: 1.5 + 1 + 10 = 12.5, rounded up to 13. The low end, Lancium's $1 billion plus about $1 billion of other staged money, is also stated. Huang's 'those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat' (e2643) and 'was never a commitment' (e4487) are offered as reasons against a further large staged commitment, not as a hard limit. Reader 1 says the passages do not establish when these deals would be signed or funded. That is true, but the format rules make F4 an estimate ('your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text'), and the system text says 'not established is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay'. The rung is the most likely outcome, not a proven one. The sums are right (7 + 2 = 9, 7 + 13 = 20), and the whole range F5 = $9-20 billion lies inside rung 1 ('below $25 billion'). The answer also names what would move it to rung 2: a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nT03 E K-overlap\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"NVIDIA's $20 billion FY2027 base and FY2028 doubling target (e3660, e4691, e4856) anchor the estimate. Planned Vera deployments and Anthropic's evaluation (e4689, e5044), along with Arm server adoption (e4692), support growth above the broader CPU market pace (e4687, e4857). AMD's performance claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) support allowing for competitive pressure. The stated 80% to 115% growth assumption gives $36 billion to $43 billion. The $39.5 billion midpoint falls in rung 3.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"F8 = apply_growth(F1, F7) = $36-43 billion, midpoint $39.5 billion, rung 3. F1 is the $20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue guidance (e3660), tied to Vera by e4691 and e4856. F7 is the writer's own estimate of 80% to 115% growth. It takes management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e3660, e4691, e4764) and discounts it for time and execution. It pulls toward the roughly 50% market growth pace (F4/e4687; F5-F6/e4857) and AMD's competing benchmarks (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404). Against those it weighs the breadth of adoption (e4689, e5044, e4692).\", 'why_not': \"What puts the forecast in rung 3 rather than rung 2 is the size of the discount on management's outlook, and no passage supports that size. The only NVIDIA-specific number is management's repeated 'more than double', from the CFO, the CEO and several outlets. It puts FY2028 at or above $40 billion, which is rung 2. The passages cited for a discount do not measure any shortfall in Vera revenue. The 50% and fivefold growth rates cover the whole server CPU or CPU market, not a product line that is ramping. AMD's results are a competitor's performance benchmarks, not revenue, share or order evidence, and the writer itself weighs them lightly. No passage shows a delay, a supply limit, a cut or cancelled order, or a revised outlook. The system text's default discount applies to a product not yet shipping or to an ambition spanning years. The writer instead treats Vera as already ramping and takes the $20 billion FY2027 base in full, while this outlook is for the next single fiscal year. The range runs over the $40 billion edge, and the rung-3 call rests on a midpoint $0.5 billion below it. The passages cannot tell 97.5% growth from 100% or more, so the band is set by the writer's choice of range, not by the evidence.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nT04 P codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half revenue was $177.84 billion (e111, e5078). The $112 billion Q3 forecast assumes a smaller beat than the outside $113.6 billion scenario (e5056), using company guidance (e2652) and the prior quarter's beat (e3200). The assumed 17% Q4 growth follows the Vera Rubin shipment ramp and expected pickup in hyperscaler demand (e2693, e5061, e5138, e2759), tempered by supply constraints and long lead times (e5067, e5205). Even if Q4 revenue stayed flat at $112 billion, the year would finish at $401.84 billion, within rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue was $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). The forecast assumes the segment's share rises from 92.5% in Q2 (e3399) to 93% in Q3 and 93.5% in Q4. NVIDIA expects Data Center to drive Q3 growth, with hyperscaler demand picking up as Vera Rubin supply expands (e5223, e5061, e5138). Applying those shares to the same company forecasts gives $390.95 billion. Holding the share at 92.5% in both quarters would still put the year in rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'First-half actual $177.84bn (F1 e111 $81.62bn + F2 e5078 $96.22bn). Q3 is $112bn, Q2 grown by a stated 16.4% (F6). That sits between the $108.0bn guidance midpoint (e2652) and the $113.6bn guidance-beat extrapolation (e5056), and the prior-quarter beat (e3200) supports it. Q4 is $131.04bn from a stated 17% sequential pace (F8), resting on Vera Rubin shipping (e2693, e5138), hyperscale growth reaccelerating in Q4 (e5061) and the larger supply commitments (e2759). With Q3 at $112bn, rung 2 [392, 431.2) holds for any Q4 from about $102bn to $141bn. Even the guidance midpoint with a flat Q4 gives about $394bn, so the band does not hinge on the aggressive Q4 point. That point is well above the Wall Street Q4 view (65% year-on-year, e5162, about $112bn). The $411bn consensus (e5204) also sits in rung 2.', 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"First-half Data Center actual $164.27bn (e109 $75.25bn + e5079 $89.02bn). Q3 and Q4 Data Center are 93% and 93.5% of the company forecasts (F16, F17). That is a modest continuation of the share's rise each quarter from 87.9% to 92.5% (e3399), with Data Center leading growth (e5223, e5061). Rung 2 [350, 400) holds for second-half Data Center revenue of about $186bn to $236bn. Holding Q2's 92.5% share on the guidance midpoint ($99.9bn, e3401) with a flat Q4 already gives about $364bn. Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) also sits in rung 2. Each half-year Data Center figure stays below the company figure for the same period.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT05 P-v8 claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'Reported Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion for H1 (e109, e2650). Q3 company guidance and recent guidance beats support the assumed 5% beat (e2652, e2649, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055). The 12% Q4 growth estimate uses Q3 guidance as a benchmark (e5005) and weighs the Vera Rubin ramp against continued supply constraints (e3406, e3733, e5067). The rising Data Center share and its expected contribution to Q3 growth support a 92.8% H2 share (e3399, e5223). These assumptions yield $387.35 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.', 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported H1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $164.25 billion (F1 e109 $75.25bn + F2 e2650 $89.0bn; consistent with e5309 $164bn) plus an H2 built from company revenue: Q3 at the $108.0bn guidance midpoint (e2652) lifted by an assumed 5% beat (F11, resting on the computed 5.7% Q2 beat F6 from e3200/e2649, the 4.6% Q4 FY2026 beat F9 from e2452, and e5054/e5055's record of beating the high end by 3.1% on average, about 5% above the midpoint), giving $113.4bn (F12, matching e5053/e5072's $113-114bn); Q4 at the 12% sequential pace the Q3 guidance implies (e5005), justified by the guided Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (e3406, e5061, e4283) and capped by supply limits (e3733, e5067), giving $127.0bn (F14); and an H2 Data Center share of 92.8% (F16), a small step up from Q2's 92.5% on the rising trend in e3399. That yields $387.35bn, inside rung 2 ($350-400bn). The band is robust to the assumptions: at the bare Q3 midpoint with a flat Q4 and a 92.5% share the outcome is still about $364bn, and reaching $400bn needs about 20% sequential Data Center growth in both H2 quarters while supply is capped. The reference (F19, Morningstar's $361bn, e2295) is an outside figure for the exact quantity, also in rung 2. One sentence of the reasons is loosely worded: H2 Data Center revenue under $185.8bn averages about $92.9bn a quarter, which is above the Q2 pace of $89.0bn, not below it. The bound still sits far below the forecast, because the Q3 guidance alone implies about $100bn of Data Center revenue (e3401), so the wording does not affect the band.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT06 S codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': 'The answer applies an assumed 90% data-center share to $745 billion of total capex, the high end of e5014, to forecast $670.5 billion in rung 3.', 'why_not': \"e5014 says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure but gives no data-center share. The comparison of Meta's AI capex estimate in e3686 with its total capex guidance in e1549 gives no data-center split for Meta or the four companies combined. e4404 reports AWS growth without a spending breakdown. At $745 billion of total capex, a data-center share of about 94% would cross into rung 4, and the passages do not establish which side of that cutoff is more likely.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"F12, the writer's own 90% data-center share of total capital expenditure (resting on F1/e5014 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure', F5/e1549 Meta capex guidance $125-145bn, F6/e3686 Meta AI capex $130bn, and e4404), applied by share() to F11, a $745bn total-capex estimate taken from the top of the Zacks range (F1/e5014, also e2079) and below the $760bn plan (F2/e2458). 745 x 90% = 670.5, which is in rung 3.\", 'why_not': \"The rung depends entirely on the 90% share. On a $745bn total, data-center capex reaches $700bn at a share of about 94%, so the forecast only stays in rung 3 because of the gap between 90% and 94%. The passages do not support a share that low. The writer's own Meta comparison gives 130/135, about 96%, at the guidance midpoint (89.7% to 104% across the range), which is above the cutoff. NVIDIA gives the same 'nearly $800 billion' as the top five's total capex (e2759, e5081) and as their data-center capex (e5376, e4766), so in that source the data-center spend is close to the total. e1416 and e4082 put data-center spending at about $750bn, which is in rung 4. No passage gives any non-data-center spending to subtract. The 10% deduction is the writer's unsourced 'allowance for other capital spending', and e4404 (AWS growth) says nothing about the spending mix. The $745bn total is well grounded, but the assumption that puts the forecast below $700bn is not.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nT07 P-v4 codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"The reported first half totals $177.84 billion (e111, e5078). The prior guidance beat, the outside Q3 estimate, and Rubin shipments support a $112.5 billion Q3 forecast (e2652, e3200, e5056, e2693, e5118). The Q3 guidance implies about 12% sequential growth, which the writer uses as a benchmark for Q4. NVIDIA's expected pickup in hyperscaler sales supports carrying that pace forward, while supply constraints and long lead times temper the forecast (e3406, e5067, e5205). That yields $126 billion for Q4 and $416.34 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported FY2027 Q1 $81.62bn (e111, F2) and Q2 $96.22bn (e5078, F3) give H1 $177.84bn (matches e4992's $177.8bn). Q3 is the estimate F6 $112.5bn: NVIDIA's guide is $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (e2652, F4), Q2 beat its outlook midpoint by $5.2bn (e3200), and Pradhan's pattern-based $113.6bn (e5056, F5) sets the upper benchmark; channel lead times (e5205) temper the beat. Q4 is F8 = F6 x 1.12 = $126bn: the 12% sequential pace (F7) is benchmarked on the Q3 guide's implied pace over Q2 (108/96.22, about 12.2%) and on hyperscaler reacceleration in Q4 as Rubin supply builds (e3406), held back by supply constraints (e5067). The sum is $416.34bn, in rung 2 [392, 431.2). The band is robust: rung 2 holds whenever H2 is at least $214.2bn (for example Q3 at the $108bn guide and a flat Q4), and rung 1 would need H2 of at least $253.4bn (about 23% sequential Q4 growth on a $113.6bn Q3), against the stated supply limits (e5067, e3733). The quoted consensus of $411bn (e5204, F1) also sits in rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT08 P K-misread\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'Reported first-half revenue is $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). The $112.5 billion Q3 forecast assumes a smaller beat than the recent average because supply is tight (e2652, e5054, e5055, e3200, e3115, e4911). The 12% Q4 growth assumption uses Q3 guidance as a benchmark and weighs the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales against continued supply constraints (e4899, e3406, e4283, e2759, e5067). That gives $416.32 billion for the year, in rung 2.'}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue is $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The assumption that Data Center accounts for 93% of second-half revenue follows its rising share, which reached 92.5% in Q2, and management's expectation that the AI Data Center business will drive most Q3 growth (e3399, e5223, e4283, e5059). Applying that share to the $238.5 billion second-half company forecast gives about $386.06 billion for the year, in rung 2.\"}}, 'defects': [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F7'], 'detail': 'e2652 quotes $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. The allowed point is the $108.0 billion midpoint, and the allowed range is $105.84 billion to $110.16 billion. F7 records $108.5 billion.'}]}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"H1 company revenue is reported: F1 $81.62bn (e111) + F2 $96.2bn (e2649) = F3 $177.82bn, which matches e4992's $177.8bn. Q3 is the estimate F9 at $112.5bn, about 2% above the $110.16bn high end of the guidance (e2652, $108.0bn plus or minus 2%). It rests on NVIDIA beating the high end in each of the last 12 quarters by 3.1% on average (e5054, e5055; e5056 gives $113.6bn), the $5.2bn beat of the Q2 midpoint (e3200) and the $3bn beat of the Q4 FY2026 guide (e2452). It takes less than the full pattern because of the stated supply caps (e3115, e4911). Q4 is F9 grown 12% (F11), the pace guided for Q3 (e4899, used only as a benchmark), backed by the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration management describes (e3406, e4283). That gives F12 $126.0bn, F13 $238.5bn and F14 $416.32bn, inside rung 2 ($392bn to $431.2bn). Rung 3 would need Q4 to fall below Q3 guidance, and rung 1 would need H2 of about $253bn; even guidance-midpoint Q3 with 5% Q4 growth gives about $399bn, so the band holds with room on both sides. The $411bn consensus reference (F19, e4333) is in the same rung. Reading the '70% full-year growth' reports as the FY2028 outlook is supported by the transcript (e2691) and by the arithmetic: 70% on $215.9bn would need Q4 below Q1.\", 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"H1 Data Center revenue is reported: F4 $75.25bn (e109) + F5 $89.0bn (e2650) = F6 $164.25bn, which matches e5309's $164bn. H2 Data Center is the estimate F16 (93.0%) applied to the family's own H2 company forecast F13 ($238.5bn), giving F17 $221.81bn. F16 extends the share series, which rose every quarter from 87.9% to 92.5% (e3399, F15). Q3 growth is said to come mainly from the data center business (e5223, e4283), and the remainder ($7.2bn = $96.2bn - $89.0bn, Edge Computing per e5059) grew a slower 13%. F18 $386.06bn is inside rung 2 ($350bn to $400bn) and below the total's $416.32bn. Rung 1 would need $235.75bn of H2 Data Center revenue, a 98.8% share of the $238.5bn H2, or H2 company revenue near $253bn. Rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below about $186bn, under even the low end of Q3 guidance carried flat at a 92.5% share. Morningstar's $361bn (F20, e2295), a quoted outside figure, is the reference and sits in the same rung.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F7'], 'detail': \"e2652 quotes '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The format rules allow that expression as low and high ($105.84bn to $110.16bn) or as its midpoint, $108.0bn, for value. F7 gives value 108.5, which is neither the midpoint nor either end. F7 is not an input to any computed figure (it appears only in F9's rests_on), and F9's basis text uses the correct $108.0bn midpoint and $110.16bn high end, so no outcome changes.\"}]}\nT09 P-v4 claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half revenue was $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). The forecast puts Q3 at $112.86 billion, 4.5% above guidance (e2652), based on past beats (e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055) and tempered by limited supply (e4911, e3733). For Q4, the writer assumes 14% sequential growth, below Q2's roughly 18% pace, as Vera Rubin shipments increase and hyperscaler growth picks up while supply remains tight (e2693, e3406, e2759, e5067). That puts FY2027 revenue at $419.34 billion, within rung 2.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"First half reported: e111 ($81.62bn Q1) + e2649 ($96.2bn Q2) = $177.82bn (F3). Q3: guidance $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (e2652, F5) raised by the stated 4.5% beat estimate (F11), which rests on the measured beats of 5.7% in Q2 (e3200, F7) and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 (e2452, F10) and the beat record in e5054/e5055 (3.1% above the high end is about 5.2% above the midpoint), trimmed for sold-out capacity (e4911, e3733): Q3 = $112.86bn (F12). Q4: stated estimate of 14% sequential growth (F13), benchmarked on the 17.9% realized Q2 pace (F4) and set below it for supply limits (e5067, e3733), held above the Q3 guidance's 12% because management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406) and Vera Rubin is shipping with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler (e2693): Q4 = $128.66bn (F14). FY2027 = $419.34bn (F15), inside rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). The rung is robust: rung 3 needs Q3 + Q4 below $214.2bn (roughly Q3 at the guidance low end and Q4 flat), rung 1 needs Q4 above $140.5bn (a 24.5% sequential jump). Reference $411bn consensus (e4333, F16) is also rung 2. Treating the 70% growth reports as FY2028 is supported by e2691 ('next year') and e5227, and checked by arithmetic (70% on FY2027 would imply a Q4 near $81bn, below Q2 and Q3 guidance). The Q4 pace sits above the analyst Q4 expectation implied by e5162 (65% on $68bn, about $112bn), but even that lower Q4 keeps the outcome in rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT10 S claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': 'F6 estimates $175 billion in Microsoft capital expenditure using e922, e4405 and outside estimates of the four companies’ total spending. F13 assumes 95–100% of combined capital expenditure goes to data centers, citing e1649, e5014, e1416, e4082 and e5376. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion in rung 4.', 'why_not': 'The passages support substantial data-center spending, but they do not establish that other spending accounts for at most 5% of the four companies’ combined capital expenditure. e1649 explains Amazon’s increase, and e5014 says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure without sizing it. The data-center estimates cover a broader or unspecified group. F8 is an outside estimate of total capital expenditure, so it does not independently show what the market expects for the data-center portion.'}}, 'defects': [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'detail': 'e1416 says “an estimated $750 billion.” F11 gives the correct amount but omits the qualifier.'}]}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Build of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure from the shown guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2), Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3), plus a stated estimate for Microsoft of $175bn (F6), set between a run-rate build from its January-March 2026 quarter of $30.88bn up 84% (e922; 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn, checked) and the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 $745bn, e2458 $760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn); combined $715-745bn (F7, sum checked). The stated 95-100% data-center share (F13) rests on outside sources labelling the same totals as data-center spending (NVIDIA's top-five nearly $800bn is called capex in e2759 and data-center capex in e4766/e5376; e4082 around $750bn of AI data-center spending); share gives $679.25-745bn (F14, checked), midpoint about $712bn, rung 4 chosen as a band the range overlaps with the reason given. Reference e5014 ($720-745bn, four companies) also sits in rung 4. A reader can check every step; the rung-4 call leans on two stated assumptions (Microsoft at $175bn rather than the ~$155bn run-rate, and a near-total data-center share, against e5014's weaker 'large portion'), and the basis text's '$175-190bn at their centres' understates the top of the residual range (the $760bn tally gives about $205bn), which does not weaken the chosen value.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-adjudication codex {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'no', 'why': \"The $679.25–745 billion range is calculated correctly and overlaps rung 4, but it depends on an unsupported assumption that at least 95% of the four companies’ capital spending goes to data centers. e5014 says only that 'a large portion' goes toward AI infrastructure. e1947 gives a figure for AI infrastructure without isolating data centers, and e5376 covers five companies. F8 measures total capital spending, so it does not establish a market reference for the data-center portion.\"}}, 'defects': [{'reader': 'Reader 1', 'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'decision': 'uphold', 'why': \"e1416 says 'an estimated $750 billion.' F11 copies the dollar amount correctly but records the qualifier as 'none.' The format requires 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number.'\"}]}\nreread2-adjudication claude {'members': {'v6': {'supported': 'yes', 'why': \"Every step can be checked against the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. The build uses the quoted guidance: Alphabet '$195 billion to $205 billion' (e1254), Meta '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549) and Amazon '$220 billion' (e1649). Microsoft's $175bn (F6) is a stated estimate placed between two checkable anchors. The first is a run-rate from '$30.88 billion, up 84%' (e922): at 15% a quarter, 30.88 + 35.5 + 40.8 + 47.0 is about $154bn. The second is the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 '$720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 '$745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion') after the other three companies' $540-570bn, which gives roughly $150-205bn and about $178-205bn at the tallies' centres. The basis text's '$175-190bn' understates the top of that span, but this does not move the chosen value outside it. F7's sum checks: 195+125+220+175 = 715 and 205+145+220+175 = 745. The 95-100% data-center share (F13) is written as its own estimate figure, as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), and the passages back it. NVIDIA's top-five figure is called 'CapEx ... nearly $800 billion' in e2759 and 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures' in e4766 and e5376: the same total under both labels. e4082 also puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year', roughly the size of the four companies' total capital expenditure. Reader 1 is right that no passage proves other spending is at most 5%, but 'supported' asks that the forecast follow from the passages plus the stated assumptions, not that a passage prove the assumption. F14's share checks: 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 and 745 x 1.00 = 745. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and rung 4 is chosen as a band the range overlaps, with the reason given, which the format permits. Reader 1's point that F8 measures total capital expenditure rather than the data-center part concerns the reference, not whether the forecast is supported, and neither reader marked it as a defect.\"}}, 'defects': [{'reader': 'Reader 1', 'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'decision': 'uphold', 'why': \"e1416's quote reads 'Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The format rule asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number', and the words just before the number are 'an estimated'. F11 records qualifier 'none', so its qualifier differs from what the quote writes. The number and scale (750, USD bn) are correct. F11 appears only in F13's rests_on and enters no calculation, so the defect does not change the outcome or the rung.\"}]}\nT11 P-v8 codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue totals $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). The Q3 guidance, prior guidance beat, and Rubin shipments support a modest Q3 beat (e2652, e3200, e5056, e2693). Expected hyperscaler growth and CoreWeave's live Rubin deployment support a further Q4 increase, with supply constraints and delivery lead times limiting the pace (e3406, e5118, e5067, e5205). The rising Data Center share and its role as the main Q3 growth driver support the estimated 93% and 93.5% shares (e3399, e5223). Those assumptions give $388.08 billion, within rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"The reported first half (e109 $75.25bn + e5079 $89.02bn = $164.27bn) plus a second half built from Q3 company guidance of $108.0bn +/-2% (e2652) lifted to $111.62bn by a stated 16% sequential estimate (beat history e3200, e5056; Rubin shipments e2693; ACIE growth e5061; supply limits e5067), taken at a 93% Data Center share (rising mix e3399, data center as the growth driver e5223) = $103.8bn, and a stated 15% Q4 sequential estimate (hyperscale reacceleration as Rubin supply builds e3406, e5061, e5118; constraints e5067, e5205) at a 93.5% share = $120.01bn. Total $388.08bn, inside the $350-400bn band. The band holds across plausible variation: Q3 at the guidance midpoint with 92.5% mix (e3401, $99.9bn) and a flat Q4 still give about $364bn, and reaching $400bn would need about 27% sequential Q4 Data Center growth under stated supply constraints. Reference: Morningstar's $361bn (e2295), also rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT12 O codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': 'The $16 billion forecast starts with the $25 billion July balance in e2620, subtracts $17 billion in assumed funding based on e2889 and e4729, and adds $8 billion in assumed new commitments still unfunded at year-end based on the announced and potential deals.', 'why_not': 'e2889 gives $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year but does not establish how much belongs to the July balance or when it will be funded. e4729 reports past purchases. The deal passages support continued investment activity but do not show which deals will be signed and still unfunded at year-end. The evidence therefore does not establish that funding from the July balance will exceed new commitments still outstanding, the condition needed to finish below $25 billion.'}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'e2620 (reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026, FY2027 Q2 end) and e2889 ($18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of the fiscal year), read as the scheduled H2 funding of the July balance; stated assumption F8 that $17 billion of it funds before year-end, leaving F9 = 25 - 17 = $8 billion; plus stated estimate F10 that $8 billion of post-July commitments (SB Energy e1957/e2404, Lancium e1919, Poolside e2207, the Anthropic and Thinking Machines talks e4648/e3934, discounted because most fund at closing or have uncertain signing dates) is still unfunded at year-end; F11 = $16 billion, below the $25 billion edge of rung 1. The weakest link is F10, a judgement not built from the sized deals (their full sizes, roughly $18-20 billion, would cross $25 billion only if all were signed and none funded by year-end), but it is stated as an estimate with its basis and a reader can check it against the passages.', 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-adjudication codex {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'yes', 'why': \"NVIDIA reported $25 billion in commitments as of July 26 (e2620) and said it had '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year' (e2889). F8 explicitly assumes that the $18 billion is part of the July balance and forecasts $17 billion in payments by year-end, leaving $8 billion. At that payment pace, new commitments would need to leave at least $17 billion unpaid to reach rung 2. The named deals include financing tied to a potential IPO and investments still under discussion, which gives a basis for discounting their full amounts. F10 estimates that $8 billion will remain unpaid, bringing the forecast to $16 billion. Both timing assumptions matter, and the passages give a reader enough to check why the answer makes them.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-adjudication claude {'members': {'v29': {'supported': 'yes', 'why': 'The chain is stated in full and a reader can check every step against the passages. F1 = $25 billion is the reported balance (\\'equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026\\', e2620). F8 = $17 billion rests on e2889 (\\'$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year\\'), which NVIDIA stated the same day it reported the $25 billion balance. The answer states its reading as an assumption in basis_text: \\'I treat the company\\'s remaining-year figure as the scheduled funding portion of the July balance.\\' That reading fits the quote. It is a commitment (\\'committed\\'), it is dated (\\'for the rest of its fiscal year\\'), and it is smaller than the $25 billion balance. The H1 pace in e4729 (\\'more than $42 billion buying equity stakes ... in the first half\\') shows funding at that scale is feasible. F9 = 25 - 17 = 8 and F11 = 8 + 8 = 16 are correct arithmetic. F10 = $8 billion is a declared estimate (kind estimate, with a basis_text and rests_on), which the format rules permit: \\'estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text\\'. Its basis names the sized post-July deals and why they are discounted: SB Energy $1.5 billion (e1957), Lancium \\'up to $3 billion\\' (e1919), Poolside, Anthropic \\'as much as $10 billion\\' (e4648) and Thinking Machines \\'approximately $2.5 billion\\' (e3934). It discounts them because they may fund at closing or have no signing date. With F9 at about $7-8 billion, the outcome reaches $25 billion only if more than $17 billion of new commitments are signed and still unfunded at year-end. That is close to every sized deal, including the Anthropic IPO amount that would fund at the IPO. Jensen Huang\\'s \\'those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat\\' (e2643) points the same way. So rung 1 (below $25 billion) follows from the passages and the stated assumptions. Reader 1\\'s ground is that e2889 \\'does not establish\\' the split or the timing. The system text rules that out as a reason: \\'\"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay.\\' Reader 1 names no passage that contradicts the stated reading, and the definition asks only that the forecast follow from the passages and the stated assumptions and be checkable against them.'}}, 'defects': []}\nT13 E K-unsupported\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"The $48 billion forecast applies 140% growth to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue expectation in e3660. The answer cites management's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856), planned Vera deployments (e4689), Anthropic's evaluation (e5044), and Arm server adoption (e4692).\", 'why_not': 'The doubling outlook points toward the $40 billion threshold if the full $20 billion base is Vera revenue, but e3660 gives that figure for NVIDIA CPU revenue as a whole. The deployment and evaluation passages give no order volumes, prices, or shipment timing to support 140% growth. The answer also calls $48 billion a discount without establishing a management target above $48 billion.'}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"F7, the writer's own estimate of 140% FY2028 growth (basis_text: 'because AI demand is strong'; rests_on F2, e4856 'more than double'), applied to the $20 billion FY2027 base (F1, e3660), gives $48 billion (F8). The reasons add unsized adoption passages (e4689, e5044, e4692) for a pace above the market.\", 'why_not': \"No passage quantifies the uplift from the guidance floor ($40 billion, F3) to $48 billion. The basis_text gives only 'AI demand is strong', and the adoption passages (e4689 deployment breadth, e5044 Anthropic evaluating, e4692 Arm share of data-center revenue) carry no Vera volume or revenue number. The answer's own reasoning points the other way. It says the FY2028 figure is a target to be discounted for time and execution, not taken in full. It says outside CPU market growth of about 50% (F4, F5, F6) is half the pace a doubling needs. It says AMD's benchmarks (e5399-e5404) show contested share. Discounting 'more than double' would put the outcome below $40 billion (rung 3), yet the forecast sits 20% above the guidance floor. Taking 'more than double' at face value could reach rung 2's bottom edge, but the answer does not give that as its ground. The closing sentence is also incoherent: it says the forecast 'would move to rung 2 if...' when it is already rung 2.\"}}, 'defects': []}\nT14 E K-disjoint\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': 'F7 assumes 80% to 115% growth on the $20 billion FY2027 base from e3660. It uses management’s doubling target in e4856 and e4691 as a benchmark, weighs market growth and adoption evidence in e4687, e4857, e4689, e5044 and e4692, and discounts for competition described in e5399 through e5404.', 'why_not': 'The stated assumption yields $36 billion to $43 billion, which overlaps rungs 3 and 2. Rung 4 requires at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion.'}}, 'defects': [{'type': 'broken structure', 'figures': ['F8'], 'detail': 'The selected rung 4 does not overlap F8’s correctly calculated $36 billion to $43 billion forecast range.'}]}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"Stated assumption F7: FY2028 Vera CPU growth of 80% to 115% on the $20 billion FY2027 base (F1, e3660), i.e. management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e4691, e4764) discounted for time and execution toward the roughly 50% CPU market pace (F4, e4687; F5/F6, e4857), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) against AMD's competitive benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404). F8 = apply_growth(F1, F7) = $36-43 billion; the answer places this in rung 4.\", 'why_not': \"The chosen rung 4 (at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion) does not hold the forecast: the answer's own outcome F8 is $36-43 billion, which overlaps only rung 3 ($30-40 billion) and rung 2 ($40-50 billion). Nothing in the passages supports a $20-30 billion outcome, which would need FY2028 growth of 0-50% against guidance of more than double; even the writer's discounted low end (80%) gives $36 billion. The reasons are also internally inconsistent: they say a confirmed delay 'would push it toward rung 4' while already answering rung 4. The growth estimate F7 itself is a stated, checkable judgement the system text permits (a discounted target), but the rung it is reported in is not supported by it or by the passages.\"}}, 'defects': [{'type': 'broken structure', 'figures': ['F8'], 'detail': \"v41 is answered rung 4 (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion), but its outcome figure F8 is the range $36-43 billion, which does not reach rung 4's band at all; it overlaps rung 3 ($30-40 billion) and rung 2 ($40-50 billion). A rung whose band the range does not reach is broken structure under the format rule for range outcomes.\"}]}\nT15 E claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"The forecast applies an estimated 85% growth rate to the $20 billion FY2027 base (e3660, e4856), yielding $37 billion in rung 3. NVIDIA's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856) anchors the estimate. Broad planned deployments (e4689) support growth above the outside market benchmarks (e4687, e4857). Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) gives no order volume, and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) support allowing for competition as Vera ramps.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"F8 = apply_growth(F1 $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660, F7 85%) = 37, rung 3 ($30-40bn). The band rests on F7's stated assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e4691, e4764) is discounted for time and execution because it is a full next-fiscal-year target for a product still ramping, with no volume shown in hand (e4689 says Vera 'will be deployed'; e5044 says Anthropic is only evaluating it). The size of the discount is argued from the outside market pace of about 50% a year (e4687; e4857's $35bn to $170bn, about 48% a year), so a doubling needs large share gains, and from AMD's competitive benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); the adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) is weighed the other way. The system text sanctions discounting a target with no volume in hand, and any discount below 2x puts the outcome in rung 3, so the band is checkable against the passages; the exact 85% is the writer's judgement. Equating the FY2027 $20bn CPU figure and the doubling with Vera CPU revenue rests on e4856's and e4691's passages, which tie both to Vera, and is stated in reasons. One loose sentence: the move condition 'if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion' would not change a forecast that already assumes a $20bn base.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT16 P claude\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': 'Reported first-half revenue of $177.82 billion (e111, e2649) anchors the forecast. Q3 guidance and the history of exceeding it (e2652, e5054, e5055, e3200), tempered by supply constraints (e3115, e4911), support the $112.5 billion Q3 estimate. The writer uses the 12% growth implied by Q3 guidance (e4899) as a benchmark for Q4, supported by the expected pickup in hyperscale growth and expanding Vera Rubin supply (e3406, e4283, e2759), with supply still constrained (e5067). This gives $416.32 billion, in rung 2.', 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). Its share of company revenue rose to 92.5% in Q2 (e3399), and management expects Data Center to drive most Q3 growth, with hyperscale growth picking up in Q4 (e5223, e4283). That supports the writer's 93% second-half share estimate. Applying it to the same $238.5 billion second-half company forecast gives $386.06 billion for the year, in rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v4': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"H1 FY2027 revenue is reported at $177.82bn (F1 e111 $81.62bn + F2 e2649 $96.2bn = F3). Q3 is the stated estimate F9 = $112.5bn, resting on Q3 guidance of $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (F7, e2652), the record of beating the guidance high end in each of 12 quarters by 3.1% on average over the last four (e5054, F8 e5055, which would give about $113.6bn), the $5.2bn beat of the Q2 midpoint (e3200) and the $3bn Q4 FY2026 beat (e2452), trimmed for the stated supply caps (e3115, e4911). Q4 is F9 grown at the stated 12% estimate F11, benchmarked to the 12% guided Q3 sequential pace (F10, e4899) and supported by hyperscale reacceleration in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e4283) and supply commitments rising to $279bn (e2759): F12 = $126.0bn, H2 F13 = $238.5bn, FY2027 F14 = $416.32bn, inside rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). The band is wide relative to the assumptions: Q3 at the guidance midpoint with only 5% Q4 growth still gives about $399bn, and rung 1 would need H2 of about $253bn. The reference, the $411bn consensus (F19, e4333), also sits in rung 2. The writer's reading of the '70% full-year growth' reports as the FY2028 outlook is backed by the transcript (e2691) and e5227 and by the arithmetic that 70% on $215.9bn would require Q4 near $81bn.\", 'why_not': ''}, 'v8': {'supported': 'yes', 'ground': \"H1 FY2027 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25bn (F4 e109 $75.25bn + F5 e2650 $89.0bn = F6; e5309 gives $164bn). H2 Data Center is the stated share estimate F16 = 93.0% of the family's own H2 company forecast F13 = $238.5bn, giving F17 = $221.81bn and FY2027 F18 = $386.06bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn). The 93.0% share rests on the quarter-by-quarter rise from 87.9% to 92.5% with narrowing gains (F15, e3399), Q2's 89.0 of 96.2, management's statement that Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (e5223, e4283, e2721), and the non-Data-Center remainder (Edge Computing, $7.2bn) growing a slower 13% (e5059). The share assumption is not decisive: at Q2's 92.5% share the outcome is about $385bn, and the guidance midpoint with 5% Q4 growth gives about $369bn; rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below about $186bn, below even the Q3 guidance low end carried flat, and rung 1 would need about $236bn. The part stays below its whole ($221.81bn < $238.5bn for H2; $386.06bn < $416.32bn for FY2027). The reference, Morningstar's $361bn (F20, e2295), is a quoted outside figure in rung 2.\", 'why_not': ''}}, 'defects': []}\nT17 E codex\nreread2-packets codex {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': 'The $34 billion forecast assumes Vera realizes 85% of NVIDIA’s $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook (e3660) and then doubles in FY2028, using management’s growth outlook as a benchmark (e4691, e4856).', 'why_not': 'Expected broad deployment (e4689) and Anthropic’s evaluation (e5044) indicate interest but give no purchase volumes or Vera revenue share. At the assumed doubling rate, Vera must realize at least 75% and less than 100% of the FY2027 CPU outlook to land in rung 3. The passages do not establish that range, and AMD’s benchmarks (e5400, e5403) do not quantify the effect on Vera sales.'}}, 'defects': []}\nreread2-packets claude {'members': {'v41': {'supported': 'no', 'ground': \"F4, the writer's own 85% estimate of FY2027 Vera revenue as a share of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU guidance (e3660), giving F5 = $17bn, combined with F6, the writer's own estimate of exactly 2x growth (benchmarked on e4856/e4691), giving F7 = $34bn. The estimates rest on e4689, e5044, e5400 and e5403.\", 'why_not': \"Rung 3 comes entirely from the 15% cut in F4. The same arithmetic without it gives $40bn (F3). The passages say FY2028 will be more than double the $20bn (e3660, e4856, e4764), which means above $40bn, in rung 2. No passage sizes the cut or gives a reason for it. e3660 restates a current-year expectation ('still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue'), and the writer recasts it as an 'opportunity'. No passage shows any non-Vera CPU revenue or any shortfall against the $20bn. e4689 (deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) argue against a cut, not for one. e5400 and e5403 are AMD's own benchmark claims and say nothing about sales. F6 takes exactly 2x where the guidance says 'more than double', so both assumptions lean down without a passage that quantifies either. The stated reason for the 85% ('the evidence gives no sales volumes') is missing confirmation, and the system text says that alone is no grounds. The system text does allow a default discount for a product with no shipments yet, but no passage shows Vera is not shipping, and the rung depends on the size of the cut, which nothing in the evidence supports.\"}}, 'defects': []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for i in reread2-packets/T*.md reread2-adjudication/T*.md; do awk 'NR <= 4 || /\"'^## / {print FILENAME, NR, substr($0,1,110)}'\"' \\\"\"'$i\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for i in reread2-packets/T*.md reread2-adjudication/T*.md; do awk 'NR <= 4 || /\"'^## / {print FILENAME, NR, substr($0,1,110)}'\"' \\\"\"'$i\"; done'","aggregated_output":"reread2-packets/T01.md 1 # Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\nreread2-packets/T01.md 2 \nreread2-packets/T01.md 3 You are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), \nreread2-packets/T01.md 4 format rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answe\nreread2-packets/T02.md 1 # Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\nreread2-packets/T02.md 2 \nreread2-packets/T02.md 3 You are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), \nreread2-packets/T02.md 4 format rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answe\nreread2-packets/T03.md 1 # Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\nreread2-packets/T03.md 2 \nreread2-packets/T03.md 3 You are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), \nreread2-packets/T03.md 4 format rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answe\nreread2-packets/T04.md 1 # Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\nreread2-packets/T04.md 2 \nreread2-packets/T04.md 3 You are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), \nreread2-packets/T04.md 4 format rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answe\nreread2-packets/T05.md 1 # Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\nreread2-packet