{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '1,133p' && nl -ba S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '132,283p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    19\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n    20\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    49\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    55\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n    56\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    58\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    60\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    65\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n    66\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n    67\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n    68\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    69\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n    70\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n    79\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n    80\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   132\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   133\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   134\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   135\t    },\n   136\t    {\n   137\t      \"id\": \"F8\",\n   138\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   139\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   140\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   141\t      \"value\": null,\n   142\t      \"low\": 720,\n   143\t      \"high\": 745,\n   144\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   145\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n   146\t      \"quote_number\": \"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\n   147\t      \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n   148\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   149\t      \"operation\": null,\n   150\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   151\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   152\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   153\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\n   154\t    },\n   155\t    {\n   156\t      \"id\": \"F9\",\n   157\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined, tracking\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   158\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   159\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   160\t      \"value\": 745,\n   161\t      \"low\": null,\n   162\t      \"high\": null,\n   163\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   164\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1856\",\n   165\t      \"quote_number\": \"$745 billion\",\n   166\t      \"qualifier\": \"near\",\n   167\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   168\t      \"operation\": null,\n   169\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   170\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   171\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   172\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   173\t    },\n   174\t    {\n   175\t      \"id\": \"F10\",\n   176\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"top four hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined, planned\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   177\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   178\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   179\t      \"value\": 760,\n   180\t      \"low\": null,\n   181\t      \"high\": null,\n   182\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   183\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2458\",\n   184\t      \"quote_number\": \"$760 billion\",\n   185\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   186\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   187\t      \"operation\": null,\n   188\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   189\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   190\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   191\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   192\t    },\n   193\t    {\n   194\t      \"id\": \"F11\",\n   195\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"tech giants\", \"measure\": \"data-center spending\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 750,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1416\",\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   205\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   206\t      \"operation\": null,\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"five largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   215\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   216\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   217\t      \"value\": 800,\n   218\t      \"low\": null,\n   219\t      \"high\": null,\n   220\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   221\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5376\",\n   222\t      \"quote_number\": \"$800 billion\",\n   223\t      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\n   224\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   225\t      \"operation\": null,\n   226\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   227\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   228\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   229\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   230\t    },\n   231\t    {\n   232\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n   233\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   234\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   235\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   236\t      \"value\": null,\n   237\t      \"low\": 95,\n   238\t      \"high\": 100,\n   239\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n   240\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   241\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   242\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   243\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   244\t      \"operation\": null,\n   245\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\n   246\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\n   247\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   248\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   249\t    },\n   250\t    {\n   251\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n   252\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   253\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   254\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   255\t      \"value\": null,\n   256\t      \"low\": 679.25,\n   257\t      \"high\": 745,\n   258\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   259\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   260\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   261\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   262\t      \"inputs\": [\"F7\", \"F13\"],\n   263\t      \"operation\": \"share\",\n   264\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   265\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   266\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v6\"],\n   267\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   268\t    }\n   269\t  ],\n   270\t  \"answers\": [\n   271\t    {\n   272\t      \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   273\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   274\t      \"rung\": 4,\n   275\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   276\t      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\n   277\t      \"why\": null,\n   278\t      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e1254\", \"e1549\", \"e1649\", \"e922\", \"e4405\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\", \"e2759\", \"e5376\", \"e4567\", \"e4566\", \"e1856\", \"e2458\", \"e122\", \"e1545\", \"e5292\", \"e2081\", \"e4807\"],\n   279\t      \"pushes\": [\n   280\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1254\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   281\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1549\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   282\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1649\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   283\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e922\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'Pass|Stop|36|correct|unresolved|qualif|discount' RESULT.md judg*txt REPAIR-DESIGN.md && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,60p;82,107p'; cd ../../.. && nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '324,350p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'Pass|Stop|36|correct|unresolved|qualif|discount' RESULT.md judg*txt REPAIR-DESIGN.md && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,60p;82,107p'; cd ../../.. && nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '324,350p'\"","aggregated_output":"judging-v13s.txt:4:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\njudging-v13s.txt:5:- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\njudging-v13s.txt:9:- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:29:| `answers[].outcome` (string) | `figures[].outcome_for`: array of member ids (enum) | the enum; C7: exactly one marked figure per forecast member, none for an unresolved one, no member listed twice on one figure |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:30:| `answers[].reference {figure, unavailable}` | `figures[].reference_for`: array of member ids (enum); `answers[].reference_unavailable`: nullable string | the enum; C5: exactly one of a marked figure or a non-empty reason for a forecast member; for an unresolved member, a marker or reason is refused (C7) |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:65:| `value` | \"A point, or the number a bound qualifies. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:70:| `expression` | \"Source only: copied word for word from that passage's Quote line, never its summary: the qualifier, the number and its scale, and nothing else, for example 'more than $2 billion', 'about 40%', '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%', '$3–4 billion', '$300 million or more', 'more than triple'. Qualifiers: more than, over, at least, nearly, almost, approximately, about, around, roughly, up to, less than, below, under, and 'or more' or 'or less' after the number. A negated figure ('will not exceed $5 billion') cannot be a source; state it as an estimate.\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:71:| `component` | \"Source only, which part of the expression the figure takes. value: a plain amount or a percentage ('$2 billion'; 'about 40%' is value 40 with bound approx). bound: a qualified amount ('more than $2 billion' is value 2 with bound '>'). low and high: one figure for each end of a written range, each with value. midpoint and relative: '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' is two figures, 4.5 USD bn and 3 %. multiplier: 'double' or 'triple' (value 2 or 3, unit x; 'more than double' adds bound '>').\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:81:| `adjusts` | \"Estimate only, optional: the figure (by id, never a passage) this estimate changes, and how: discount, uplift or trim; the effect of the change is shown to readers.\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:82:| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is. Each member answered as a forecast has exactly one figure marked; an unresolved member has none. Its what must equal the member's outcome key.\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:89:| `shape` | \"forecast: one figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference figure or reference_unavailable. unresolved: why; no rung, no marked figure, no reference.\" |\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:160:- **A1, correct forms in v13r.** Each must pass:\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:171:  - an unresolved member with nothing marked;\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:180:  - \"$300 billion\" stripped of its qualifier (C1);\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:191:  - no outcome marker, two outcome markers, and a marker on an unresolved member (C7);\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:223:  - whether the answer is correct against the shown passages.\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:225:- **Reported:** per case, supported forecasts, justified unresolved answers, and refusals by rule.\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:227:**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:231:- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a correct answer;\nREPAIR-DESIGN.md:236:**A clean result** goes to Alex as a request for the paid comparison: the same 36 requests rebuilt with v13r, under a new decision\njudging-v13-draft.txt:4:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\njudging-v13-draft.txt:5:- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\njudging-v13-draft.txt:9:- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\nRESULT.md:31:  - adding amounts of unknown overlap (#36: $25B + $18B);\nRESULT.md:50:the total added a CPU forecast whose overlap was unresolved (D721).\nRESULT.md:65:  The model keeps importance: drivers, weights, discounts, and overlap stated as an assumption. A blind reader judges whether an\nRESULT.md:67:- **When figures cannot be reconciled, the forecast shows as unresolved,** not as two incompatible answers.\nRESULT.md:71:  sourced mix), q19 (an execution discount), q22 (FY2028 from q01's base, widened by judgement).\nRESULT.md:86:- **The paid gate** (Alex's ceiling; 36 calls, about $0.2–0.4 estimated):\nRESULT.md:90:  - **Pass:** the joint arm publishes both answers usable and source-supported in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot,\nRESULT.md:92:    unresolved overlap, scope or unsized adjustments that decide the band.\nRESULT.md:93:  - **Stop:** any such published defect, either coverage line missed, or an incomplete comparison.\n     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n    60\t\n    82\t| Field | Description [52] |\n    83\t|---|---|\n    84\t| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n    85\t| `id` | \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\" |\n    86\t| `quote_number` | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\" |\n    87\t| `qualifier` | enum adds `near`; \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    88\t| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure built on the part's quantity to one built on the whole's (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole only to give the rest. When its inputs are periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    89\t| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    90\t| `reference_for` | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure.\" |\n    91\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\" |\n    92\t\n    93\t**[52] The family header is rendered from the family's data** (no member id is fixed in code):\n    94\t- \"Relations: <part> is part of <whole>. For each of <compare periods> and for the outcomes, the part's lowest value may not exceed the\n    95\t  whole's highest. A sum never adds the part into the whole.\"\n    96\t- \"Shared quantities: …\" (unchanged list).\n    97\t- \"Outcome keys …, in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    98\t- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\n    99\t\n   100\t**figures[]**\n   101\t\n   102\t| Field | Type | Description |\n   103\t|---|---|---|\n   104\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n   105\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n   106\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n   107\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n   324\t  stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 7.3 Iterating\n   327\t\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   346\t- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n   347\t  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n   348\t  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   349\t  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n   350\t  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def check\\\\b|def publish_decision|def from_answer|def format|def tally|QUALIFIERS|qualifier' small_cases.py small_mutants.py paid_gate.py calc_small.py render_v13.py; cd ../../.. && rg -n '\"'^def check_schema'\"' src/hivemind/model.py && ls -a | rg 'venv'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def check\\\\b|def publish_decision|def from_answer|def format|def tally|QUALIFIERS|qualifier' small_cases.py small_mutants.py paid_gate.py calc_small.py render_v13.py; cd ../../.. && rg -n '\"'^def check_schema'\"' src/hivemind/model.py && ls -a | rg 'venv'\"","aggregated_output":"render_v13.py:46:                                'it, qualifiers included (for example \"more than $2 billion\", \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\", \"more than triple\").'}),\nrender_v13.py:78:QUALIFIERS = list(calc_small.QUALIFIERS)                 # one owner: the checker's closed list\nrender_v13.py:110:        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\nsmall_mutants.py:13:    ('S1 qualifier', \"        if q not in written:\\n\", \"        if False:\\n\"),\ncalc_small.py:19:QUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\ncalc_small.py:21:QUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\ncalc_small.py:22:QUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\ncalc_small.py:152:FIGURE_FIELDS = ('id', 'what', 'kind', 'basis', 'value', 'low', 'high', 'unit', 'quote_number', 'qualifier', 'inputs', 'operation',\ncalc_small.py:160:def from_answer(data, family):\ncalc_small.py:397:        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\ncalc_small.py:398:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\ncalc_small.py:487:        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\ncalc_small.py:491:            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\ncalc_small.py:493:                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\ncalc_small.py:496:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\ncalc_small.py:750:        elif (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none':\ncalc_small.py:751:            recorded.append({'type': 'qualified source', 'figure': fid, 'qualifier': f['qualifier']})\ncalc_small.py:764:def check(family, passages, calc):\ncalc_small.py:782:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\nsmall_cases.py:21:FIELDS = ['id', 'what', 'kind', 'basis', 'value', 'low', 'high', 'unit', 'evidence_id', 'quote_number', 'qualifier', 'inputs', 'operation',\nsmall_cases.py:83:        F('F1', W('company', 'FY2027 Q1'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=81.6, evidence_id='e1', quote_number='$81.6 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:84:        F('F2', W('company', 'FY2027 Q2'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=96.2, evidence_id='e2', quote_number='$96.2 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:86:          quote_number='$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:91:        F('F7', W('Data Center', 'FY2027 Q1'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=75.3, evidence_id='e4', quote_number='$75.3 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:92:        F('F8', W('Data Center', 'FY2027 Q2'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=89.0, evidence_id='e5', quote_number='$89.0 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:94:          quote_number='92.5%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:100:          qualifier='none', reference_for=['v4']),\nsmall_cases.py:175:    figs = [F('B', W('company', 'FY2026 Q4'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=68.1, evidence_id='e9', quote_number='$68.1 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:176:            F('G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q4', measure='revenue growth'), 'source', 'third_party', '%', value=65.0, evidence_id='e8', quote_number='65%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:187:@case('A1', '\"over $300 billion\" (from \"well over $300 billion\") with qualifier over', 'pass', recorded='qualified source')\nsmall_cases.py:189:    figs = [F('S', W('Data Center', 'FY2028'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=300.0, evidence_id='e7', quote_number='$300 billion', qualifier='over'),\nsmall_cases.py:196:    figs = [F('S', W('company', 'FY2027', measure='margin change'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=-3.0, evidence_id='e12', quote_number='-3%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:201:@case('A1', '\"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\"', 'pass')\nsmall_cases.py:203:    figs = [F('S', W('plant', 'FY2027', measure='cost'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=0.3, evidence_id='e11', quote_number='$300 million', qualifier='or more'),\nsmall_cases.py:210:    figs = [F('S', W('four companies', 'CY2026', measure='capex'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=1200.0, evidence_id='e13', quote_number='$1.2 trillion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:243:                       quote_number='92%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:252:    figs = [F('S1', W('market', 'CY2026'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=35.0, evidence_id='e23', quote_number='$35 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:253:            F('S2', W('market', 'CY2030'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=170.0, evidence_id='e23', quote_number='$170 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:262:              quote_number='$35 billion to $170 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:270:              quote_number='$195 billion to $205 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:287:              quote_number='$745 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:296:    figs = [F('B', W('company', 'FY2027', measure='CPU sales'), 'source', 'guidance', 'USD bn', value=20.0, evidence_id='e10', quote_number='$20 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:297:            F('X', W('company', 'FY2028', measure='CPU sales multiple'), 'source', 'guidance', 'x', value=2.0, evidence_id='e10', quote_number='twice', qualifier='roughly'),\nsmall_cases.py:304:    figs = [F('P', W('unit', 'FY2027', measure='price change'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=12.0, evidence_id='e17', quote_number='12%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:313:    figs = [F('A1', W('bonds', 'FY2027', measure='size'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=10.0, evidence_id='e14', quote_number='US$10 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:314:            F('A2', W('company', 'FY2027 Q2'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=96.2, evidence_id='e14', quote_number='$96.2bn', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:316:              quote_number='between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:345:    figs = [F('S1', W('four companies', 'CY2026', measure='capex'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=745.0, evidence_id='e18', quote_number='$745 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:346:            F('S2', W('four companies', 'CY2026', measure='capex'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=760.0, evidence_id='e19', quote_number='$760 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:383:defect('a qualifier the quote does not write (S1)', 'S1', lambda a: fig(a, 'F1').update(qualifier='about'))\nsmall_cases.py:422:      quote_number='12%', qualifier='none')]),\nsmall_cases.py:434:    qualifier='none')), a['figures'].append(F(\nsmall_cases.py:451:    qualifier='over', outcome_for=['v8'])), a['answers'][1].update(rung=3)))\nsmall_cases.py:453:    qualifier=None, value=110.0, basis_text='mix'), fig(a, 'F10').update(value=121.0), fig(a, 'F11').update(value=126.5),\nsmall_cases.py:503:    figs = [F('S', W('program', 'FY2027', measure='cost'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=5.0, evidence_id='e15', quote_number='€5 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:510:    figs = [F('S', W('four companies', 'CY2026', measure='capex'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=1.2, evidence_id='e13', quote_number='$1.2 trillion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:517:    figs = [F('S', W('company', 'FY2027', measure='margin change'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=3.0, evidence_id='e12', quote_number='3%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:522:@case('A2', 'a qualifier dropped: \"over $300 billion\" as none (S1)', 'S1')\nsmall_cases.py:524:    figs = [F('S', W('Data Center', 'FY2028'), 'source', 'third_party', 'USD bn', value=300.0, evidence_id='e7', quote_number='$300 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:537:    'G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q1', measure='growth'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=5.0, evidence_id='e1', quote_number='5%', qualifier='none')))\nsmall_cases.py:541:    'T', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='tolerance'), 'source', 'guidance', '%', value=2.0, evidence_id='e3', quote_number='2%', qualifier='none')))\nsmall_cases.py:543:    'L', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3'), 'source', 'guidance', 'USD bn', value=105.84, evidence_id='e14', quote_number='$105.84 billion', qualifier='none')))\nsmall_cases.py:578:    return [F('Q1', W('company', 'FY2027 Q1'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=81.6, evidence_id='e1', quote_number='$81.6 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:579:            F('Q2', W('company', 'FY2027 Q2'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=96.2, evidence_id='e2', quote_number='$96.2 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:581:              quote_number='$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:582:            F('C', W('company', 'FY2027'), 'source', 'consensus', 'USD bn', value=411.0, evidence_id='e6', quote_number='$411 billion', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:617:    figs = [F('M', W('company', 'FY2027', measure='gross margin'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=40.0, evidence_id='e22', quote_number='40%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:618:            F('S', W('segment', 'FY2027', measure='share of margin'), 'source', 'reported', '%', value=25.0, evidence_id='e22', quote_number='25%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:627:              quote_number='between 72% and 73%', qualifier='none'),\nsmall_cases.py:629:              quote_number='$10 billion', qualifier='as much as'),\nsmall_cases.py:659:    qualifier='none')))\n184:def check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\nkeys=json.load(open(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\"))\nfor i,v in keys.items():\n print(i,v[\\\"case\\\"],v[\\\"writer\\\"])\n for sub in (\\\"reread2-packets\\\",\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\"):\n  for reader in (\\\"codex\\\",\\\"claude\\\"):\n   f=pathlib.Path(f\\\"{sub}/{i}.{reader}.json\\\")\n   if f.exists():\n    o=json.load(f.open());print(sub,reader,{k:m[\\\"supported\\\"] for k,m in o[\\\"members\\\"].items()},o[\\\"defects\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\nkeys=json.load(open(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\"))\nfor i,v in keys.items():\n print(i,v[\\\"case\\\"],v[\\\"writer\\\"])\n for sub in (\\\"reread2-packets\\\",\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\"):\n  for reader in (\\\"codex\\\",\\\"claude\\\"):\n   f=pathlib.Path(f\\\"{sub}/{i}.{reader}.json\\\")\n   if f.exists():\n    o=json.load(f.open());print(sub,reader,{k:m[\\\"supported\\\"] for k,m in o[\\\"members\\\"].items()},o[\\\"defects\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"T01 P K-midpoint\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nT02 O claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v29': 'no'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v29': 'yes'} []\nreread2-adjudication codex {'v29': 'no'} []\nreread2-adjudication claude {'v29': 'yes'} []\nT03 E K-overlap\nreread2-packets codex {'v41': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v41': 'no'} []\nT04 P codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nT05 P-v8 claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v8': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v8': 'yes'} []\nT06 S codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v6': 'no'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v6': 'no'} []\nT07 P-v4 codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes'} []\nT08 P K-misread\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F7'], 'detail': 'e2652 quotes $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. The allowed point is the $108.0 billion midpoint, and the allowed range is $105.84 billion to $110.16 billion. F7 records $108.5 billion.'}]\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F7'], 'detail': \"e2652 quotes '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The format rules allow that expression as low and high ($105.84bn to $110.16bn) or as its midpoint, $108.0bn, for value. F7 gives value 108.5, which is neither the midpoint nor either end. F7 is not an input to any computed figure (it appears only in F9's rests_on), and F9's basis text uses the correct $108.0bn midpoint and $110.16bn high end, so no outcome changes.\"}]\nT09 P-v4 claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes'} []\nT10 S claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v6': 'no'} [{'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'detail': 'e1416 says “an estimated $750 billion.” F11 gives the correct amount but omits the qualifier.'}]\nreread2-packets claude {'v6': 'yes'} []\nreread2-adjudication codex {'v6': 'no'} [{'reader': 'Reader 1', 'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'decision': 'uphold', 'why': \"e1416 says 'an estimated $750 billion.' F11 copies the dollar amount correctly but records the qualifier as 'none.' The format requires 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number.'\"}]\nreread2-adjudication claude {'v6': 'yes'} [{'reader': 'Reader 1', 'type': 'number misread', 'figures': ['F11'], 'decision': 'uphold', 'why': \"e1416's quote reads 'Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The format rule asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number', and the words just before the number are 'an estimated'. F11 records qualifier 'none', so its qualifier differs from what the quote writes. The number and scale (750, USD bn) are correct. F11 appears only in F13's rests_on and enters no calculation, so the defect does not change the outcome or the rung.\"}]\nT11 P-v8 codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v8': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v8': 'yes'} []\nT12 O codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v29': 'no'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v29': 'yes'} []\nreread2-adjudication codex {'v29': 'yes'} []\nreread2-adjudication claude {'v29': 'yes'} []\nT13 E K-unsupported\nreread2-packets codex {'v41': 'no'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v41': 'no'} []\nT14 E K-disjoint\nreread2-packets codex {'v41': 'no'} [{'type': 'broken structure', 'figures': ['F8'], 'detail': 'The selected rung 4 does not overlap F8’s correctly calculated $36 billion to $43 billion forecast range.'}]\nreread2-packets claude {'v41': 'no'} [{'type': 'broken structure', 'figures': ['F8'], 'detail': \"v41 is answered rung 4 (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion), but its outcome figure F8 is the range $36-43 billion, which does not reach rung 4's band at all; it overlaps rung 3 ($30-40 billion) and rung 2 ($40-50 billion). A rung whose band the range does not reach is broken structure under the format rule for range outcomes.\"}]\nT15 E claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v41': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v41': 'yes'} []\nT16 P claude\nreread2-packets codex {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v4': 'yes', 'v8': 'yes'} []\nT17 E codex\nreread2-packets codex {'v41': 'no'} []\nreread2-packets claude {'v41': 'no'} []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba RESULT.md | sed -n '52,112p' && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '100,144p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '594,647p;729,821p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba RESULT.md | sed -n '52,112p' && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '100,144p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '594,647p;729,821p'\"","aggregated_output":"    52\t## The converged design (for a new judge version Alex opens)\n    53\t\n    54\t- **One judging call per related family.** Same company and fiscal year, overlapping quantities. Each question keeps its own answer,\n    55\t  and every answer reads from one calculation.\n    56\t- **The judge labels the figures it uses:** what each measures (population, scope, period) and whether it is a source figure, a\n    57\t  calculation or an assumption. Extraction v74i is unchanged.\n    58\t- **Code checks identity and arithmetic only:**\n    59\t  1. a source figure appears in the shown passage it cites;\n    60\t  2. the arithmetic recomputes;\n    61\t  3. a declared part fits within its whole for the same period and scenario;\n    62\t  4. one value per shared quantity, role and scenario across the family;\n    63\t  5. a market reference points to shown external material, or says none.\n    64\t\n    65\t  The model keeps importance: drivers, weights, discounts, and overlap stated as an assumption. A blind reader judges whether an\n    66\t  assumption that crosses a boundary is supported.\n    67\t- **When figures cannot be reconciled, the forecast shows as unresolved,** not as two incompatible answers.\n    68\t- **Bounded to the figures the open questions consume** (D24, D28).\n    69\t- **Families:** declared from stored cases for the test. Production would need a matching version that names the relationship.\n    70\t- **The frozen gold's reasoning is kept:** q01 (upside drivers without a fixed increment), q02 (a segment from the shared total and a\n    71\t  sourced mix), q19 (an execution discount), q22 (FY2028 from q01's base, widened by judgement).\n    72\t\n    73\t## The interim for a first release\n    74\t\n    75\tPublish questions, evidence and events, and show number questions as \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the forecast text and its\n    76\thistory withheld together (`bundle.py`).\n    77\t\n    78\t## The one test (registered with the design)\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The $0 gate.** Hand-reconstruct, in the new format:\n    81\t  - old-2 8729/8737, 8734 and 8595, and old-3 9166;\n    82\t  - the four gold controls;\n    83\t  - three planted errors (an amount absent from its quote, a wrong sum, an internal forecast labelled consensus).\n    84\t\n    85\t  The format must expose every defect, represent every control, and fail every plant. Any miss stops the line.\n    86\t- **The paid gate** (Alex's ceiling; 36 calls, about $0.2–0.4 estimated):\n    87\t  - **The arms:** the 09-20 total and Data Center pair in each draw, three samples each, judged jointly and separately under the same\n    88\t    format and checks. Plus three samples each on the O, S and E requests. Every output is read against its source with the arm\n    89\t    hidden.\n    90\t  - **Pass:** the joint arm publishes both answers usable and source-supported in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot,\n    91\t    and at least 3 more usable pairs than the separate arm. Zero published hard contradictions, internal-as-market references, or\n    92\t    unresolved overlap, scope or unsized adjustments that decide the band.\n    93\t  - **Stop:** any such published defect, either coverage line missed, or an incomplete comparison.\n   100\t        'unit': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['USD bn', '%', 'x'], 'description': 'USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = '\n   101\t                 '1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).'},\n   102\t        'evidence_id': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids, 'description': 'Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.'}),\n   103\t        'quote_number': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a \"\n   104\t                                  \"piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent \"\n   105\t                                  \"sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', \"\n   106\t                                  \"'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written \"\n   107\t                                  \"range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change \"\n   108\t                                  \"written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, \"\n   109\t                                  \"plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"}),\n   110\t        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\n   111\t                               \"the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"}),\n   112\t        'inputs': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"}),\n   113\t        'operation': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': ['sum', 'difference', 'product', 'ratio', 'share', 'growth', 'apply_growth'],\n   114\t                               'description': 'Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); '\n   115\t                               'product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units '\n   116\t                               'give x; a ÷ x keeps a\\'s unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; '\n   117\t                               'growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and '\n   118\t                               'product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range '\n   119\t                               'input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the '\n   120\t                               'midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds '\n   121\t                               \"a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the \"\n   122\t                               'part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds '\n   123\t                               'one quantity for the same period twice. When '\n   124\t                               'a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 '\n   125\t                               \"Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"}),\n   126\t        'basis_text': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Estimate only: the assumption, in words.'}),\n   127\t        'rests_on': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': 'Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.'}),\n   128\t        'outcome_for': dict(members, description='The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure '\n   129\t                            'with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, '\n   130\t                            \"none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"),\n   131\t        'reference_for': dict(members, description=\"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath \"\n   132\t                              'it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted '\n   133\t                              \"consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period \"\n   134\t                              \"(a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"),\n   135\t    })\n   136\t    push = obj({'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   137\t    theme_push = obj({'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': theme_ids or ['none']},\n   138\t                      'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   139\t    answer = obj({\n   140\t        'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids},\n   141\t        'shape': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['forecast', 'unresolved'], 'description': 'forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and '\n   142\t                  'a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable '\n   143\t                  'null; no figure marked for it.'},\n   144\t        'rung': nullable({'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it '\n   594\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\n   595\t    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n   596\t    period between them. A figure beneath another of its own quantity and period (a point a final range rests on) is its basis, not a\n   597\t    second value.\"\"\"\n   598\t    tops, seen_ids = set(), set()\n   599\t\n   600\t    def walk(fid, keys):\n   601\t        if fid not in figs or (fid, keys) in seen_ids:\n   602\t            return\n   603\t        seen_ids.add((fid, keys))\n   604\t        f = figs[fid]\n   605\t        k = _key(f['what'])\n   606\t        if k not in keys:\n   607\t            tops.add(fid)\n   608\t        for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs]:\n   609\t            walk(r, keys | {k})\n   610\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   611\t        walk(out, frozenset())\n   612\t    return tops\n   613\t\n   614\t\n   615\tdef _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\n   616\t    errors = []\n   617\t    by_key = {}\n   618\t    for fid in _adopted(outcomes, figs):                   # [54] forecasts the outcomes adopt; an unused alternative is not counted\n   619\t        if figs[fid]['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   620\t            by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   621\t    nodes = {}\n   622\t    for fid, f in figs.items():\n   623\t        if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   624\t            nodes.setdefault(_key(f['what']), []).append(fid)\n   625\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   626\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   627\t            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n   628\t    for want in family.get('shared', []):\n   629\t        key = _key(want)\n   630\t        node = by_key.get(key) or nodes.get(key, [])\n   631\t        for v, out in sorted(outcomes.items()):\n   632\t            reached, stack, seen_ids = [], [out], set()\n   633\t            while stack:\n   634\t                fid = stack.pop()\n   635\t                if fid in seen_ids or fid not in figs:\n   636\t                    continue\n   637\t                seen_ids.add(fid)\n   638\t                f = figs[fid]\n   639\t                if _key(f['what']) == key:\n   640\t                    reached.append(fid)\n   641\t                    if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   642\t                        continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n   643\t                stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n   644\t            if not reached:\n   645\t                continue\n   646\t            if len(node) != 1:\n   647\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n   729\t    return errors\n   730\t\n   731\t\n   732\tdef _recorded(family, figs, outcomes):\n   733\t    recorded = []\n   734\t    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n   735\t    for f in figs.values():\n   736\t        fid = f['id']\n   737\t        if f['kind'] == 'estimate':\n   738\t            recorded.append({'type': 'estimate', 'figure': fid, 'assumption': f.get('basis_text')})\n   739\t            rested = {figs[r]['what']['scope'] for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs}\n   740\t            if any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= rested for ps in scopes):\n   741\t                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole in one estimate', 'figure': fid})\n   742\t        elif f['kind'] == 'computed':\n   743\t            money = [figs[i] for i in f['inputs'] if figs[i]['unit'] == 'USD bn']\n   744\t            if any(_quantity(i['what']) != _quantity(f['what']) for i in money) and f['operation'] != 'share':\n   745\t                recorded.append({'type': 'conversion between quantities', 'figure': fid})\n   746\t            if f['operation'] in ('apply_growth', 'product') and any(i['what']['period'] != f['what']['period'] for i in money):\n   747\t                recorded.append({'type': 'carried across periods', 'figure': fid})\n   748\t            if f['operation'] == 'sum' and any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= {i['what']['scope'] for i in money} for ps in scopes):\n   749\t                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole added for different periods', 'figure': fid})\n   750\t        elif (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none':\n   751\t            recorded.append({'type': 'qualified source', 'figure': fid, 'qualifier': f['qualifier']})\n   752\t    used = set()\n   753\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   754\t        used |= {out} | _beneath(out, figs)\n   755\t    by_key = {}\n   756\t    for fid in used:\n   757\t        by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   758\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   759\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   760\t            recorded.append({'type': 'one quantity, several figures', 'figure': sorted(ids)[0], 'figures': sorted(ids), 'what': list(k)})\n   761\t    return recorded\n   762\t\n   763\t\n   764\tdef check(family, passages, calc):\n   765\t    figs = {f['id']: f for f in calc['figures'] if isinstance(f.get('id'), str)}\n   766\t    errors = _structure(family, passages, calc, figs)\n   767\t    if errors:\n   768\t        return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': [], 'published': {}}\n   769\t    engine = _Calc(figs)\n   770\t    errors = _sources(passages, figs) + _arithmetic(figs, engine)\n   771\t    for f in figs.values():\n   772\t        if engine.interval(f['id']) is None and not any(e[1].startswith(f['id'] + ':') for e in errors):\n   773\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{f[\"id\"]}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n   774\t    if errors:\n   775\t        return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': [], 'published': {}}\n   776\t    answer_errors, outcomes = _answers(family, figs, engine, calc['answers'])\n   777\t    errors = answer_errors + _shared(family, figs, outcomes) + _part_whole(family, figs, engine, outcomes) + _sums(family, figs)\n   778\t    published = {v: list(engine.interval(o)) + [None] for v, o in outcomes.items()}\n   779\t    return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': _recorded(family, figs, outcomes), 'published': published}\n   780\t\n   781\t\n   782\tdef publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n   783\t    \"\"\"'refused' when a check fails; 'held' when a member is unresolved and a declared relation joins it to another member; else\n   784\t    'publish' (an unresolved member is shown as unresolved).\"\"\"\n   785\t    if result['errors']:\n   786\t        return 'refused'\n   787\t    unresolved = {a['view_id'] for a in calc['answers'] if a.get('shape') == 'unresolved'}\n   788\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   789\t        ends = {rel.get('part'), rel.get('whole'), rel.get('later'), rel.get('earlier')} - {None}\n   790\t        if ends & unresolved and ends - unresolved:\n   791\t            return 'held'\n   792\t    return 'publish'\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && ls -a | rg 'reread|tally|census'; cd ../../.. && sed -n '177,239p' src/hivemind/model.py; sed -n '11474,11490p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && rg -n 'would need Q4|\\\\\"'$138 billion|not supported|15%|unsupported'\"' P/answers/codex.json reread2-packets/T17.codex.json reread2-packets/T17.claude.json reread2-packets/T11.claude.json | cut -c 1-1100\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && ls -a | rg 'reread|tally|census'; cd ../../.. && sed -n '177,239p' src/hivemind/model.py; sed -n '11474,11490p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && rg -n 'would need Q4|\\\\\"'$138 billion|not supported|15%|unsupported'\"' P/answers/codex.json reread2-packets/T17.codex.json reread2-packets/T17.claude.json reread2-packets/T11.claude.json | cut -c 1-1100\"","aggregated_output":"blind_reread.py\nblind_reread2.py\ns4_census.py\n            time.sleep(5 * (attempt + 1))\n    failure = TransportError(last or 'no answer')\n    if last_http:\n        failure.http_status, failure.response_body, failure.response_body_base64 = last_http\n    raise failure\n\n\ndef check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n    \"\"\"A small validator for the subset of JSON Schema the prompts use: object/required/additionalProperties,\n    array/items/minItems/maxItems, string/enum/pattern, integer, number, boolean, null in anyOf, and $ref into the root's $defs.\"\"\"\n    root = root if root is not None else schema\n    if '$ref' in schema:\n        name = schema['$ref'].rsplit('/', 1)[1]\n        schema = root['$defs'][name]\n    kind = schema.get('type')\n    if 'anyOf' in schema:\n        # a value matching no option reports the closest options' errors (fewest), not merely the last option's\n        failures = []\n        for option in schema['anyOf']:\n            errors = check_schema(value, option, path, root)\n            if not errors:\n                return []\n            failures.append(errors)\n        fewest = min((len(errors) for errors in failures), default=0)\n        closest = list(dict.fromkeys(error for errors in failures if len(errors) == fewest for error in errors))\n        return closest or [f'{path}: matches no option']\n    if kind == 'object':\n        if not isinstance(value, dict):\n            return [f'{path}: expected object']\n        errors = []\n        for name in schema.get('required', []):\n            if name not in value:\n                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: required')\n        properties = schema.get('properties', {})\n        for name, item in value.items():\n            if name in properties:\n                errors.extend(check_schema(item, properties[name], f'{path}.{name}', root))\n            elif schema.get('additionalProperties') is False:\n                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: not allowed')\n        return errors\n    if kind == 'array':\n        if not isinstance(value, list):\n            return [f'{path}: expected array']\n        errors = []\n        if len(value) < schema.get('minItems', 0):\n            errors.append(f'{path}: fewer than {schema[\"minItems\"]} items')\n        if 'maxItems' in schema and len(value) > schema['maxItems']:\n            errors.append(f'{path}: more than {schema[\"maxItems\"]} items')\n        for index, item in enumerate(value):\n            errors.extend(check_schema(item, schema.get('items', {}), f'{path}[{index}]', root))\n        return errors\n    if kind == 'string':\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            return [f'{path}: expected string']\n        if 'enum' in schema and value not in schema['enum']:\n            return [f'{path}: {value!r} not in {schema[\"enum\"]}']\n        if 'pattern' in schema and not re.search(schema['pattern'], value):\n            return [f'{path}: {value!r} does not match {schema[\"pattern\"]}']\n        return []\n    if kind == 'integer':\n        return [] if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected integer']\n    if kind == 'number':\n        return [] if isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected number']\n  - The control adjudication path must resolve control answers.\n  - Recommendation: take the STOP and the spending disagreement to Alex, and ask him to authorize one bounded blinded re-read of the\n    same 12 replies. A fourth writer run would change the answers while the open question is the reading.\n  - Falsifier: any failed control or any registered stop ends the re-read. Both spending replies' votes and reasons are reported beside\n    the verdict.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22) to \"要不要批准再重读一次？\": \"批准再重读一次 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不再重读，直接议花钱重发\" and\n  \"先停在这里\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** one bounded blinded re-read under the shared rule text, with the five controls. Any failed control or registered\n  stop ends it. A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\nreread2-packets/T17.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4, the writer's own 85% estimate of FY2027 Vera revenue as a share of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU guidance (e3660), giving F5 = $17bn, combined with F6, the writer's own estimate of exactly 2x growth (benchmarked on e4856/e4691), giving F7 = $34bn. The estimates rest on e4689, e5044, e5400 and e5403.\", \"why_not\": \"Rung 3 comes entirely from the 15% cut in F4. The same arithmetic without it gives $40bn (F3). The passages say FY2028 will be more than double the $20bn (e3660, e4856, e4764), which means above $40bn, in rung 2. No passage sizes the cut or gives a reason for it. e3660 restates a current-year expectation ('still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue'), and the writer recasts it as an 'opportunity'. No passage shows any non-Vera CPU revenue or any shortfall against the $20bn. e4689 (deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) argue against a cut, not for one. e5400 and e5403 are AMD's own benchmark claims and say nothing about sales. F6 take\nreread2-packets/T11.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"The reported first half (e109 $75.25bn + e5079 $89.02bn = $164.27bn) plus a second half built from Q3 company guidance of $108.0bn +/-2% (e2652) lifted to $111.62bn by a stated 16% sequential estimate (beat history e3200, e5056; Rubin shipments e2693; ACIE growth e5061; supply limits e5067), taken at a 93% Data Center share (rising mix e3399, data center as the growth driver e5223) = $103.8bn, and a stated 15% Q4 sequential estimate (hyperscale reacceleration as Rubin supply builds e3406, e5061, e5118; constraints e5067, e5205) at a 93.5% share = $120.01bn. Total $388.08bn, inside the $350-400bn band. The band holds across plausible variation: Q3 at the guidance midpoint with 92.5% mix (e3401, $99.9bn) and a flat Q4 still give about $364bn, and reaching $400bn would need about 27% sequential Q4 Data Center growth under stated supply constraints. Reference: Morningstar's $361bn (e2295), also rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in codex-r56*.md codex-r57.md codex-r58.md; do wc -l \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,26p' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c 1-300; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in codex-r56*.md codex-r57.md codex-r58.md; do wc -l \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,26p' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c 1-300; done'","aggregated_output":"      18 codex-r56.md\n**STOP on one confirmed line. Reject the blanket `%` and `x` cutoff.** The saved answer used $108B to derive a growth rate and $112.5B as its Q3 revenue. The proposed fix also accepts an answer that uses $108B as the dollar starting point for Q4 while counting $112.5B for Q3.\n\n1. **Serious — The verdict is right under the adjudication procedure used in both runs.** Both refusal reviewers judged the P-v4 answer right. I reconciled all 12 first replies with the saved requests and checker results. All 12 passed the schema, 11 published, and one was refused. There are 24 firs\n\n   **Wording — R07 has two disputed defects on F3.** One reader flagged an estimate presented as the market’s reference. The other flagged two values for the same quantity. The registered line requires agreement on the named defect itself. Each adjudicator pair split, so neither defect adds a confir\n\n   **Minor — Two details in the write-up need correction.** Only E’s Claude writer copied the two CPU-market figures and exercised the range fix. The write-up also omits R11’s split over F13, which assigns a time period and a funded/unfunded scope to an outside $70B figure. F13 feeds neither the out\n\n2. **Serious — The proposed checker change is wider than Alex’s ruling.** I reproduced the reported census: one of 24 saved replies changes, and the candidate passes all 104 existing cases. I then tested a variation of the saved P-v4 reply. It keeps the $112.5B Q3 forecast in the annual sum and proj\n\n3. **Serious — Change both request sentences with the narrower checker fix.** The system asks writers to carry guided growth forward. The schema says the rate can inform an `estimate`, which is a specific figure type, and the header can be read as requiring every calculation path to go through the q\n\n4. **Serious — Disclose the $18B split as affecting O’s answer. D741 ruling 2 leaves it open.** The sources report a $25B July balance and $18B committed for the rest of the year. They do not establish that the $18B is included in the July balance or will all be paid by year-end. Those assumptions c\n\n   **Minor — Wrong numbers confined to prose are a disclosure under this test.** The registered definitions cover copied and computed figures. The errors still matter to a reader. R09’s example reaches $430.82B against a $431.2B threshold, and R11’s full-slip scenario gives $32B on its own assumptio\n\n5. **Recommendation and falsifier.** Narrow the checker to the demonstrated use of guidance as a growth benchmark, align the request and reader wording, and replay the 24 saved replies. The repair fails if R03 still refuses, either counterexample publishes, or another saved decision changes. Once th\n\nNo files were edited. The two counterexamples ran in memory.      12 codex-r56b.md\n**Do not build the narrowed change.** It publishes an answer that counts **$112.5B** for Q3 in the annual sum, then resets Q4’s starting amount to the **$108B** Q3 guidance. The current checker refuses that answer.\n\n1. **Serious — The growth-rate exemption still lets a second quarterly value into the outcome.** I changed the saved P-v4 reply to start Q4 at `$112.5B × (1 + growth($112.5B, $108B))`, then grow that starting amount by 12%. The first step gives exactly $108B. The narrowed checker publishes **$411.28\n\n   **Minor — `rests_on` is an existing reader case.** I put the same substitution in a Q4 estimate that explicitly relied on the $108B guidance as its starting amount. Both versions published and recorded the competing Q3 figures. The check for two adopted forecasts follows `rests_on`. The check for\n\n2. **Serious — The code is both wider and narrower than Alex’s answer.** Alex approved using guidance to inform a 13% Q4 forecast when $113.6B supplies the only Q3 dollar amount. I encoded that example as a 13% estimate resting on the guidance-implied rate. Both versions published. The narrowed code\n\n3. **Wording — State the dollar-value rule consistently.** The schema says *guidance*, while the code skips any qualifying percentage. The header calls it the “one exception” and says “built on,” which leaves writers guessing whether an estimate resting on the forecast qualifies. Both reader version\n\n4. **Minor — Both new mutants are decisive, and one of the three refusal cases overlaps an existing check.** Both new passing cases fail with the exemption disabled. I also tried requiring the forecast as the immediate base. Only the Data Center case failed, so it earns its place. The Q2-built run-r\n\n**Verdict — Do not build.**      16 codex-r56c.md\n**Choose A′ with revised wording.** The saved answer used $112.5B for Q3 and used guidance to set Q4’s pace. The blanket change would also let an answer quietly switch Q4’s starting point back to $108B.\n\n1. **Serious — Keep S4.** D738 explicitly checks whether the arithmetic reaches a figure for a listed shared quantity without going through its forecast. D741 approves using guidance to inform an estimate. Alex’s example uses the roughly 12% guided pace to choose a 13% Q4 forecast, then applies 13% \n\n2. **Serious — The proposed wording still misses the boundary.** “Never as an input of a computed figure” bans more than the code does. “For a shared quantity” can also point writers at the wrong quantity. I checked all three cases in memory. [S4 implementation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/researc\n\n   - **Unlisted quantity.** Removing Q3 from the shared list made the identical arithmetic pass. The separate check for two adopted forecasts still applies to unlisted quantities. [Shared-quantity check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:615)\n   - **Outside Q3 figure.** Replacing guidance with the outside $113.6B produces the same refusal when the rate feeds Q4 directly. It passes when the outside rate informs a stated Q4 growth estimate. Readers still judge whether adopting that pace is supported. D741 specifically approves the guidance\n   - **Data Center.** The direct rate is refused when the segment outcome reaches the listed **company** Q3 guidance through that rate. The same arithmetic passes when the writer states and supports an estimated **segment** growth rate using the company rate as a benchmark. The segment’s own Q3 fore\n\n   **Wording — Change the header too.** Say “Follow every arithmetic path from each outcome, including through rates and multiples. For each listed shared quantity and period, the first figure of that quantity on every path that reaches it must be the same forecast.” In the system text, ask writers \n\n3. **Serious — Keep the stop line and register how readers apply it.** Record two judgments independently. Was the forecast and its reasoning financially sound? Did the exact request clearly state the condition that triggered refusal? A sound answer refused under a clear condition still stops the ru\n\n4. **Minor — Agree to one fresh $0 run with 12 writers after the wording and the procedure are registered.** First replay the saved refusal and the two reset examples, and show that the stated-estimate version passes. Keep the saved refusal as a test of the stated format, with its financial correctn\n\n**Verdict — A′ with revised wording and the existing stop line.**      12 codex-r57.md\n1. **Serious — The third run stopped under the rule as registered.** R01 forecast $679.25–745 billion and chose rung 4, which starts at $700 billion. Both readers marked the same defect on F14. That independently triggers the stop at §5 line 252. D742 kept that line and assigned adjudication to disa\n\n2. **Wording — The counts reconcile with two corrections to the report.** All 12 replies passed the schema and published on replay, with zero refusals. There were 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Both adjudicators rejected all eight individual defect marks, covering\n\n3. **Serious — R07 exposes a forecast that can publish without enough evidence. It adds zero registered stops.** The arithmetic is right. At $745 billion of total spending, the 90% share gives $670.5 billion, and a share of about 94% reaches rung 4. Both adjudicators found the evidence too weak to j\n\n4. **Serious — R01 has a separate problem that the range ruling leaves open.** It labels Zacks’s $720–745 billion of **all** capital spending as the market reference for **data-center** spending. The writer’s instructions ask for a reference for the question itself. Claude’s first reading noticed th\n\n5. **Minor — Two disclosures deserve more precise wording.** Both O replies depend on reading the $18 billion as funding from the July balance. R04 states the overlap and payment timing as estimates. R06 presents the $18 billion as the company’s schedule, and the adjudicators split on whether that r\n\n6. **Recommendation — Take the recorded STOP through Council B and ask Alex to authorize option (a).** Register the schema’s exact range rule in both reading instructions, then have fresh readers and adjudicators review all 12 original replies. Give them reshuffled IDs and access only to their packe\n\n   **Falsifier.** If the corrected reading rejects the valid overlap or accepts the disjoint control, the repair fails. If the reread hits any registered stop, including S losing its sole usable reply, the result stays with the council.      12 codex-r58.md\n**The registered STOP stands. Both range controls passed their range check, and both adjudicators agreed that Q10’s $108 billion midpoint was valid. Ask Alex to authorize one more blinded read with the full instructions the writers saw.**\n\n1. **Serious — Q10 was scored correctly.** Both first readers marked the same figure as misread. Under the registered rule, that alone stops the run. Both adjudicators cleared the mark because the writer quoted “$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%” in full and used the permitted midpoint. The two range\n\n2. **Minor — The overlap control passed a narrower test than the report’s wording suggests.** The builder added the new $36–43 billion forecast but kept the old 85% growth assumption and $37 billion forecast in the explanation. Both readers correctly flagged the competing figures. That leaves the ra\n\n3. **Serious — Give readers the same rule text as adjudicators.** Use one shared source for the writer’s system text, all schema descriptions, and the definitions readers apply. The current readers got the passages and answer. The adjudicators also got the writer’s rules, which resolved both the mid\n\n4. **Serious — The spending forecast still shows how much the result can depend on who reads it.** The first adjudicators rejected the 90% share as unsupported. Both fresh readers accepted the identical reply. At $745 billion of total spending, 90% gives $670.5 billion, while about 94% reaches $700 \n\n**Recommendation.** Take the STOP and the spending disagreement to Alex now. Ask him to authorize one bounded, blinded re-read of the same 12 replies, with the shared instructions and controls agreed before release. A fourth writer run would change the answers while the open question is how readers \n\n**Falsifier.** The revised reading must accept the full quote recorded as $108 billion and catch the identical quote recorded as $108.5 billion. I checked that changing only that value triggers the number check. It must also distinguish clean overlapping and disjoint bands, and flag a format-correct\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sys,json,copy,re\nrepo=Path.cwd()\nsys.path[:0]=[str(repo/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\\\"),str(repo/\\\"src\\\")]\nimport calc_small as cs\nfrom hivemind import model\nfrom blind_reread2 import controls\nd=repo/\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\\\"\nkeys=json.loads((d/\\\"reread2-key.json\\\").read_text())\ndef load(name): return json.loads((d/name).read_text())\ncases={v[\\\"case\\\"]:(load(v[\\\"case\\\"]+\\\"/item.json\\\"), load(v[\\\"case\\\"]+\\\"/schema.json\\\")) for v in keys.values()}\ndef qualifier_list(schema):\n    if isinstance(schema,dict):\n        if schema.get(\\\"description\\\",\\\"\\\").startswith(\\\"Source only: the qualifier\\\"):\n            return next(x[\\\"enum\\\"] for x in schema[\\\"anyOf\\\"] if \\\"enum\\\" in x)\n        for v in schema.values():\n            result=qualifier_list(v)\n            if result is not None: return result\n    if isinstance(schema,list):\n        for v in schema:\n            result=qualifier_list(v)\n            if result is not None: return result\ndef check(a,item,schema):\n    errors=model.check_schema(a,schema)\n    if errors: return (\\\"schema\\\",errors)\n    calc=cs.from_answer(a,item[\\\"family\\\"]); x=cs.check(item[\\\"family\\\"],item[\\\"passages\\\"],calc)\n    return (cs.publish_decision(item[\\\"family\\\"],calc,x), x[\\\"errors\\\"],x[\\\"published\\\"])\nbefore={}\nest=[]\nn=0\nfor pid,v in keys.items():\n    if v[\\\"writer\\\"].startswith(\\\"K-\\\"):continue\n    item,schema=cases[v[\\\"case\\\"]]\n    a=load(v[\\\"answer\\\"])\n    before[pid]=check(a,item,schema)\n    for f in a[\\\"figures\\\"]:\n        if f[\\\"kind\\\"]\"'!=\"source\":continue\n        n+=1\n        quote=item[\"passages\"][f[\"evidence_id\"]][\"text\"]\n        adjacent=[re.search(r\"'\"\\\\bestimated\\\\s*\"'$\",quote[:s],re.I) is not None for s,e in cs.expressions(quote) if quote[s:e]==f[\"quote_number\"]]\n        if any(adjacent):est.append((pid,f[\"id\"],f[\"evidence_id\"],f[\"quote_number\"],f[\"qualifier\"],adjacent))\nprint(\"baseline\",len(before),{x[0] for x in before.values()},\"source figures\",n,\"estimated\",est)\nfor name,(case,a,_) in controls(d).items():\n    assert a==load(\"reread2-controls/\"+name+\".json\")\n    print(\"control\",name,check(a,*cases[case]))\nitem,schema=cases[\"S\"]\nstored=load(keys[\"T10\"][\"answer\"])\ndef variant(q,ev=None,number=None,val=None):\n    a=copy.deepcopy(stored)\n    f=next(f for f in a[\"figures\"] if f[\"id\"]==\"F11\")\n    f[\"qualifier\"]=q\n    if ev is not None:f.update(evidence_id=ev,quote_number=number,value=val)\n    return a\nprint(\"old enum\",qualifier_list(schema))\nprint(\"old estimated\",check(variant(\"estimated\"),item,schema))\ncs.QUALIFIERS_BEFORE.append(\"estimated\")\nfor _,schema_i in cases.values():\n    qualifier_list(schema_i).append(\"estimated\")\nprint(\"patched estimated\",check(variant(\"estimated\"),item,schema))\nprint(\"patched none\",check(variant(\"none\"),item,schema))\nprint(\"patched invented\",check(variant(\"estimated\",\"e2458\",\"$760 billion\",760),item,schema))\nprint(\"changed originals\", [pid for pid,x in before.items() if x!=check(load(keys[pid][\"answer\"]),*cases[keys[pid][\"case\"]])])\nu=copy.deepcopy(stored)\nfor f in u[\"figures\"]:\n    f[\"outcome_for\"]=[];f[\"reference_for\"]=[]\nu[\"answers\"][0].update(shape=\"unresolved\",rung=None,reference_unavailable=None,reasons=\"The same-scope data-center share or a band-setting bound is absent from the shown passages.\")\nnext(f for f in u[\"figures\"] if f[\"id\"]==\"F11\")[\"qualifier\"]=\"estimated\"\nprint(\"unresolved\",check(u,item,schema))\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
