{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba E/answers/codex.json | sed -n '58,149p' && nl -ba S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '193,282p' && rg -n '\\\\\"'$138 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$431.2 billion'\"' P/answers/*.json P-v4/answers/*.json | cut -c 1-850\"","aggregated_output":"    58\t      },\n    59\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n    60\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    61\t      \"value\": 40,\n    62\t      \"low\": null,\n    63\t      \"high\": null,\n    64\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    65\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    66\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    67\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    68\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n    69\t      \"operation\": \"product\",\n    70\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    71\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    72\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    73\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    74\t    },\n    75\t    {\n    76\t      \"id\": \"F4\",\n    77\t      \"what\": {\n    78\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    79\t        \"measure\": \"revenue realization share\",\n    80\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity\",\n    81\t        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n    82\t      },\n    83\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n    84\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n    85\t      \"value\": 85,\n    86\t      \"low\": null,\n    87\t      \"high\": null,\n    88\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n    89\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    90\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    91\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    92\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    93\t      \"operation\": null,\n    94\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue.\",\n    95\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\"],\n    96\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    97\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    98\t    },\n    99\t    {\n   100\t      \"id\": \"F5\",\n   101\t      \"what\": {\n   102\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   103\t        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n   104\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n   105\t        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n   106\t      },\n   107\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   108\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   109\t      \"value\": 17,\n   110\t      \"low\": null,\n   111\t      \"high\": null,\n   112\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   113\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   114\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   115\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   116\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F4\"],\n   117\t      \"operation\": \"share\",\n   118\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   119\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   120\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   121\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   122\t    },\n   123\t    {\n   124\t      \"id\": \"F6\",\n   125\t      \"what\": {\n   126\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   127\t        \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\",\n   128\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU FY2028 versus FY2027\",\n   129\t        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n   130\t      },\n   131\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   132\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   133\t      \"value\": 2,\n   134\t      \"low\": null,\n   135\t      \"high\": null,\n   136\t      \"unit\": \"x\",\n   137\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   138\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   139\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   140\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   141\t      \"operation\": null,\n   142\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments.\",\n   143\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F5\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5403\"],\n   144\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   145\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   146\t    },\n   147\t    {\n   148\t      \"id\": \"F7\",\n   149\t      \"what\": {\n   193\t    {\n   194\t      \"id\": \"F11\",\n   195\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"tech giants\", \"measure\": \"data-center spending\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 750,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1416\",\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   205\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   206\t      \"operation\": null,\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"five largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   215\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   216\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   217\t      \"value\": 800,\n   218\t      \"low\": null,\n   219\t      \"high\": null,\n   220\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   221\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5376\",\n   222\t      \"quote_number\": \"$800 billion\",\n   223\t      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\n   224\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   225\t      \"operation\": null,\n   226\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   227\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   228\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   229\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   230\t    },\n   231\t    {\n   232\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n   233\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   234\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   235\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   236\t      \"value\": null,\n   237\t      \"low\": 95,\n   238\t      \"high\": 100,\n   239\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n   240\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   241\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   242\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   243\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   244\t      \"operation\": null,\n   245\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\n   246\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\n   247\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   248\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   249\t    },\n   250\t    {\n   251\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n   252\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   253\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   254\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   255\t      \"value\": null,\n   256\t      \"low\": 679.25,\n   257\t      \"high\": 745,\n   258\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   259\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   260\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   261\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   262\t      \"inputs\": [\"F7\", \"F13\"],\n   263\t      \"operation\": \"share\",\n   264\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   265\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   266\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v6\"],\n   267\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   268\t    }\n   269\t  ],\n   270\t  \"answers\": [\n   271\t    {\n   272\t      \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   273\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   274\t      \"rung\": 4,\n   275\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   276\t      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\n   277\t      \"why\": null,\n   278\t      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e1254\", \"e1549\", \"e1649\", \"e922\", \"e4405\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\", \"e2759\", \"e5376\", \"e4567\", \"e4566\", \"e1856\", \"e2458\", \"e122\", \"e1545\", \"e5292\", \"e2081\", \"e4807\"],\n   279\t      \"pushes\": [\n   280\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1254\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   281\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1549\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   282\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1649\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\nP-v4/answers/claude.json:314:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: the Wall Street consensus for FY2027 revenue is $411 billion (e4333), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion); the Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth on FY2026's $215.9 billion (about $405 billion, e4990) is also rung 2. Outcome: the first half is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion). Q3 is guided to $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, above the $104.6 billion consensus for the quarter; NVIDIA beat its guidance midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has topped the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, so I forecast Q3 at about $112.9 billion (4.5% above the midpoint, trimmed because capacity is sold out and supply covers roughly 70% of demand). For Q4 I forecast about 14% sequential growth to about $128.7 billion: \nP/answers/claude.json:390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on FY2026's $215.9 billion give about $405 billion, also rung 2. Outcome from the evidence: H1 is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion; F3). Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, excluding China data center compute; given 12 straight beats of the high end (3.1% on average over the last four) and a $5.2 billion beat of the Q2 midpoint, trimmed for the stated memory, power and packaging caps, I forecast Q3 at $112.5 billion (F9). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth, the pace guided for Q3, because hyperscale growth is said to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'deduction|supported bound|narrower|def _references|def _answers|reference.*what|exact quantity|def nullable' render_v13.py calc_small.py blind_adjudicate.py; nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '139,177p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '514,594p'; cd ../../.. && ls -d poc/data/judging-v13s-blind*; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && rg -n 'deduction|specific|why_unresolved' S/user.txt S/schema.json reread2-packets/T10.md | cut -c 1-420\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'deduction|supported bound|narrower|def _references|def _answers|reference.*what|exact quantity|def nullable' render_v13.py calc_small.py blind_adjudicate.py; nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '139,177p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '514,594p'; cd ../../.. && ls -d poc/data/judging-v13s-blind*; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && rg -n 'deduction|specific|why_unresolved' S/user.txt S/schema.json reread2-packets/T10.md | cut -c 1-420\"","aggregated_output":"calc_small.py:550:def _answers(family, figs, calc, answers):\nrender_v13.py:20:def nullable(schema):\nrender_v13.py:157:                            'To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the '\nrender_v13.py:158:                            'deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. '\n   139\t    answer = obj({\n   140\t        'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids},\n   141\t        'shape': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['forecast', 'unresolved'], 'description': 'forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and '\n   142\t                  'a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable '\n   143\t                  'null; no figure marked for it.'},\n   144\t        'rung': nullable({'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it '\n   145\t                          '(a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.'}),\n   146\t        'reference_unavailable': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.'}),\n   147\t        'reasons': S,\n   148\t        'why': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.'}),\n   149\t        'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}},\n   150\t        'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': push}, 'theme_pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': theme_push},\n   151\t    })\n   152\t    return obj({'figures': {'type': 'array', 'items': figure, 'description': 'The family\\'s calculation. Use one forecast figure for each '\n   153\t                            'quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a '\n   154\t                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why '\n   155\t                            'it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared '\n   156\t                            'quantity\\'s guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. '\n   157\t                            'To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the '\n   158\t                            'deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. '\n   159\t                            'Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.'},\n   160\t                'answers': {'type': 'array', 'items': answer}})\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\tdef v13s_header(family, shown):\n   164\t    \"\"\"[52] The family header's rule lines, rendered from the family's data.\"\"\"\n   165\t    lines = []\n   166\t    for r in family.get('relations', []):\n   167\t        if r['type'] == 'part_of' and {r['part'], r['whole']} <= shown:\n   168\t            periods = ', '.join(p for p in family.get('compare_periods', []) if p != family['members'][r['part']]['what']['period'])\n   169\t            lines.append(f'Relations: {r[\"part\"]} is part of {r[\"whole\"]}. For each of {periods} and for the outcomes, the part\\'s figure may '\n   170\t                         'not exceed the whole\\'s: for every value of anything both rest on, and otherwise the part\\'s low end may not exceed '\n   171\t                         'the whole\\'s high end.')\n   172\t    if not lines:\n   173\t        lines.append('Relations: none.')\n   174\t    for ps in family.get('part_scopes', []):\n   175\t        lines.append(f'Scopes: {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"part\"]} is part of {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"whole\"]}. A sum '\n   176\t                     'never adds the part into the whole for the same period; a difference may take the part from the whole of the same '\n   177\t                     'period only to give the rest.')\n   514\t        got = {i: calc.interval(i) for i in ins}\n   515\t        if not all(got.values()):\n   516\t            continue                                       # an input that cannot be evaluated is refused at that input\n   517\t        if op in ('ratio', 'growth'):\n   518\t            denominator = got[ins[1]] if op == 'ratio' else got[ins[0]]\n   519\t            if denominator[0] <= 0 <= denominator[1]:\n   520\t                errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: divides by a figure whose value can be zero'))\n   521\t                continue\n   522\t        if op == 'share' and not (0 <= got[ins[1]][0] and got[ins[1]][1] <= 100):\n   523\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\n   524\t            continue\n   525\t        value = calc.interval(fid)\n   526\t        if value is None:\n   527\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n   528\t            continue\n   529\t        lo, hi = value\n   530\t        if _is_range(f):\n   531\t            ok = _close(f['low'], lo, unit) and _close(f['high'], hi, unit)\n   532\t        else:\n   533\t            ok = _close(f['value'], (lo + hi) / 2, unit)\n   534\t        if not ok:\n   535\t            declared = f'{f[\"low\"]:g}–{f[\"high\"]:g}' if _is_range(f) else f'{f[\"value\"]:g}'\n   536\t            got_text = f'{lo:.4g}' if lo == hi else f'{lo:.4g}–{hi:.4g}'\n   537\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: declared {declared} but {op} of {\", \".join(ins)} gives {got_text}'))\n   538\t    return errors\n   539\t\n   540\t\n   541\tdef _overlaps_band(interval, band):\n   542\t    lo, hi = interval\n   543\t    b_lo = band[0] if band[0] is not None else -math.inf\n   544\t    b_hi = band[1] if band[1] is not None else math.inf\n   545\t    if lo == hi:\n   546\t        return b_lo <= lo < b_hi\n   547\t    return lo < b_hi and hi >= b_lo\n   548\t\n   549\t\n   550\tdef _answers(family, figs, calc, answers):\n   551\t    errors, outcomes = [], {}\n   552\t    members = family['members']\n   553\t    for a in answers:\n   554\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   555\t        member = members.get(v)\n   556\t        if member is None or a.get('shape') == 'unresolved':\n   557\t            continue\n   558\t        out = figs.get(a.get('outcome'))\n   559\t        if out is None:\n   560\t            continue                                       # reported by from_answer\n   561\t        if out['kind'] not in ('computed', 'estimate') or out['basis'] != 'forecast':\n   562\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n   563\t        if _key(out['what']) != _key(member['what']):\n   564\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n   565\t        if out['unit'] != member.get('unit', 'USD bn'):\n   566\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n   567\t        outcomes[v] = out['id']\n   568\t        rung = a.get('rung')\n   569\t        if not isinstance(rung, int) or isinstance(rung, bool) or not 1 <= rung <= len(member['bands']):\n   570\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung!r} is not one of 1..{len(member[\"bands\"])}'))\n   571\t            continue\n   572\t        interval = calc.interval(out['id'])\n   573\t        if interval is not None and not _overlaps_band(interval, member['bands'][rung - 1]):\n   574\t            shown = f'{interval[0]:g}' if interval[0] == interval[1] else f'{interval[0]:g}–{interval[1]:g}'\n   575\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung} {member[\"bands\"][rung - 1]} does not hold its outcome {shown} as recomputed'))\n   576\t        ref = (a.get('reference') or {}).get('figure')\n   577\t        if ref in figs:\n   578\t            chain = {ref} | _beneath(ref, figs)\n   579\t            if any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'estimate' for g in chain):\n   580\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\n   581\t            elif any(figs[g]['basis'] == 'forecast' for g in chain):\n   582\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\n   583\t            elif not any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'source' and figs[g]['basis'] in EXTERNAL for g in chain):\n   584\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\n   585\t            else:                                          # [D741] a past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period\n   586\t                carried = sorted(g for g in chain if figs[g]['basis'] == 'reported' and figs[g]['unit'] in ('%', 'x')\n   587\t                                 and figs[g]['what']['period'] != figs[ref]['what']['period'])\n   588\t                if carried:\n   589\t                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\n   590\t                                         f'{figs[ref][\"what\"][\"period\"]}; that makes it our comparison estimate, not the market\\'s reference'))\n   591\t    return errors, outcomes\n   592\t\n   593\t\n   594\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\nreread2-packets/T10.md:36:  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\nreread2-packets/T10.md:45:- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling qu\nreread2-packets/T10.md:54: \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculati\nreread2-packets/T10.md:148:    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\nS/user.txt:67:    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\nS/schema.json:308:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport sys,json,re\nrepo=Path.cwd()\nsys.path.insert(0,str(repo/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\\\"))\nfrom calc_small import expressions\nfor run in sorted((repo/\\\"poc/data\\\").glob(\\\"judging-v13s-blind*\\\")):\n n=m=0; hits=[]\n for p in sorted(run.glob(\\\"*/answers/*.json\\\")):\n    if p.stem not in {\\\"claude\\\",\\\"codex\\\"}:continue\n    item=json.loads((p.parents[1]/\\\"item.json\\\").read_text());a=json.loads(p.read_text());n+=1\n    for f in a[\\\"figures\\\"]:\n       if f[\\\"kind\\\"]\"'!=\"source\":continue\n       m+=1\n       quote=item[\"passages\"][f[\"evidence_id\"]][\"text\"]\n       if any(re.search(r\"'\"\\\\bestimated\\\\s*\"'$\",quote[:s],re.I) for s,e in expressions(quote) if quote[s:e]==f[\"quote_number\"]):\n         hits.append((p.parents[1].name,p.stem,f[\"id\"],f[\"evidence_id\"],f[\"quote_number\"],f[\"qualifier\"]))\n print(run.name,n,m,hits)\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
