{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && rg -n '\"'^[[:space:]]*\"(qualifier|enum|description)\"|estimated'\"' S/schema.json reread2-packets/T10.md reread2-adjudication/T10.md | rg 'qualifier|estimated' | cut -c 1-260; cd ../../../docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && sed -n '1,115p' blind_adjudicate.py; cd ../../.. && nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '352,367p'\"","aggregated_output":"S/schema.json:176:     \"qualifier\": {\nS/schema.json:200:        \"description\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"\nS/schema.json:298:     \"qualifier\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:99:    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:106:    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:140:- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\nreread2-packets/T10.md:164:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on\nreread2-packets/T10.md:167:- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend r\nreread2-packets/T10.md:232:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:251:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:270:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:289:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:308:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:327:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-packets/T10.md:346:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-packets/T10.md:365:      \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:384:      \"qualifier\": \"near\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:403:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:422:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:441:      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\nreread2-packets/T10.md:460:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-packets/T10.md:479:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:35:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F11']): e1416 says “an estimated $750 billion.” F11 gives the correct amount but omits the qualifier.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:97:    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:104:    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:138:- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:162:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billi\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:165:- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could sp\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:229:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:248:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:267:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:286:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:305:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:324:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:343:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:362:      \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:381:      \"qualifier\": \"near\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:400:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:419:      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:438:      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:457:      \"qualifier\": null,\nreread2-adjudication/T10.md:476:      \"qualifier\": null,\n\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n\nAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\nthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\nand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n\nRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\nanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\nclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n\nUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\nfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n\nHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n\nTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\nbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n\nFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\npassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n\n\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n\nReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _json(text):\n    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n\n\ndef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    lines = []\n    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n        if mark.get('why_not'):\n            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n        lines.append(line)\n    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    if not defects:\n        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    for defect in defects:\n        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    return lines\n\n\ndef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    out = folder / out_name\n    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    for pid in packet_ids:\n        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n                          format_material(folder / case), '',\n                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n        if target.exists():\n            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n        target.write_text(text)\n        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n\n\nREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n\nA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\nalone:\n1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\nReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n    if target.exists():\n        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    target.write_text(text)\n    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   352\t  by readers (Alex, 2026-11-10: \"你应该跑council loop 来reflect 你每一轮的思路还有决策，需要你打成共识，记录决策，向前直行测试。不要drift,我们有PRD和量化目标，\n   353\t  你自己作为llm 下去看所有trace\").\n   354\t- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n   355\t  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n   356\t  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n   357\t  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n   358\t  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n   359\t  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n   360\t  control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the model's own account and the\n   361\t  request locate where the decision is made (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你跑这么多次真的有意义吗\"; \"我感觉你在为了跑而跑\"). Several fixes on one\n   362\t  base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02: \"快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整\"; D266).\n   363\t- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n   364\t  rounds of review (Claude's own, Codex's, an independent reader's) on the traces until a round finds nothing serious; never a menu of\n   365\t  arms or options, never a conclusion the next probe overturns (a choice that changes what the system does still goes to him with its\n   366\t  options and a recommendation, Authority and scope) (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"就测试一种，一次 … 你就没有自己看5轮review，能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议？\").\n   367\t  An agreed recommendation is first challenged by an independent reader on the concrete evidence behind it (\"why you give\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport json,sys,copy\nroot=Path.cwd()\nsys.path[:0]=[str(root/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\\\"),str(root/\\\"src\\\")]\nfrom blind_reread2 import READER_BRIEF\nfrom blind_rules import format_material\nfrom hivemind.model import check_schema\nimport calc_small as cs\nd=root/\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\\\"\ndef load(n):return json.loads((d/n).read_text())\nkeys=load(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\")\nfor i,item in keys.items():\n case=item[\\\"case\\\"]\n expected=\\\"\\\\n\\\".join([READER_BRIEF,format_material(d/case),\\\"\\\",\\\"# The request as the writer saw it\\\",\\\"\\\",load(case+\\\"/item.json\\\")[\\\"user\\\"],\\\"\\\",\\\"# The answer\\\",\\\"\\\",(d/item[\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text(),\\\"\\\"])\n assert expected==(d/f\\\"reread2-packets/{i}.md\\\").read_text(),i\n for p in (d/\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\").glob(i+\\\".md\\\"):\n  text=p.read_text()\n  assert format_material(d/case) in text,i\n  assert (d/item[\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text() in text,i\nprint(\\\"17 writer packets and three adjudication packets match key and format material\\\")\ni=load(\\\"S/item.json\\\"); s=load(\\\"S/schema.json\\\")\na=load(\\\"S/answers/codex.json\\\")\nfor f in a[\\\"figures\\\"]:f[\\\"outcome_for\\\"]=[]; f[\\\"reference_for\\\"]=[]\na[\\\"answers\\\"][0].update(shape=\\\"unresolved\\\",rung=None,reference_unavailable=None,why=\\\"The shown passages do not support a deduction or bound that establishes the band.\\\",reasons=\\\"Total capex is a comparison for scale.\\\")\nassert not check_schema(a,s)\nc=cs.from_answer(a,i[\\\"family\\\"]); x=cs.check(i[\\\"family\\\"],i[\\\"passages\\\"],c)\nprint(\\\"unresolved replay\\\",cs.publish_decision(i[\\\"family\\\"],c,x),x[\\\"errors\\\"],x[\\\"published\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from pathlib import Path\nimport json,sys,copy\nroot=Path.cwd()\nsys.path[:0]=[str(root/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\\\"),str(root/\\\"src\\\")]\nfrom blind_reread2 import READER_BRIEF\nfrom blind_rules import format_material\nfrom hivemind.model import check_schema\nimport calc_small as cs\nd=root/\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\\\"\ndef load(n):return json.loads((d/n).read_text())\nkeys=load(\\\"reread2-key.json\\\")\nfor i,item in keys.items():\n case=item[\\\"case\\\"]\n expected=\\\"\\\\n\\\".join([READER_BRIEF,format_material(d/case),\\\"\\\",\\\"# The request as the writer saw it\\\",\\\"\\\",load(case+\\\"/item.json\\\")[\\\"user\\\"],\\\"\\\",\\\"# The answer\\\",\\\"\\\",(d/item[\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text(),\\\"\\\"])\n assert expected==(d/f\\\"reread2-packets/{i}.md\\\").read_text(),i\n for p in (d/\\\"reread2-adjudication\\\").glob(i+\\\".md\\\"):\n  text=p.read_text()\n  assert format_material(d/case) in text,i\n  assert (d/item[\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text() in text,i\nprint(\\\"17 writer packets and three adjudication packets match key and format material\\\")\ni=load(\\\"S/item.json\\\"); s=load(\\\"S/schema.json\\\")\na=load(\\\"S/answers/codex.json\\\")\nfor f in a[\\\"figures\\\"]:f[\\\"outcome_for\\\"]=[]; f[\\\"reference_for\\\"]=[]\na[\\\"answers\\\"][0].update(shape=\\\"unresolved\\\",rung=None,reference_unavailable=None,why=\\\"The shown passages do not support a deduction or bound that establishes the band.\\\",reasons=\\\"Total capex is a comparison for scale.\\\")\nassert not check_schema(a,s)\nc=cs.from_answer(a,i[\\\"family\\\"]); x=cs.check(i[\\\"family\\\"],i[\\\"passages\\\"],c)\nprint(\\\"unresolved replay\\\",cs.publish_decision(i[\\\"family\\\"],c,x),x[\\\"errors\\\"],x[\\\"published\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"17 writer packets and three adjudication packets match key and format material\nunresolved replay publish [] {}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"agent_message","text":"**Council 59 verdict: both registered stops stand. Choose (c) and take a targeted $0 test to Alex.** At $745 billion of total spending, a 90% data-center share gives $670.5 billion. A share of about 94% crosses $700 billion and changes the answer. The passages give no reliable way to choose between those shares. [S answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:213) · [S passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:29)\n\n1. **Serious — The second re-read was scored correctly.** I checked all 34 first readings and six adjudications. All five controls met their registered expectations. No original reply drew the same defect mark from both first readers. Both adjudicators upheld the F11 mark on T10, which triggers the first stop. Both readers rejected T06’s forecast, and T10 stayed split at both levels, which leaves S with zero usable replies and triggers the second stop. The other five cases each had a usable reply, and both joint replies supported both forecasts. [Control and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:333) · [Tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:77)\n\n2. **Minor — Add `estimated` to the existing list.** The instructions tell the writer to preserve the qualifier beside the number, but the allowed list could only represent this quote as unqualified. The readers and adjudicators saw the description without the list. I found one affected figure among 251 source figures across the three original writer runs. In memory, adding `estimated` let the corrected F11 pass and left its forecast unchanged. The check rejected both dropping the word and adding it to a quote that lacked it. Disabling the qualifier comparison let the dropped word through. Show readers the same allowed values so they can judge what writers could express. Narrowing the description alone would leave “an estimated $750 billion” recorded as unqualified. [Stored F11](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:193) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:110) · [Number check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:487) · [Reader packet builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:47)\n\n3. **Serious — S is the priority, and the same weakness appears in E.** Both S writers stated their deductions as D741 requires. Zacks gives total spending for the four companies, the $730 billion figure covers AI infrastructure, and NVIDIA’s nearly $800 billion covers five companies. Those figures suggest data centers account for a large share. They do not justify 90% or a 95% floor for the four companies in the question. T10 also uses Zacks’s total as the market reference for data-center spending, a separate scope error already noted in round 57. In E, both readers rejected the 85% share applied to the $20 billion base. That assumption turns a $40 billion comparison into a $34 billion forecast and changes its band. [D741 ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11353) · [T10 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-adjudication/T10.claude.json:3) · [Round 57](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r57.md:7) · [E calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:75)\n\n4. **Minor — The five controls passed the checks they were built for.** The overlap control tested whether readers recognized a valid range, and those readers still split on whether its forecast was supported. First readers were explicitly asked to name the evidence for the assumption that decides the band. Adjudicators got the broader definition of support without that instruction. T10 shows why this matters. Both adjudicators could check the arithmetic, but they disagreed on whether the sources justified the 95% floor. [Overlap tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:12) · [Reader instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py:22) · [Adjudicator instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:24)\n\n5. **Recommendation — After Council B agrees, ask Alex to authorize one targeted $0 test.** Prove the qualifier repair on saved replies. Then test a short clarification of the existing instruction: when a deduction decides the band, show what evidence supports its size or a bound that justifies the chosen band. Leave the forecast unresolved if neither is available. Use S and one stored case where the passages do support deriving a narrower figure. Option (a) would mostly sample fresh writers against the same known weakness. Option (b) would require Alex to explicitly change the registered sequence, which sends a clean result to him for the paid request. I verified that S can be shown as unresolved, but that supplies no supported forecast for the current test. If Alex wants a justified unresolved answer to count, register that before a new test. The 36 requests remain the measurement of gpt-6-luna. [Existing unresolved instruction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt:9) · [Usability and paid sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:263) · [D725 paid test](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md:86)\n\n   **Falsifier.** The clarification fails if a fresh S answer still picks a band using an unsupported share, or if it withholds the forecast in the case with adequate evidence. Both readers should be able to point to the passage that decides which outcome is warranted.\n\nThe review and replays were read-only."}}
